{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/verification.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-10T01:34:34.165824Z","id":"7081ac09c683ef93c85d3b251d305fb9141ee4180cbe604b224348fe9b1ef900","body":{"detail":"§10.1 drives a real `claude -p` with this branch's plugin against a scratch daemon and a scripted Messages API that calls the publish tool, then replies as a LAN viewer with curl and validates the export with Ajv. Unlike scripts/smoke-claude-ask.sh, its fake model (a variant of scripts/fake-anthropic.py whose first turn calls `mcp__plugin_clax_clax__publish`) and its driver were not committed, so the check is described, not rerunnable as is. A scripts/smoke-claude-toolpath.sh, reusing fake-anthropic.py with a publish scenario, would make it repeatable.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1085},"end":{"line":1100},"content_hash":"2a2bc6f18be95108c6873fc23dd7f10d97f1cebac973297f961d3f8979d1de8b"},"summary":"The Toolpath manual check (§10.1) has no script in the repository","tags":["verification","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T06:02:11.811733Z","id":"06d36deac10389c296e42470276d7a5f535689b98d1be753d0be1f645f583d9d","body":{"detail":"Added for the shell: participants agents' name and person ({public_id, name, owner}); a thread owner's person_name; a thread's approves_first (public IDs, null for the owner; browsers only, absent when empty); and owner: true on GET /api/viewers/me and the artifact boot payload's viewer for the owner's browser. Agents' payloads change only by the name and person keys spec 2026-10-08 section 19 item 6 allows.","kind":"concern","summary":"The contract does not yet describe the fields the Send menu and owner chip read","tags":["task:24","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T06:49:42.668690Z","id":"35572e2274d3fc3b78b5fe674c391f6b9d2183a26a7f9d6a0c39d06971a1e776","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"06d36deac10389c296e42470276d7a5f535689b98d1be753d0be1f645f583d9d","summary":"Since bb547990: thread views no longer carry approves_first; the new browser-only route GET /api/artifacts/{aid}/approves-first?threads=<tid,…> answers {approves_first: [public ID | null]} (403 for LAN viewers, anonymous callers and agents; 404 for a thread of another artifact; at most 200 threads). Question views and inbox items' agent gain name; browsers get people's names as stored, agents the payload-safe form.","tags":["task:24","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T12:02:04.319721Z","id":"d74a29be48f8826b30999dbe3bf8a9e7486f491197c67db70c9b595700e55335","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-mcp/src/tools.rs (the status tool's annotations) changed status to read_only_hint false, destructive false, idempotent true, because `name` writes. Under Codex's `writes` approval mode (crates/clax-cli/src/codex_approvals.rs:12) a tool not annotated readOnlyHint asks, so status, which ran unasked, now prompts; docs/contract.md:56 still says readOnlyHint true.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":54},"end":{"line":61},"content_hash":"966a28b85777889409a04e068b00adbd01e067425963e6aedf0515c1a43859cb"},"suggested_fix":"Move status to the destructiveHint false list in docs/contract.md and say there that `writes` mode asks for it.","summary":"The contract still lists status among the readOnlyHint tools, but status is now annotated read_only_hint = false","tags":["review","docs","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T02:55:01.457198Z","id":"e104b84998514f5d815bba578eba18ce20226e26f2a33019af423d45e7df973d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7a06e2cd","summary":"The contract now lists status as not read-only (destructiveHint false, idempotentHint true) because it names the agent with `name`, and comments_handoff among the state-changing tools (7a06e2cd).","supersedes":"d74a29be48f8826b30999dbe3bf8a9e7486f491197c67db70c9b595700e55335","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-15T04:07:31.464183Z","id":"b49fdc77c91f68d06fd9447da725617e40cffcd4fe5b5b544b833edd4aa66d67","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:28338345","summary":"docs/contract.md now describes them (28338345): participants' agents carry name and person {public_id, name, owner} ('Versions, seen marks and attention'); a thread's owner {handle, name, person, live, person_name} ('Thread ownership'); GET /api/artifacts/<aid>/approves-first?threads= and who learns it ('Approvals'); owner: true on GET /api/viewers/me and the boot payload's viewer ('Security model'); the question view's and inbox item's agent name. grep -n 'approves-first?threads\\|person_name}' docs/contract.md finds them.","supersedes":"06d36deac10389c296e42470276d7a5f535689b98d1be753d0be1f645f583d9d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-15T05:30:31.539101Z","id":"a212e22cc55dcf1dbcad7306fd3b81d0fa56aa37c0136d878bb92518f3f05cec","body":{"detail":"The text line is as written, but the tool result's withheld entry is {thread_id, artifact_id, count, asked: false, no_access: true} (docs/contract.md:122-123; crates/clax-core/src/feedback.rs Placeholder; asserted in rows_on_an_artifact_the_session_may_not_read_are_held_and_told_once). An agent sent a thread on an artifact before its first take, whose access then ends, learns that artifact's ID only from this entry.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3134},"end":{"line":3136},"content_hash":"4476c5f57c01898b3c87530d102614b68370d620a002bdce4e8dec9e39b7238e"},"suggested_fix":"Say 'the thread's and artifact's IDs and the count' here, or leave artifact_id out of no_access entries.","summary":"The contract says the lost-access notice carries the thread's ID and the count only; its withheld entry also carries the artifact ID","tags":["review","task:24","docs","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-15T05:58:14.169491Z","id":"ec17c3b6a225aca6867dad990faa159ca2b8d567fd706a0a79a0a2e3b2ec8fe5","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:73c2b8e3","summary":"Fixed: docs/contract.md says the session is told with the thread's and the artifact's IDs and the count only (the line names the thread; the withheld entry adds artifact_id), and names a removed raise among the causes.","supersedes":"a212e22cc55dcf1dbcad7306fd3b81d0fa56aa37c0136d878bb92518f3f05cec","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-15T22:24:19.594217Z","id":"f45591f71b69438e60aebfd7b4bf854ba4d1fb1d7492685b43dd4f4339c11dd3","body":{"detail":"docs/usage.md:367 says writes need the daemon token, \"so LAN viewers can read artifacts and comment on them but not publish or change them\". With people, a connected person's agents publish, reply, resolve and write documents with key-bound grants, and their browsers approve and narrow. The guide's Use section (docs/usage.md:126-166) lists no `clax connect`, `clax people`, `clax approvals` or `clax agents rename`, says nothing of People (/people) or approvals, and nowhere says \"use a trusted LAN: Clax traffic is not encrypted\" (spec 2026-10-08 O7, §21). Only the developer note at docs/usage.md:266 and docs/contract.md describe them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":365},"end":{"line":367},"content_hash":"6a597844d96b84358c643af87ae66b0c05a053a40d9f8cf9b0b466791212e432"},"suggested_fix":"Add a short \"Working with other people\" part to Use (invite, `clax connect`, People, approvals, the not-connected switch, trusted LAN only) and rewrite the security model paragraph for grants and signatures.","summary":"The user guide's security model says LAN viewers cannot publish or change anything, and the guide never explains connecting a second person","tags":["review","final-review-ui","docs","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-16T03:13:17.305871Z","id":"52ec6e9f30f037d530b20b373fbe6a5ecee0323d788d66f764412335668f05c9","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:107d63a5","summary":"docs/usage.md: a People subsection in Use (invites, requests, after connecting, agents, approvals, viewers not connected, revoking), the CLI lines for clax connect, clax people, clax agents and clax approvals, and the security model rewritten for connected people's signed requests, browser-only approvals and rights, and 'Clax traffic on the LAN is not encrypted: serve on the LAN only on a network you trust'. Checked against docs/contract.md 'People and their agents', 'Approvals', 'Names' and 'Security model'.","supersedes":"f45591f71b69438e60aebfd7b4bf854ba4d1fb1d7492685b43dd4f4339c11dd3","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T06:21:31.835131Z","id":"4bf9be8f14c9bb823e2fc299dc181992172fb8a6e19598a4440247c1aba1caaa","body":{"detail":"The prerequisite is Node 22, the Node MODULE.bazel pins for the web, Pi and browser checks and native-check.yml installs. demo/local.ts and crates/clax-worker's scripts (e2e*.mjs, test-wasm.mjs, deploy.mjs) use no API newer than Node 22; .ts scripts run by node rely on type stripping, unflagged from Node 22.18.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":7},"end":{"line":7},"content_hash":"5e4fd852a88daddd1c554f232f5b49da8d6ad99f6f093d825f6237e288cb4ef3"},"summary":"Node 26 for development","tags":["build","revisit:a hosted or demo script needs a Node API newer than 22","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:17:24.147571Z","id":"cd89cedc177194b40fed826c2cfbb8aadcf00b75946a02a626b70e0da14f802f","body":{"detail":"Store::restore_version (crates/clax-core/src/store/artifacts.rs) writes version.publish with an extra body field restored_from: n (the restored version) and sets carried to the paths of n, index.html aside, whose sha256 equals the previous version's, so carried keeps meaning 'unchanged from the previous version'. The row at docs/contract.md:4052 lists neither restored_from nor the restore case. The redaction table classes restored_from as safe (crates/clax-core/src/toolpath/redact.rs, body_class). Tests: store::artifacts::tests::a_restore_records_its_source_and_only_unchanged_files_as_carried and a_publish_records_no_source_version.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":4052},"end":{"line":4052},"content_hash":"1b98ee2a2cf240d177ed1a6ceeb0ac292f462a2364dc06e07669afeb99d57c5f"},"suggested_fix":"Add `restored_from (n, a restore only)` to the version.publish body column and say carried lists files kept unchanged from the previous version.","summary":"version.publish gains restored_from for a restore; carried there lists only files unchanged from the previous version","tags":["docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:31:23.488120Z","id":"638ad98630f77762e7b7d92558445f2e8b81602f9f39af6962b14bdf30ddf7da","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/contract.md now documents POST /api/artifacts/<id>/versions:restore under publish, and the Kinds row for version.publish names restored_from and the restore meaning of carried (git show 7c58e3dd28c6 -- docs/contract.md).","supersedes":"cd89cedc177194b40fed826c2cfbb8aadcf00b75946a02a626b70e0da14f802f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:35:44.618796Z","id":"7cb6a69070272895ae9c487f357e212cfed5a93a04d12f2eaed4fae820fcb917","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md 'The Clax app's agent' gives the registration body as {\"harness\": \"app\", \"harness_session_id\": <install ID>, \"cwd\": \"\", \"pid\": ...}. apps/clax_app/lib/app.dart:286 builds AgentRuntime with cwd: _s.supportDir, which apps/clax_app/lib/app.dart:191 passes to HttpClaxApi.registerAppSession (apps/clax_app/lib/clax/http_api.dart:156-170), so the row's cwd is the app's support directory (the '' default in http_api.dart is never used by the app).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":919},"end":{"line":922},"content_hash":"9ea418c61b422418ac70ed80f532fac36901b0583ee25c6c5dcf69ec2f0e129e"},"suggested_fix":"Say \"cwd\": <the app's support directory>.","summary":"The app's session registration is documented with \"cwd\": \"\", but the app sends its application support directory","tags":["review","D1","docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:35:44.618888Z","id":"2ab22e89ddcbaae5d901096bcf80abe59b3e2cff06af12c94e9dcf550325929c","body":{"detail":"docs/usage.md says adb is in ~/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools and then runs `adb devices` and `adb install -r ...` as written. `which adb` finds nothing here (only ~/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb exists), so the block fails with 'command not found' when pasted.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":372},"end":{"line":379},"content_hash":"8cdb9113f3780785d6091dd24da8e6425976fce71a7051c739879800390d15dd"},"suggested_fix":"Use ~/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb in the commands, or add a line exporting PATH=\"$HOME/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools:$PATH\" before them.","summary":"The Android steps run bare `adb`, which is not on PATH on this machine","tags":["review","D1","docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:35:44.619069Z","id":"3ce8945573b50018ce598527b41a86a6501c0e0e907facd18efaf5ff7782150d","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/db_caller.rs reader_caller checks Store::may_read first (crates/clax-core/src/store/grants.rs:151: a share needs B's db.share, so a B without db is Granted only through an owner grant) and returns not_granted unless Granted; only then does it check doc_declared and return not_declared. So 'when B does not declare db it is 403 not_declared, the token included' holds only for a granted pair; an ungranted B without db answers not_granted. The page side (web/shell/src/caps/cross-read.ts) reports not_declared itself after reading B's declaration.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":3188},"end":{"line":3192},"content_hash":"f02b03eb15ac8c41eb21e6ece180dca001948d97da8c6a7d4bca873998054645"},"suggested_fix":"Order the sentence as the code does: without consent 403 not_granted; with consent but B not declaring db, 403 not_declared.","summary":"Over HTTP, a source without db answers not_declared only when the owner granted the read; with no grant it is not_granted","tags":["review","D1","docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:35:44.619204Z","id":"99932e97414db194d4c33e8a15038efc02126f22cc08a3f6b9b5fdefae4a8111","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-core/src/db.rs ids_at refuses an array longer than MAX_READ_IDS (16) before deduplicating, so 17 entries naming 2 IDs are 400 invalid_capabilities. 'at most 16 artifact IDs ... a repeated ID counts once' reads as if the limit applied after deduplication.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":3162},"end":{"line":3164},"content_hash":"4c69efa06950d51a2e63362d4b53f594e576b543eec81b12e5b562839eeb63ce"},"suggested_fix":"'Each list holds at most 16 entries ...; a repeated ID is kept once.'","summary":"The 16-ID limit counts entries before duplicates are dropped","tags":["review","D1","docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:36:38.269152Z","id":"4e5478faca60bc3c447e248b277fa4f21ef22c8c9aa72dab52023f7c06a3611e","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/contract.md 'The Clax app's agent' now gives cwd as the app's application support directory, as apps/clax_app/lib/app.dart:286 sends it.","supersedes":"7cb6a69070272895ae9c487f357e212cfed5a93a04d12f2eaed4fae820fcb917","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:36:38.269575Z","id":"84ddf52ae294ac68993d734abe2e049d34dbe57e046808d59d46d88b7f442823","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/contract.md now says each list holds at most 16 entries, repeats included, and that a repeated ID is kept once after that (crates/clax-core/src/db.rs ids_at).","supersedes":"99932e97414db194d4c33e8a15038efc02126f22cc08a3f6b9b5fdefae4a8111","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:36:38.269918Z","id":"6d15370147ea3cf3383ea0bda358add4fca42bf35ea6950c4436d550c5d25379","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/contract.md 'Reading another app's documents' now says not_granted comes first and not_declared only for a granted pair, and that the owner's page reports not_declared itself (crates/clax-server/src/db_caller.rs reader_caller order).","supersedes":"3ce8945573b50018ce598527b41a86a6501c0e0e907facd18efaf5ff7782150d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T12:36:38.270256Z","id":"33a6aa7c2e0f95690e0b3644e633f35e34b8fda1b145cb984ea0f7079d6999aa","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/usage.md's Android block now starts with export PATH=\"$HOME/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools:$PATH\" before adb devices and adb install, so it runs as pasted.","supersedes":"2ab22e89ddcbaae5d901096bcf80abe59b3e2cff06af12c94e9dcf550325929c","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T04:20:09.515208Z","id":"dce0e708dbd4ffbbd4bb2befc3fb8800f5c2294c1af5be02c982c554e5f3a373","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/content.rs content_policy sends only the sandbox (main origin) or frame-ancestors (artifact origin); connect-src 'none' appears only in snapshot_policy, for live pages. Either the daemon should send connect-src 'none' on app content, or this sentence (and the module comments in crates/clax-server/src/net.rs:1-3 and web/shell/src/caps/net.ts:5) should say what does keep a page from connecting.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3760},"end":{"line":3762},"content_hash":"f736141ca0b8291d6b8ac7b5c705d6810eef365e0b94e679cfa204caddd08fe5"},"summary":"The net section says an app page's content security policy keeps connect-src 'none', but artifact content carries no connect-src","tags":["session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T04:20:09.598607Z","id":"c7ba08db1f28af9ea5ae8848a0916afb87beec9e98d85b81639b0803f6847ff7","body":{"detail":"Until the updater restores the title, icon and capabilities too, a rolled-back app keeps the update's declaration. Update this sentence if the fix changes what a rollback restores.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2106},"end":{"line":2110},"content_hash":"6c094b21858797d0bc89e4bc486a576593fe69d31ccbe976941602f238f7daa5"},"summary":"Sharing apps step 7 says the version before the update is restored, but versions:restore puts back files only (see blocker fd8f300e on apps/clax_app/lib/bundle/updater.dart:446:459)","tags":["session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T04:30:15.139087Z","id":"a2d8f71d280d2c5cee210c40524a4811537af80f411c7bbcf6c43fa2727d7166","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"Sharing apps step 7 now says the rollback publishes the version before with its files, title, icon and capabilities, the app is covered and the agent held off during the run, data/users documents are not in the snapshot, and a stale interrupted update asks Restore or Discard; docs/usage.md likewise. The behaviour landed in f1c07f346b12.","supersedes":"c7ba08db1f28af9ea5ae8848a0916afb87beec9e98d85b81639b0803f6847ff7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T04:49:33.998045Z","id":"97015ed52a1213c76296159d82b3bf3c0a856abc1610b706c0a9377117892ee0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:20e1ed98c3fc","summary":"Fixed in 20e1ed98c3fc: artifact content now carries connect-src 'none' and loads only its own files, assets, data: and blob: in both frame modes (crates/clax-server/src/routes/content.rs content_policy/load_policy); the contract's net section and security model say so (7c58e3dd28c6). Evidence: cargo test -p clax-server --test integration: 517 passed (api_content::an_artifacts_content_loads_only_its_own_files_and_connects_nowhere); npx playwright test e2e/lockdown.spec.ts: 2 passed (subdomain and sandbox: direct fetch to another host and to the daemon refused with connect-src violations, another host's <img> refused with img-src and never requested, kit modules, own image and db work); full e2e: 484 passed, 48 skipped, 0 failed.","supersedes":"dce0e708dbd4ffbbd4bb2befc3fb8800f5c2294c1af5be02c982c554e5f3a373","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-16T05:19:28.067024Z","id":"4785edb8565c8a6606d69d763cd3deee79ab81c3164051847b9fbf4efbcf9a4b","body":{"detail":"web/bridge/src/protocol.ts documents both (clax:route {depth, hash} after a welcome with app: true; clax:back {depth}). The contract's app-shell section describes clax:scroll beside them.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1831},"end":{"line":1834},"content_hash":"5b27e433fc3030cf32729ff730fb1621eb76cf8887f96ac7355ff0eaf8f4be22"},"suggested_fix":"Add both next to clax:scroll in docs/contract.md.","summary":"docs/contract.md does not describe the bridge's clax:route report or the shell's clax:back order","tags":["docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-16T05:31:59.481288Z","id":"b3bf5e0498569277982c73432b922a69967e7d059246282de647554cbe59e291","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"Fixed: the Clax app section's Back paragraph describes clax:route {depth, hash} and clax:back {depth}; the claxHost table gains back(), the messages table history {back} and the Not sent notice.","supersedes":"4785edb8565c8a6606d69d763cd3deee79ab81c3164051847b9fbf4efbcf9a4b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-16T16:12:07.619009Z","id":"ecbf58834974ad92d27bd20c00f797051746b537120ba94ada74deb8c0147fd8","body":{"detail":"HomeRoute counts shownWithin from each page load it builds (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_route.dart:170-185, 198-207): the first one, and the reload after each Undo or Reset. A new version landing while the home screen shows is not timed (record 81d5a0ac9b). 'Undo last change (back to the version before the latest)' restores files only; the capabilities stay (docs/contract.md:232-248).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":559},"end":{"line":565},"content_hash":"fe20fe52c3b4f94d1b764ad529e8ccf08d637b5acb8f1755ad0367c1be435159"},"summary":"usage.md says the built-in home screen takes over when the home app has not shown within 10 seconds of a start; it is 10 s from each load of its page, and Undo keeps the declaration","tags":["review","home","docs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-16T16:49:54.838795Z","id":"03c8f9469c17dc5dee235972b0257782003fd08daf26437922c455e72125dc57","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"Fixed in 7c58e3dd28c6: docs/usage.md:559-572 says the 10 s rule runs from each load of the home app's page (the first at a start, the reload after each Undo or Reset), describes the step-back Undo, and says Undo restores files only, the capabilities staying; usage.md:653-654 says the same of the Versions tab's Restore and Undo. git show 7c58e3dd28c6 -- docs/usage.md shows the change.","supersedes":"ecbf58834974ad92d27bd20c00f797051746b537120ba94ada74deb8c0147fd8","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T12:48:34.779686Z","id":"e188686964d04e7797976a045a52971a0dd915682d9cb139f2a5370d4d3e70b2","body":{"detail":"JobsOwners::claim (crates/clax-http/src/jobs_owner.rs) spawns the drain task before answering, so when the old write ends at once the event is published before the HTTP answer is written, and the stream may deliver it first. A worker that follows the contract literally (on owner_draining, wait for held true, then claim) waits forever. The app's queue handles it (apps/clax_app/test/queue/update_test.dart, 'a drain's end heard before the claim's answer claims again'), but the contract and the route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs:182-197) state only the order that follows.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2495},"end":{"line":2502},"content_hash":"cfd92fc9ebc64fbea0ed0974d898260bb9caed9b078ea9801d12acf607489e4c"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the contract: a held true heard for the client after the claim was sent counts, so on owner_draining claim again at once if one was heard.","summary":"The contract does not say that the drain's jobs_owner held true can arrive on the stream before the claim's 409 owner_draining","tags":["session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T22:27:54.171866Z","id":"dfbad1dc2ecbe2eb3ff3aa5e6846a78a9c3fb8d7f21794972932ffbac6971be7","body":{"detail":"JobsOwners::claim (crates/clax-http/src/jobs_owner.rs) spawns the drain task before answering, so when the old write ends at once the event is published before the HTTP answer is written, and the stream may deliver it first. A worker that follows the contract literally (on owner_draining, wait for held true, then claim) waits forever. The app's queue handles it (apps/clax_app/test/queue/update_test.dart, 'a drain's end heard before the claim's answer claims again'), but the contract and the route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs:182-197) state only the order that follows.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2496},"end":{"line":2503},"content_hash":"cfd92fc9ebc64fbea0ed0974d898260bb9caed9b078ea9801d12acf607489e4c"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the contract: a held true heard for the client after the claim was sent counts, so on owner_draining claim again at once if one was heard.","summary":"The contract does not say that the drain's jobs_owner held true can arrive on the stream before the claim's 409 owner_draining","supersedes":"e188686964d04e7797976a045a52971a0dd915682d9cb139f2a5370d4d3e70b2","tags":["session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T15:48:29.105032Z","id":"b4b2294155d77685bdace9aa6060c078ee680f919c909cc4f6502a633e528c1b","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7c58e3dd28c6","summary":"docs/contract.md (Jobs, the queue owner) and the claim route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs) now say the drain's held true may reach the stream before the 409 owner_draining, that one heard after the claim was sent counts, and that the worker then claims again at once. Evidence: git show 7c58e3dd28c6 -- docs/contract.md.","supersedes":"e188686964d04e7797976a045a52971a0dd915682d9cb139f2a5370d4d3e70b2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T02:15:44.978208Z","id":"4d35601cd97c935d2127445e627941ad8ed5f40ea8a3c4afa56c4a35bd5a6867","body":{"detail":"POST /api/jobs/<id>/steps/<key> takes reply.session_id (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs ReplyBody; Store::commit_reply_step in crates/clax-store/src/jobs.rs) and uses it when the version's publishing session has ended or the version names none (a change imported from the app's ledger by crates/clax-store/src/imports.rs, or one whose session the person ended or revoked). The Dart runner in apps/clax_app still sends {threads, body, resolve, ack}; until it adds its registered session's ID as session_id on every reply step, such a change answers 400 unknown_session on each attempt.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2613},"end":{"line":2626},"content_hash":"936ba9bdb685f4171712cf5e43e45a9812653dd822cd6645d205f4396c776daa"},"suggested_fix":"In the app's job runner, set reply.session_id to the session it registered at launch (POST /api/sessions, before JobsLoaded) on every reply step.","summary":"The app's worker does not yet send session_id in its reply steps, so a change whose publishing session ended still cannot reply","tags":["app","contract","jobs","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T02:43:14.301581Z","id":"8788f54bab30a22089a9ffec4be9756c0e9b75e316fcf87d71d4d7cb3968b521","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:f1c07f346b12","summary":"EffectRunner._reply (apps/clax_app/lib/queue/runner.dart) sends reply.session_id, the session the worker registered at launch (EffectRunner.session, WorkerAgent.session), on every reply step; without one to hand it sends the step bare and the core decides. FakeJobs (apps/clax_app/test/support/fake_core_jobs.dart) refuses unknown_session as commit_reply_step does. Evidence: bazel test //apps/clax_app:queue_runner_test //apps/clax_app:queue_reply_real_test passes at f1c07f346b12; with runner.dart from flutter-app, 4 runner tests and queue_reply_real_test (the embedded core answering the runner's Reply with Refused unknown_session on a version that names no session) fail.","supersedes":"4d35601cd97c935d2127445e627941ad8ed5f40ea8a3c4afa56c4a35bd5a6867","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T02:46:16.741611Z","id":"b91ee745b29b0fcd46371e1d23b740f56eb9a70c8ec8cea62c775f896c64a99e","body":{"detail":"scripts/perf-app-launch.sh --android now reads each launch's timings from the app's log, because a release APK is not debuggable and 'adb run-as dev.empathic.clax cat files/timings.jsonl' fails on it (the earlier script read the file that way, so it could not measure such a build either). The baseline step 5 measures the build installed on the phone, which comes from before the app ran on the embedded core and so from before the log lines: every launch would count as missing. Options: (a) take the phone baseline from a debuggable build of the baseline commit and give the script back a run-as path for it; (b) backport the clax-timing: log lines onto the baseline commit in a scratch build; (c) take no phone baseline and compare the phone against its first measured in-process build.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1082},"end":{"line":1097},"content_hash":"299e52f36dd259f1a71b2741714ed1699c91f3b52ea945df12b491bd319dc4ef"},"summary":"The phone's launch baseline (step 5) cannot be taken on a release build from before 7c58e3dd28c6: it logs no clax-timing: lines and run-as cannot read its timings.jsonl","tags":["perf","android","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:07:07.074149Z","id":"5c91017d84e3ea75af5d567f8d3621c96e68fb604e89dacbff6620e1ef684a62","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/threads.rs:1282 sets person = false for a resolved thread, so send_target (threads.rs:1196-1235) skips the handoff_target branch and returns unknown_agent ('no live agent on this artifact has that handle', or 'not an agent handle' for a name) before send_routed can answer thread_resolved. Only a to that already owns or watches the artifact reaches send_in's thread_resolved (crates/clax-store/src/feedback.rs send_in). The gallery dialog and CLI comments send --to (docs/contract.md 'comments send') would show the wrong reason. No test covers a resolved thread with a non-watching to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1459},"end":{"line":1475},"content_hash":"64e56d14fb83e4a9415b80ede360b47e7b526392de3abb9f20e5ed3b2f5472b9"},"suggested_fix":"Check t.status == \"resolved\" in send() before send_target and return thread_resolved, or change the contract text; add a test either way.","summary":"Contract says a send naming a non-watching agent on a resolved thread is 400 thread_resolved; the daemon answers 400 unknown_agent","tags":["review","review:new-artifact","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:10:48.567809Z","id":"6b20fa29e049ec6e1477c62637de9b1b03dd14da7b617edfae3df15f7a5d5730","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md:1470-1471 says 'a resolved thread 400 thread_resolved'. crates/clax-server/src/routes/threads.rs:1281-1283 sets person = person && t.status != \"resolved\" before send_target; with person false, send_target (:1205-1234) skips the handoff_target branch for a to that is not a live watcher and returns 400 unknown_agent ('no live agent on this artifact has that handle' or 'not an agent handle') before send_routed (:1291) is reached. grep of crates/clax-server/tests/api_briefs.rs for thread_resolved finds no case.","kind":"comment","references":"5c91017d84e3ea75af5d567f8d3621c96e68fb604e89dacbff6620e1ef684a62","summary":"confirmed: a resolved thread naming a non-watching agent answers unknown_agent, not the contract's thread_resolved","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:48:23.027070Z","id":"04b29731b85fb6cc2bf6783c54e2ca57dd3d893e4722d1e8142196bf5e62b0fa","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:a46ec7df2","summary":"Fixed in the code: send() answers 400 thread_resolved for a resolved thread before resolving to (crates/clax-server/src/routes/threads.rs), as the contract says. Evidence: api_briefs::the_dialog_lists_and_a_send_reaches_only_agents_that_may_read_and_watch asserts (400, thread_resolved) and no watch for a non-watching to; //crates/clax-server:integration__4 passes.","supersedes":"5c91017d84e3ea75af5d567f8d3621c96e68fb604e89dacbff6620e1ef684a62","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T17:23:32.344766Z","id":"d5bbb3797b32f887ce537781d285e7dd53af280aee5b5b26e390fd35d1702ccf","body":{"detail":"It says the rest of a group is \"listed by the plain query with artifact=<its ID> (or artifact=none)\". A caller that follows that literally drops the other parameters (read=unread, q, kind, agent, dates) and gets a page of the group's items under no filter, mixing read and unread. The shell repeats its filter (web/shell/src/q/InboxPage.svelte more()), and the API test passes read=unread (crates/clax-server/tests/api_inbox.rs), but the contract does not require it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":3136},"end":{"line":3153},"content_hash":"0adef5975912a6b033ad4d6147bf74ee71ac854bb86a38657aebd7ba2ee69ed5"},"suggested_fix":"Say: listed by the plain query with the same parameters, artifact set to its ID (or none), and before set to it.","summary":"The contract does not say a group's next_cursor pages with the same filters, read included","tags":["review","review:inbox-by-artifact","inbox","docs","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T17:26:04.630749Z","id":"e7e31801a972a0a6c838b4a51e054f799e285dbcdc98fac42e110d1f9fa59d75","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md:3148-3149 reads \"`next_cursor` is the `before` of the rest, listed by the plain query with `artifact=<its ID>` (or `artifact=none`), or null.\" It does not say the group's other parameters (read, q, kind, agent, dates) must be repeated. The shell does repeat them (web/shell/src/q/InboxPage.svelte:207, inbox.page({...filter, read: which, artifact: key}, ...)).","kind":"comment","references":"d5bbb3797b32f887ce537781d285e7dd53af280aee5b5b26e390fd35d1702ccf","summary":"confirmed: the contract names only artifact= for paging a group's rest, not the other filters","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:21:41.336972Z","id":"1979d99fb42d9498bb70b6c5859ded770e0742e29349088a04052ad4187cde25","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:6fe6abb7b","summary":"Fixed: docs/contract.md says a group's rest is the plain query with the same parameters, artifact set to the group's artifact_id (or none) and before to its cursor; the route's doc comment says the same.","supersedes":"d5bbb3797b32f887ce537781d285e7dd53af280aee5b5b26e390fd35d1702ccf","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T19:09:28.096168Z","id":"86d3418fe6db945e305c42f6a3e1629b2ee44a3b8303c46f72ba9dfbd107d2f7","body":{"detail":"The prerequisite is Node 22, the Node MODULE.bazel pins for the web, Pi and browser checks and native-check.yml installs. demo/local.ts and crates/clax-worker's scripts (e2e*.mjs, test-wasm.mjs, deploy.mjs) use no API newer than Node 22; .ts scripts run by node rely on type stripping, unflagged from Node 22.18.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":9},"end":{"line":9},"content_hash":"5e4fd852a88daddd1c554f232f5b49da8d6ad99f6f093d825f6237e288cb4ef3"},"summary":"Node 26 for development","supersedes":"4bf9be8f14c9bb823e2fc299dc181992172fb8a6e19598a4440247c1aba1caaa","tags":["build","revisit:a hosted or demo script needs a Node API newer than 22","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:45:14.254765Z","id":"ea2bf5be0ac792c6583e4e9543afc892c13d28e6de3849360aad0a5fbc7a3639","body":{"detail":"scripts/test-release-script.sh has no case for: the tag existing here or on origin on another commit (scripts/release.sh:218-221), the finished-release refusal (222-225), a checkout newer than the version or with disagreeing versions (199-202), missing tools or a non-GitHub origin; nor for resuming after the pin commit is made but not pushed, after main is pushed (steps 13-14), after a failed CI or publish-pi run, a rejected push of main, a Release run that is not listed yet when the wait starts (the fake gh lists run 101 as soon as the tag is pushed, so await_output's polling until a run appears never runs), or bazel-sync still changing files after 4 runs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":567},"end":{"line":571},"content_hash":"07e9fc5d6ae8a63e9b7ed4209240ba560c5977c7c208d8a0b90064b8f3e76e72"},"suggested_fix":"Add those cases, or narrow the sentence in docs/usage.md to what the test runs.","summary":"The release command's test is said to cover each refusal and the resumes, but several refusals and most resume points are untested","tags":["review","review:release-script","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:47:33.575745Z","id":"b5c0659a95c98ea6db5899e4dd04e4d6216af81789419a2d25fc29ea002ecdd2","body":{"detail":"docs/usage.md:567-571 says the test runs \"resuming after a failed just ci and after a failed or unfinished Release run, and each refusal\". scripts/test-release-script.sh's refusal cases (287-320) cover only uncommitted/untracked files, behind origin, a foreign commit, another branch, older/equal/malformed versions, a tag only origin has, gh signed out, broken signing and an unknown flag. `grep -nE 'disagree|GitHub repository|exists here|exists on origin|not on PATH|4th run'` on it finds nothing; the only \"finished\" match (228-230) is a no-op rerun, not the refusal at scripts/release.sh:222-225. GH_REPO is exported at test line 29, so resolve_repo's non-GitHub refusal (release.sh:169) cannot run. Resume cases are only at steps 4 and 8 (232-272). The fake gh (test line 84) lists run 101 as soon as the tag is on origin, so await_output's empty-poll loop is never exercised. Kind concern is right.","kind":"comment","references":"ea2bf5be0ac792c6583e4e9543afc892c13d28e6de3849360aad0a5fbc7a3639","summary":"confirmed: the test covers far fewer refusals and resume points than docs/usage.md:567-571 claims","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T02:46:30.660299Z","id":"3d3b93cf0bf8b28478516189285da9ae363972a266e9de6b06029dfa345f43f6","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:cf9ed3d07","summary":"The listed gaps have cases in scripts/test-release-script.sh (tags on another commit here and on origin, a newer or disagreeing checkout version, a missing just, a non-GitHub origin, the pin made but not pushed, steps 13-14 after main is pushed, failed CI and publish runs, a refused push of main, a Release run listed late or never, bazel-sync that never settles), and docs/usage.md lists what the test covers, naming the refusals it leaves out (missing Bazel, python3, curl, npm, pnpm). Evidence: //scripts:release_script PASSED at 0d25dd1f7 with 52 checks.","supersedes":"ea2bf5be0ac792c6583e4e9543afc892c13d28e6de3849360aad0a5fbc7a3639","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T03:03:55.566260Z","id":"f0056e6622c61d913ba625b60b8fc66ca4db4898512d86c51dd7ea602dadff34","body":{"detail":"docs/usage.md:521 says the release refuses unless the checkout is 'equal to origin/main', but scripts/release.sh:321-339 accepts being ahead by the release's commits and behind once the release commit exists (the merge path). docs/usage.md:558 says the package is published 'from the pin commit, whatever main's tip is by then', but scripts/release.sh:220-227 and 652 publish from the merge commit when step 12 merged. The script header (scripts/release.sh:13-14, 'not behind origin/main') has the same gap.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":519},"end":{"line":560},"content_hash":"784fb8a28ec2c59442b5cd1b3e4a9f3185feab68d0cdd92d232265143a585278"},"suggested_fix":"Describe the actual preflight rule (behind is allowed once the release commit exists and is not on origin/main) and say 'the pin commit, or the merge on it' at docs/usage.md:558.","summary":"The Releasing section still says the checkout must equal origin/main and that the Pi package is published from the pin commit","tags":["review","review:release-script-2","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T03:05:39.189638Z","id":"2a04931810787dd8f033b1fb05d2703dde59a390f26bd43e37c83e52257690c1","body":{"detail":"docs/usage.md:520-522 says the release refuses unless the checkout is 'equal to origin/main'; scripts/release.sh:321-326 allows being behind once REL_COMMIT exists and is not on origin/main, and :329-339 allows being ahead by the release, pin and merge commits. docs/usage.md:557-558 says the package is published 'from the pin commit, whatever main's tip is by then'; scripts/release.sh:220-226 sets PUBLISH_COMMIT to the merge on the pin commit when there is one, and :652 dispatches with sha=$PUBLISH_COMMIT. The header at scripts/release.sh:13-14 says 'not behind origin/main'.","kind":"comment","references":"f0056e6622c61d913ba625b60b8fc66ca4db4898512d86c51dd7ea602dadff34","summary":"confirmed: the preflight and publish-commit wording disagree with scripts/release.sh","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T03:21:06.267634Z","id":"dd40bccc395fcb0200303866db31404ce3c50f8526193b44b859bba1e73e087c","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:4fde7462c","summary":"docs/usage.md and scripts/release.sh's header give the actual rule (ahead of origin/main only by the release's commits; behind only between the release commit and step 12, which merges origin/main in) and say the Pi package is published from the pin commit, or the merge on it when step 12 merged. Evidence: the text at docs/usage.md 'Releasing' and scripts/release.sh:13-20 at 4fde7462c.","supersedes":"f0056e6622c61d913ba625b60b8fc66ca4db4898512d86c51dd7ea602dadff34","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-21T19:04:46.728021Z","id":"f52d324a41c93afa9fbb2470bfec64a2049a088505d29026d72419e2aeedb778","body":{"detail":"Since the Clax app dropped its launch snapshot, HomeHost.call (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_host.dart, case 'shown' and case 'tiles') resolves shown() with null and takes tiles() without doing anything, and the shipped home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) calls neither tiles() nor reads opened. The contract text here, its copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (runtime capabilities list), web/contract/clax-extensions.d.ts, the app's prompt copies (apps/clax_app/assets/prompt, kept by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh) and web/shell/src/caps/home.ts still offer both. Options: remove tiles() and the {opened} answer from the contract and every copy (an agent then stops writing tiles() calls into home apps), or keep them as documented no-ops for home apps already written.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6036},"end":{"line":6038},"content_hash":"46b35fff334dd252970d856668c02df6b944c56dfaf93197b0af33993246a08f"},"summary":"The home capability's contract still describes the launch image: shown() resolving {opened: id} and tiles(list) kept with an image shown on the next cold start, which the Clax app no longer does","tags":["status:needs-decision","home","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T04:55:09.785497Z","id":"6af660c717febc8201af1b6da1eb05057743c9254c3d466289c15c3b51f1e8b4","body":{"detail":"grep -rn getApps docs plugins finds only this row. The home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) offers it from More and its empty state.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6036},"end":{"line":6036},"content_hash":"33416509a2278633f3206fe649c38ba3c7c55c29c353c70e95d86ab3040dbda0"},"summary":"getApps() is in the home capability's table only: the contract's summary paragraph and the plugin skills' copies do not list it, and the Mac toolbar and app menu do not offer Get my apps","tags":["docs","area:remote","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T05:43:06.868997Z","id":"4a14fb9d66a79949c63ec06ec5311d8bb3360aa289edfd438e33b24bc616706b","body":{"detail":"web/shell/src/app/host.ts posts remote (the remote a linked app's thread came from, absent for one started here) on each row of the threads message, and apps/clax_app/lib/ui/host_protocol.dart ThreadSummary.remote reads it; this table still lists {id, status, summary, label, sent, comments, version, clip}.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2604},"end":{"line":2604},"content_hash":"910d37aad696ec82bf83dc7c6868f5a6eab34f7c6d161aef8218f128e5e65623"},"summary":"The host's threads message gained remote, which the contract's Messages to the app table does not list","tags":["review","docs","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T06:07:25.957997Z","id":"6a7e10aea94fe687adfe4d6cda2c8585674a37a306e014e8c9c441f4efc451e9","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:fdfdf5de0","summary":"The Messages to the app table's threads row lists remote? (the remote a linked app's thread came from, absent for one started here), as web/shell/src/app/host.ts posts it.","supersedes":"4a14fb9d66a79949c63ec06ec5311d8bb3360aa289edfd438e33b24bc616706b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T14:59:27.479433Z","id":"8d40829145ede593ab338c16fea1db23c884fa62c4c76910c66c89cfc579d01d","body":{"detail":"POST /api/artifacts/<id>/push mode app with version and forked_from (crates/clax-server/src/routes/remotes.rs push, public.rs Mode::App, push_app, app_version_key), POST /api/artifacts forked_from on a hosted Clax (routes/artifacts.rs create), and the forwarding routes of crates/clax-server/src/routes/remote_apps.rs exist only on akesling/app-pp-r. This text was written from `git show akesling/app-pp-r:<path>` at 9521ddfc9. On merge, check each route, body field and error code here against the merged code; the route table rows for /api/remotes/{id}/... and PUT /api/artifacts/{aid}/remotes/{rid}/bundle come with that branch's docs/contract.md change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1290},"end":{"line":1316},"content_hash":"bfd5fc4b4492a84f2ce34f1b2142c232c54feaae1c218994e6d56095e619235a"},"summary":"The core's app mode bullet documents routes that are on akesling/app-pp-r (9521ddfc9) and not yet on this branch: re-check it once that branch merges","tags":["docs","area:remote","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T14:59:27.479787Z","id":"18162f148aa944fdc5e2a5e537f3a4698bbbd3074b4ed81f48781634dae55203","body":{"detail":"Those are still changing on akesling/app-pp-d (373191c2b: apps/clax_app/lib/remote/sync_state.dart, merge.dart, ui/remote/diverged_sheet.dart, home_host.dart HomeApp.sync, crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs head's capabilities). The Tell @owner bullet here was written from that branch's ui/bundle_sheets.dart (TellOwner, tellOwnerDraft) and ui/remote/remote_flows.dart tellOwner, and the comments route it posts through is akesling/app-pp-r's POST /api/remotes/<rid>/artifacts/<raid>/comments. The Apps from the Clax app bullet 'The head' (docs/contract.md:1238-1247) lists the head's fields without capabilities, which 86357646b adds on that branch.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3329},"end":{"line":3405},"content_hash":"339aec66b125900e6718e2484fd5b616c39eeb4bd05994c27fa019bdc5cc340b"},"summary":"Linked apps does not yet describe sync states, divergence (Combine, Take theirs, Publish as a new app), forks of linked apps, the tiles' sync badges or the head's capabilities: add them once akesling/app-pp-d lands, and re-check Tell @owner","tags":["docs","area:remote","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T17:29:52.134884Z","id":"148960ba665a836ebc5c3a1fae11f9c191b83da084b8af5d030a23a33068507e","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:4de5ab9e4","summary":"akesling/app-pp-r merged into this branch (5a282c5b9). The core's app mode bullet in docs/contract.md 'Apps from the Clax app' now matches the merged code: push body {mode: app, version, remote?, forked_from?, title?} and its refusals (crates/clax-server/src/routes/remotes.rs push doc and checks), app_version_key's ':on:<n>' (public.rs), the forwarding routes and their sandboxed, nosniff answers (routes/remote_apps.rs), and forked_from on the hosted POST /api/artifacts (routes/artifacts.rs create). The route table rows for those routes are in docs/contract.md from the merge. ./scripts/test-plugins.sh: exit 0, 160 PASS, no FAIL.","supersedes":"8d40829145ede593ab338c16fea1db23c884fa62c4c76910c66c89cfc579d01d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T17:29:52.304392Z","id":"ee39be3b48863e923a107aeed6cbed03fc57adf8bdcdf384f8bc5a1f19b8b7fd","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:4de5ab9e4","summary":"akesling/app-pp-d merged into this branch (5a282c5b9). Linked apps in docs/contract.md now describes the sync states and tile badges as apps/clax_app/lib/remote/sync_state.dart (syncState, syncBadge) and ui/remote/remote_card.dart (syncStateLabel) give them, including the recipient's ⑂ Changed here fork state; Bring vN into mine and Diverged's three choices as remote/merge.dart (bringIntoMine, resolve) and ui/remote/diverged_sheet.dart; Publish fork with '<title> (yours)' as ui/remote/remote_flows.dart and remote/publisher.dart publishFork. The head bullet lists note and capabilities (crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs head). Tell @owner was checked against the merged ui/bundle_sheets.dart and remote_flows.dart tellOwner. ./scripts/test-plugins.sh: exit 0, 160 PASS, no FAIL.","supersedes":"18162f148aa944fdc5e2a5e537f3a4698bbbd3074b4ed81f48781634dae55203","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.151201Z","id":"6676fba6c14eb51db276ad6a798a7f1d3e4bc3fdf8896bc4b963e73528fd6188","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs:61-68 (`card`) answers name, login and public_id, used for head.published_by, head.owner, bundle.published_by and app.published_by/owner (app_bundles.rs:344, 355, 429-430). docs/contract.md:1262-1263, 1278 and the doc comments at app_bundles.rs:293 and :414 say {name, login}. Also docs/contract.md:1219 says `GET …/sharing` carries `comments`; it does only for whoever may share (crates/clax-server/src/hosted.rs:861 vs the can_share: false answer).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1260},"end":{"line":1279},"content_hash":"b1691213da89fe65a38829ab681f2b3cdb751a4762f9f6df323df0389cda9104"},"suggested_fix":"Document public_id (null when there is none) in the contract and both doc comments, and say `GET …/sharing` carries `comments` for whoever may share.","summary":"The head's and the artifact's `app` person cards are documented as {name, login}, but the server sends {name, login, public_id}","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.151409Z","id":"e943e459fd3378d3b4e80ba66df600ad7789995f2071bcea0e18194b1517218f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-store/src/linked.rs:389-404 (mirrored_author_key) keys an agent comment with no person `remote:<origin>#*owner` and any other author with none `remote:<origin>#*unconnected`. docs/contract.md:1132 and :5336 name only `<public ID>`. A reader approving keys from the People page needs the two special forms.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1130},"end":{"line":1134},"content_hash":"b885fe048914275bb87643bfd0eca7d1512cabe1343a1f9540372d77d0a336c5"},"suggested_fix":"Add: an author with no public ID there is keyed `remote:<origin>#*owner` (that Clax's owner's agents) or `remote:<origin>#*unconnected` (an anonymous viewer).","summary":"The mirrored-comment visibility key is documented as remote:<origin>#<public ID>, but authors without a public ID get *owner or *unconnected","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.151595Z","id":"48f9058681c02c4a86f88fae7b73f217f278fb84b52841eecd5163100a885353","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/shell/get_page.rs:297 refuse_agents gives not_for_agents for the owner's agents; another agent token is refused 403 forbidden by DeniedToGrants (crates/clax-http/src/agent.rs), and the `refusals` layer (get_page.rs:340-373) passes on only not_for_agents, turning any other 403 into the generic 404 sign-in page.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1294},"end":{"line":1296},"content_hash":"af72676b0467d2b0f09ef76dc10a785d1c82e2e2dd2b9f7a5a0e1a27caf36001"},"suggested_fix":"Say: the owner's agents are refused 403 `not_for_agents`; any other agent token gets the 404 page.","summary":"The contract says an agent is refused /a/<id>/get 403 not_for_agents; on a hosted Clax a non-owner agent token gets the 404 page","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.151773Z","id":"a40d8b0ce78b8176a89f807b1e79fb0a3f666f531d829f1a4726b1677fc194fc","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/remote_apps.rs `link` (around :285-325) forwards nothing: it writes the link locally with `url` rebuilt as `<origin>/a/<raid>` (remote_apps.rs:313), so the body's `url` field is accepted and unused. docs/contract.md:1326-1331 says 'Its other routes forward to the remote with the core's token … POST /api/remotes/<remote>/links {artifact_id, remote_artifact_id, url, remote_n}'. A caller that relies on `url` (e.g. a remote under a path prefix) gets a different link than it sent.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1326},"end":{"line":1332},"content_hash":"cb58fc099458d497b98367d6b3661f26889c3644183b2f767454bd3a379801ee"},"suggested_fix":"Describe /links as a local write that needs a signed-in remote, and either drop `url` from the body (deny it) or document that the link's address is always <origin>/a/<remote ID>.","summary":"POST /api/remotes/<remote>/links is listed among routes that forward to the remote, but it only writes a local link and ignores its `url`","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.151960Z","id":"4dbd8714560194281ebd069b54a60d90a8fe498525f45e27cd525c8388a9251b","body":{"detail":"apps/clax_app/lib/ui/remote/remote_flows.dart:166-190 shows the PublishSheet (with Include my data) only when the app has no link (`if (link == null)`); every later publish, manual or automatic, sends includeData false. docs/contract.md:3371-3373 and docs/usage.md:315-318 read as if each publish can include data. A person who wants updated seed data in v5 finds no way to send it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3370},"end":{"line":3373},"content_hash":"ac4e3b6cc127781c947a268783f22eb371e65addaac25d749a7496623fa2c576"},"suggested_fix":"Say: Include my data is chosen on the first publish (and Publish fork); later versions go up without documents. Or offer the choice on later publishes.","summary":"The contract says a publish carries documents when Include my data is on 'for that publish', but only a first publish or Publish fork offers the choice","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.152135Z","id":"6167cbeb9b1d7b17275c25d25e07d5cf0876b3f82d6008332b37fb26b66872a8","body":{"detail":"The table's texts (☁ Live, ↑ Newer here, ⑂ Changed here, ⑂ Changed in both places) are the Overview card's (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/remote/remote_card.dart:19-27). The app screen's pill reads '↑ v<N> here · v<M> public', '↓ v<N> ready' or 'Diverged' (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/working_chip.dart:474-483) and shows nothing for Live, Changed here or Kept mine (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/app_screen.dart:641-657). docs/usage.md:344 ('its pill reads ⑂ Changed here') and :351 ('the app shows ⑂ Changed in both places') repeat it, and docs/usage.md:319 names a 'Publish v<N> on the app's screen' control that has no such label.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3421},"end":{"line":3434},"content_hash":"7889805a540725bdeb504e85d7acc382740ac08d31870127b69689e49b9e08c5"},"suggested_fix":"Attribute the table to the Overview card and home badge, list the screen pill's own texts, and fix docs/usage.md:319, :344 and :351 to name the controls as built (or change the pill to match).","summary":"The sync-state table is said to show as a pill on the app's screen, but the screen's pill has other texts and none for Live, Changed here or Kept mine","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:23:01.152207Z","id":"0c56b0e8e28e92fd475577b064b5f3a4bf2c4f76aac1f8defff304b5a78a5495","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/remotes.rs:268-285 removes the remote whatever public::revoke returns and answers `revoked: false` on failure (logged only). The app's disconnect (apps/clax_app/lib/remote/remote_api.dart, apps/clax_app/lib/ui/settings_screen.dart) does not read `revoked`. Offline, or against a hosted Clax without the revoke route, the token stays live on clax.dev, able to publish and change sharing as the person, while the person was told it was revoked.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":309},"end":{"line":314},"content_hash":"e0f9a09ccf5bef86a698ec9ed4f39c08b62746f9ed31eb391d6e5c81020a9474"},"suggested_fix":"Show 'Couldn't revoke this device on <host>: revoke it under People › Devices' when `revoked` is false, and word usage.md to match.","summary":"usage.md says Disconnect revokes the device, but the core forgets the remote even when the revoke fails, and the app ignores the answer","tags":["review","review:app-push-pull-deep","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.801623Z","id":"a6d34042ddd8620bbe6074f68f42e097ba902925bb71a22ae9223b1edf7c607b","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs:61-67 card() answers {name, login, public_id}, used at :344, :355, :357, :429, :430; the doc comments at :293 and :414 and docs/contract.md:1262-1263 say {name, login}. crates/clax-server/src/hosted.rs:848 answers {artifact_id, can_share: false} with no `comments`; only the can_share branch (:859) adds it, while docs/contract.md:1219 says GET …/sharing carries `comments` unconditionally.","kind":"comment","references":"6676fba6c14eb51db276ad6a798a7f1d3e4bc3fdf8896bc4b963e73528fd6188","summary":"confirmed: the cards carry public_id, undocumented; GET …/sharing carries comments only when can_share","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.801872Z","id":"b10c2c1c568e90641b667f9a1381cdb783ceb7a5670951cb44d0f538ccc128d6","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-store/src/linked.rs:396-400 picks clax_core::visibility::OWNER for an agent with no public ID and UNCONNECTED for any other; crates/clax-core/src/visibility.rs:15,19 define them as \"*unconnected\" and \"*owner\", and remote_author (:30-31) formats `remote:<origin>#<id>`. docs/contract.md:1132 and :5336 name only `remote:<origin>#<public ID>`.","kind":"comment","references":"e943e459fd3378d3b4e80ba66df600ad7789995f2071bcea0e18194b1517218f","summary":"confirmed: authors with no public ID are keyed remote:<origin>#*owner or #*unconnected, which the contract does not name","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.802122Z","id":"b55420ef2516362139b9023e562008cec8213d8ff090647123b918fc76718a84","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/agent.rs:1098-1102: DeniedToGrants rejects any is_grant_request (crates/clax-http/src/auth.rs:154-166, any `Bearer cxa_…`) with grants_refused, 403 `forbidden` (auth.rs:212-216); refuse_agents (agent.rs:1071-1080) gives not_for_agents only to the daemon token with a session or agent marker. crates/clax-server/src/shell/get_page.rs:341-373 passes on a 403 only when its code is not_for_agents, else returns unavailable(). docs/contract.md:1294-1295 says 'an agent is refused 403 not_for_agents'.","kind":"comment","references":"48f9058681c02c4a86f88fae7b73f217f278fb84b52841eecd5163100a885353","summary":"confirmed: only the owner's agents get 403 not_for_agents; a cxa_ agent token gets 403 forbidden, which the hosted refusals layer turns into the 404 page","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.802371Z","id":"86a3fe9c3f6eb463c1da6fe7ebdb1603009cea0c34681c1a3bcd5cf8f7c05281","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/remote_apps.rs:278-281 marks LinkBody.url #[allow(dead_code)]; link() (:292-325) rebuilds url as format!(\"{}/a/{raid}\", remote.origin) at :313 and only calls st.link_remote locally, with no request to the remote. docs/contract.md:1327-1331 lists it among 'other routes [that] forward to the remote with the core's token'.","kind":"comment","references":"a40d8b0ce78b8176a89f807b1e79fb0a3f666f531d829f1a4726b1677fc194fc","summary":"confirmed: POST /api/remotes/<remote>/links forwards nothing and ignores `url`","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.802618Z","id":"bec2688ff7d61eb942181087e4fe8eef605c3a9b73b74cb7434de8c5051ad8ad","body":{"detail":"apps/clax_app/lib/ui/remote/remote_flows.dart:166-167 sets link null only for a fork; :183 starts includeData = false and only the `if (link == null)` branch (:186-208) shows PublishSheet and sets it from the choice. The automatic publish passes includeData: false (apps/clax_app/lib/remote/publisher.dart:510). docs/contract.md:3371-3372 reads 'with the documents only when Include my data is on for that publish', with no word that later publishes cannot turn it on.","kind":"comment","references":"4dbd8714560194281ebd069b54a60d90a8fe498525f45e27cd525c8388a9251b","summary":"confirmed: Include my data is offered only when the app has no link (first publish or Publish fork)","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.802884Z","id":"b9b4abab3e8662c2f0c2be59ea81583a1d668fc7e6bf7ba1dba95e16e6769e7f","body":{"detail":"syncStateLabel (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/remote/remote_card.dart:19-27) is the only source of '☁ Live', '⑂ Changed here' etc., used only at remote_card.dart:144 (grep of apps/clax_app/lib). The screen pill's texts are apps/clax_app/lib/ui/working_chip.dart:474-483 ('↑ v$version here · v$p public', '↓ v$version ready', 'Diverged'), and _pillState (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/app_screen.dart:641-657) has states only for ahead, update and diverged. No 'Publish v<N>' label exists in apps/clax_app/lib except 'Publish v$n and reply' (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/thread_detail.dart:600). docs/contract.md:3421-3423 says the table shows 'as a pill on its screen'; docs/usage.md:319, :344, :351 repeat it.","kind":"comment","references":"6167cbeb9b1d7b17275c25d25e07d5cf0876b3f82d6008332b37fb26b66872a8","summary":"confirmed: the table's texts are the Overview card's; the app screen's pill has other texts and none for Live, Changed here or Kept mine","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T05:25:21.803138Z","id":"c1e5388bbcf44ababd2536dcdfd0bb335c384c62befd2e6ccb2ebb840b903065","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-server/src/routes/remotes.rs:271-284: a failed public::revoke only logs (tracing::warn) and sets revoked false, then remove_remote runs regardless. apps/clax_app/lib/remote/remote_api.dart:222-223 removeRemote discards the answer; apps/clax_app/lib/ui/settings_screen.dart:249-257 shows a snackbar only when the call throws. docs/usage.md:313-314 says 'Disconnect revokes it'.","kind":"comment","references":"0c56b0e8e28e92fd475577b064b5f3a4bf2c4f76aac1f8defff304b5a78a5495","summary":"confirmed: the core forgets the remote whatever the revoke returns, and the app never reads `revoked`","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:53.967932Z","id":"0b0a0939ef54b9bd5cd281c27b5ee33112d73dfe4cdbc196a33f78df584c94b9","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md now gives the head's published_by and owner as {name, login, public_id}, says published_by carries the public ID (the owner's own; null for an owner with none), and that GET .../sharing carries comments to whoever may share it. The doc comments at crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs:293 and :414 still say {name, login}: a crates/ edit, left for whoever owns that file. Close once they match.","kind":"comment","references":"6676fba6c14eb51db276ad6a798a7f1d3e4bc3fdf8896bc4b963e73528fd6188","summary":"Contract fixed in ce209e545; the doc comments in crates/ are left","tags":["session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:53.968199Z","id":"39563f3a8864efc2a01c93a4ab5a3dbd70f2bfdd709b311f326803d691bbf3c3","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md's home bullet in 'Runtime capabilities' now lists getApps (from a click handler, only where chrome().getApps is true). Left: apps/clax_app/assets/prompt/runtime-capabilities.md (regenerated by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh; //apps/clax_app:prompt_sync fails until it is run), the copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (~line 588, 'openSettings and importApp'), and Get my apps in the Mac toolbar and app menu (apps/).","kind":"comment","references":"6af660c717febc8201af1b6da1eb05057743c9254c3d466289c15c3b51f1e8b4","summary":"Contract summary fixed in 0e2cef5da; the skill copies and the Mac toolbar are left","tags":["session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.556349Z","id":"a2d40c0874605018cecd21697e89b7cf16e25b7061d89545a25aff2e8db2bf00","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:ce209e545","summary":"docs/contract.md (mirrored comments, and the visibility list) names remote:<origin>#*owner and #*unconnected for authors with no public ID there, as crates/clax-store/src/linked.rs mirrored_author_key keys them, and that a core signed in with an agent token keys none. Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"e943e459fd3378d3b4e80ba66df600ad7789995f2071bcea0e18194b1517218f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.628747Z","id":"26c56fc630b25d297e0bdf1670e0ec1c67be06face5a2626bb9a3b213e3793e8","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:ce209e545","summary":"docs/contract.md 'Get in Clax' now says the owner's agents get 403 not_for_agents, any other agent credential 403 forbidden (crates/clax-http/src/agent.rs DeniedToGrants), and that a hosted Clax shows every refusal but not_for_agents as its 404 page (get_page.rs refusals). It also describes the 307 to a page's own get or get/index.html (get_page.rs page, Found::File). Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"48f9058681c02c4a86f88fae7b73f217f278fb84b52841eecd5163100a885353","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.702324Z","id":"b919c24fc6b7b65fff9854f057c80d3c48188285fd9058da55ea21628fd39c93","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:ce209e545","summary":"docs/contract.md describes POST /api/remotes/<remote>/links apart from the forwarding routes: a local write needing the core signed in (400 not_connected), url required and unread, the link's address always <origin>/a/<remote_artifact_id> (crates/clax-server/src/routes/remote_apps.rs link). Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"a40d8b0ce78b8176a89f807b1e79fb0a3f666f531d829f1a4726b1677fc194fc","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.774376Z","id":"3026a8a9fe1b430e0def93952e5e2f257da801dcb526a6f574a916c4c5e5074d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e2cef5da","summary":"docs/contract.md 'Publishing' and docs/usage.md 'Publish' say Include my data is offered on a first publish and Publish fork… alone and later versions go up without documents, as apps/clax_app/lib/ui/remote/remote_flows.dart builds it. Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"4dbd8714560194281ebd069b54a60d90a8fe498525f45e27cd525c8388a9251b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.850361Z","id":"a864b2639ea95229169d7127078454c266c2816a05213a672f3e74ad133767dc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e2cef5da","summary":"docs/contract.md attributes the sync-state table to the Overview card and home badge, drops '(Publish v<N>)', lists the screen pill's own texts and taps (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/working_chip.dart, app_screen.dart _pill), and docs/usage.md names the Overview card and the pill as built. Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"6167cbeb9b1d7b17275c25d25e07d5cf0876b3f82d6008332b37fb26b66872a8","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T06:35:54.926280Z","id":"2354608a3fd3534352ee3a6c7bda56871586f97277539aff6ed883612948d6cb","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e2cef5da","summary":"docs/usage.md now says Disconnect forgets clax.dev here and asks it to revoke the device, and that when clax.dev can't be reached the device stays listed under People › Devices there until revoked, as crates/clax-server/src/routes/remotes.rs remove does. The app does not yet tell the person when revoked is false (apps/clax_app/lib/ui/settings_screen.dart); that is an app change. Documentation only: no test applies.","supersedes":"0c56b0e8e28e92fd475577b064b5f3a4bf2c4f76aac1f8defff304b5a78a5495","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T15:36:04.261278Z","id":"b1bbed90901064a5dd728d2dac50de607c7a4a07f09b9d62dc96519ad6f28b59","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:554767afb52fd1524d3dbe45813efc2f733b3afd","summary":"Fixed in 554767afb: the doc comments of crates/clax-server/src/routes/app_bundles.rs head (GET /api/artifacts/<aid>/head) and stamp_app_fields now give published_by and owner as {name, login, public_id}, public_id null for an owner with none, matching docs/contract.md (fixed in ce209e545) and card(). Doc comments only; //crates/clax-server:all passes.","supersedes":"6676fba6c14eb51db276ad6a798a7f1d3e4bc3fdf8896bc4b963e73528fd6188","tags":["session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T15:36:04.341588Z","id":"707126cf9425f1ceab004c806f76eb1e283e92879e1343ba328eeccb3a895595","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:032fe66341289d04ecc4eb3058f85385bf56f065","summary":"Remainder fixed: 4c09716ed lists getApps in the home capability paragraph of all four plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md, word for word as docs/contract.md (scripts/sync-skill-tools.py --check exits 0; the Page contract sections equal the contract's; scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh --check prints nothing). 032fe6634 adds File > Get My Apps... (ClaxCommand.getApps, tag 31 in macos/Runner/Base.lproj/MainMenu.xib, enabled only where HomeHost.offersGetApps) and a Get My Apps item in the home toolbar on the Mac. Tests: test/ui/app_menu_test.dart 'File > Get My Apps... runs Get my apps where it is offered, and is disabled where it is not', test/home/app_screen_home_test.dart 'on the Mac, where Get my apps is offered, the home app's toolbar has Get My Apps, which runs it', test/ui/shortcuts_test.dart tag 31. bazelisk test //apps/clax_app:ui_app_menu_test //apps/clax_app:home_app_screen_home_test //apps/clax_app:ui_shortcuts_test //apps/clax_app:app_test: pass; with the menu handler or the toolbar click removed, both new tests fail. Not run on a Mac build: the xib item is checked only by tag.","supersedes":"6af660c717febc8201af1b6da1eb05057743c9254c3d466289c15c3b51f1e8b4","tags":["session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-22T16:57:09.678462Z","id":"042c276e8c8d28b18c46bb7654354a6c2912b1de664beeb80a104eabb1da27e5","body":{"detail":"Since the Clax app dropped its launch snapshot, HomeHost.call (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_host.dart, case 'shown' and case 'tiles') resolves shown() with null and takes tiles() without doing anything, and the shipped home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) calls neither tiles() nor reads opened. The contract text here, its copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (runtime capabilities list), web/contract/clax-extensions.d.ts, the app's prompt copies (apps/clax_app/assets/prompt, kept by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh) and web/shell/src/caps/home.ts still offer both. Options: remove tiles() and the {opened} answer from the contract and every copy (an agent then stops writing tiles() calls into home apps), or keep them as documented no-ops for home apps already written.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6072},"end":{"line":6074},"content_hash":"46b35fff334dd252970d856668c02df6b944c56dfaf93197b0af33993246a08f"},"summary":"The home capability's contract still describes the launch image: shown() resolving {opened: id} and tiles(list) kept with an image shown on the next cold start, which the Clax app no longer does","supersedes":"f52d324a41c93afa9fbb2470bfec64a2049a088505d29026d72419e2aeedb778","tags":["status:needs-decision","home","session:claude-code:7580ce1b-abf0-421d-8a2a-2be245f95865"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-23T23:51:45.822769Z","id":"15ca54c0075e7a1539e0ff38d9038faf6db77500be199ca7ee88a281a946e4ea","body":{"detail":"Since the Clax app dropped its launch snapshot, HomeHost.call (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_host.dart, case 'shown' and case 'tiles') resolves shown() with null and takes tiles() without doing anything, and the shipped home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) calls neither tiles() nor reads opened. The contract text here, its copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (runtime capabilities list), web/contract/clax-extensions.d.ts, the app's prompt copies (apps/clax_app/assets/prompt, kept by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh) and web/shell/src/caps/home.ts still offer both. Options: remove tiles() and the {opened} answer from the contract and every copy (an agent then stops writing tiles() calls into home apps), or keep them as documented no-ops for home apps already written.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from docs/contract.md:6036:6038 to docs/contract.md:6103:6105: originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai); the code it describes moved when this branch changed the file.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6103},"end":{"line":6105},"content_hash":"46b35fff334dd252970d856668c02df6b944c56dfaf93197b0af33993246a08f"},"summary":"The home capability's contract still describes the launch image: shown() resolving {opened: id} and tiles(list) kept with an image shown on the next cold start, which the Clax app no longer does","supersedes":"f52d324a41c93afa9fbb2470bfec64a2049a088505d29026d72419e2aeedb778","tags":["status:needs-decision","home","session:claude-code:5ba3638a-dc4b-4c8f-8349-140e70a28c92"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-24T16:31:26.389527Z","id":"36e57290534b50ed2d8f0ec1d3d683631a1ed6990ea93cb4bdaed0be4c06912b","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Duplicate of 15ca54c0 (docs/contract.md:6103:6105): the same record re-anchored on two lines of history (this branch's rebases and main), differing only in the span it cites.","supersedes":"042c276e8c8d28b18c46bb7654354a6c2912b1de664beeb80a104eabb1da27e5","tags":["reason:duplicate","session:claude-code:5ba3638a-dc4b-4c8f-8349-140e70a28c92"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T18:17:34.252808Z","id":"0934210125200cf3ef90b09589e5333fad980d2add6b671bc6e8925638a4acfa","body":{"detail":"Cloudflare's API token permissions reference names no Artifacts permission, and wrangler's lookups are not documented per permission. The first deploy-prod.yml run with a token made from this table confirms it; amend the table with what that run needed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":76},"end":{"line":107},"content_hash":"cdb8038d6424e3f63ba94c5872a9b6d1bc79d2cdcbb78322ab4af89192950610"},"summary":"The Cloudflare token's permissions are partly unconfirmed: Artifacts, queue event subscriptions, Zone Read, Account Settings Read, User Details and Memberships Read, DNS Edit","tags":["untested","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T18:41:56.977326Z","id":"3a524e37b199a14c4d42e637b052b59910ce29e743322f0bee3b601f320fb6c2","body":{"detail":"scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh reads PRODUCTION only from setup.env or --production (scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh:116-130), and deploy.mjs gates only a deploy given --production (crates/clax-worker/deploy-lib.mjs productionGate). A setup.env written before this flag existed has no PRODUCTION line, so with such a setup.env for clax.dev `just clax-dev-deploy --i-know-what-i-am-doing` still deploys clax.dev from a laptop at any commit. The design's goal (\"just clax-dev-deploy refuses to deploy the production instance from a laptop unless given an explicit override\") holds only after that step, which docs/prod.md's \"One-time GitHub setup\" and this section do not list.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":163},"end":{"line":171},"content_hash":"e74da03bb0b2c21f500c9b1b12b7eaa72d1198830a38f12a7324201b9e1642c0"},"suggested_fix":"Add a one-time step to docs/prod.md: `just clax-dev-deploy --production` (a dry run, which writes PRODUCTION=1 to setup.env), and say that until then laptop deploys are not refused.","summary":"Nothing in the one-time setup marks the owner's laptop setup.env as production, so a laptop deploy of clax.dev is not refused until the owner passes --production once","tags":["review","review:prod-releases","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T19:57:31.908424Z","id":"3b8a4e936560111982031994a06f770e58f7e199456435445c8377653e8d0c29","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"3a524e37b199a14c4d42e637b052b59910ce29e743322f0bee3b601f320fb6c2","summary":"Confirmed: the justfile's clax-dev-deploy recipe runs `./scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh --legal bundled \"$@\"` with no --production; cloudflare-setup.sh sets PRODUCTION only from setup.env or --production (scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh:116-130) and passes --production to deploy.mjs only then (:177), and productionGate (crates/clax-worker/deploy-lib.mjs:58-62) lets a deploy without it through. A setup.env with no PRODUCTION line deploys clax.dev from a laptop at any commit.","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T20:08:22.603076Z","id":"fcbd8cb9b3dffbd136689d45f6d451d46ffe64b7de27f309cc4a033b8c1ef9ee","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:46e56dca7","summary":"Fixed: the justfile's clax-dev-deploy recipe runs ./scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh --legal bundled --production \"$@\", so every laptop deploy of clax.dev is gated by productionGate whatever setup.env holds (and its first run writes PRODUCTION=1 there); docs/prod.md ('Deploying from a laptop') and crates/clax-worker/README.md say so. Evidence: bash scripts/test-justfile.sh exits 0; before the recipe change its new check failed with 'just clax-dev-deploy does not pass --legal bundled --production; got: --legal bundled --smoke'.","supersedes":"3a524e37b199a14c4d42e637b052b59910ce29e743322f0bee3b601f320fb6c2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T17:44:36.356731Z","id":"693c2da4eb0376e28e6f2790a5a6d9c75916c9b8641ca8b94c1d39f11fc076bb","body":{"detail":"docs/feedback.md has outside products copy web/sdk/src service.ts, client.ts, sign.ts and types.ts into their backends, save <instance>/_clax/overlay.d.ts beside their code, and optionally depend on clax-core for clax_core::sig. Per LICENSE, web/ is AGPL-3.0-only and crates/clax-core is GPL-2.0-only OR AGPL-3.0-only, so a third-party backend that follows the guide takes on copyleft obligations; Empathic's own products are unaffected (same copyright holder). The guide now says so (section 'Licences of the code you copy') and points at the protocol-only path (docs/contract.md 'Signed requests' and 'Report queue routes'). The option: license the backend-facing SDK files, web/overlay/overlay.d.ts and web/overlay/guide/ under 0BSD or MIT, as apps/clax_app/assets/kit already is 0BSD, and update LICENSE and web/package.json to match.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":91},"end":{"line":103},"content_hash":"d819fafbc5c925ac79761f40f1a885e26a757a2ac174df38869692ab5e259ef7"},"summary":"Relicense web/sdk reporter files, overlay.d.ts and the signing code permissively (as the starter kit is 0BSD) so outside products may copy them?","tags":["status:needs-decision","licence","feedback","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800903Z","id":"3b2623d8acce4053159d7e3166c7ed15a2d13a5539c1c5c7dac5e980d4e43b0a","body":{"detail":"Removing an entry only stops the instance (fleet.rs reload). Its home stays under <data_dir>/instances/<name> on the shard's volume, and on PostgreSQL every clax_fleet_* row stays keyed by the name (pg.rs SCHEMA_1). An operator who later adds a different customer under a freed name (catalog names are short and human: 'acme', 'demo') starts that customer on the previous tenant's database: their people, sessions, artifacts and sealed secrets. Neither this section nor the catalog code says so.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":79},"end":{"line":84},"content_hash":"9d59ab6ed10ad1aae23153a63198937aa1dd799fc4791d9e086649155e1ae1bc"},"suggested_fix":"Say here that removing an instance keeps its data and that a name must never be reused for another tenant; better, give each catalog entry an immutable ID (or record the public origin with the data) and refuse to start an instance whose stored origin differs from its entry's.","summary":"An instance removed from the catalog keeps its data, and a new instance given the same name inherits it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800928Z","id":"0e3e2c3a9fcdbc9ad02c13c35fe2f8d760b3d858cf574eab382893fbede3bf37","body":{"detail":"Hosted::client (crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs, client) believes CF-Connecting-IP on any request once behind_cloudflare is set. A cluster's Ingress or load balancer is normally reachable directly by its IP, so a client that skips Cloudflare sends a fresh CF-Connecting-IP per request and escapes the per-address limits on setup secrets, sign-in codes and guest names, and the per-client stream caps.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":176},"end":{"line":179},"content_hash":"ae587fddee83af1e44bbf67f333efa6bc8934c2c557d94862170a3f9b9af469d"},"suggested_fix":"Add here that with behind_cloudflare the Ingress must accept only Cloudflare (its published IP ranges in the controller's allow list, or Authenticated Origin Pulls), and say how to configure it for the example ingress-nginx controller.","summary":"The advice to set behind_cloudflare omits that CF-Connecting-IP is then trusted from every peer","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214979Z","id":"708f6b4dca223f7349f0f81bd2ea5eef621520b4da78267e32ae7ac011e5b86f","body":{"detail":"/status lists only the instances running on the pod asked, and port 7481 is in no Service (deploy/k8s/base/service.yaml, sqlite/peers.yaml), so on PostgreSQL an operator must `kubectl port-forward` to each pod in turn, or query clax_fleet_leases by hand, a table docs/kubernetes.md never describes. Finding the pod whose logs hold an instance's error, or confirming a handover, needs this daily.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":181},"end":{"line":186},"content_hash":"169bbb2902ca535a0d5f01bf94d166f0e3f86f2e60fa6482c803423ac708fe8e"},"suggested_fix":"Add `clax-fleet status` (reading clax_fleet_members and clax_fleet_leases with CLAX_POSTGRES_URL, or asking each shard's peer on SQLite) that prints instance, pod, epoch and lease expiry, runnable through `kubectl exec deploy/clax -- clax-fleet status`; or have /status include the leases it can read. Document it in Operating it.","summary":"There is no way to see which pod runs which instance across the fleet short of port-forwarding to every pod","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.215Z","id":"c314337323b1c745bcf529c8822fb6832c6afb8af81e0a23adc1c2e26dff3c7c","body":{"detail":"The SQLite deployment (docs/kubernetes.md:87-111) says nothing about backing up the per-shard volumes, whose homes are live WAL databases: a volume snapshot is only crash-consistent, and copying files out with kubectl exec while instances run can capture a torn database. For PostgreSQL, `pg_dump -t 'clax_fleet_*'` also dumps clax_fleet_members and clax_fleet_leases; restoring those into a running fleet, or restoring at all while pods hold leases, puts stale holders and lower epochs back under live fences (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs Fence), and the docs give no order (scale to zero, restore, scale up).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":162},"end":{"line":165},"content_hash":"294022b117e7df0e63dce5a370dde4d48d5cde102aa680b6a2b333052cbd0e1f"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Backups and restore section for both backends: for SQLite, how to take a consistent copy (stop the instance via the catalog, or an online backup command on the binary, e.g. `clax-fleet backup <instance>` using SQLite's backup API) and how to restore a home; for PostgreSQL, scale the Deployment to 0 before a restore and exclude or truncate the members and leases tables.","summary":"Backups are described only for PostgreSQL and restore not at all","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.295092Z","id":"44336b63d4a61ea14715649ff6b88f721364c0e640d4dd7090ce6d7dd3de9e8b","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:80-85 says a removed instance 'stops' and nothing more. The fleet's DELETEs (crates/clax-fleet/src/content.rs:143,290,321,364; pages.rs:146,168; coord.rs:143) remove single files, versions or members, never an instance's rows, and crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs open (~433-439) reuses data_dir/instances/<name> for whatever entry carries that name; no remove_dir exists in crates/clax-fleet/src.","kind":"comment","references":"3b2623d8acce4053159d7e3166c7ed15a2d13a5539c1c5c7dac5e980d4e43b0a","summary":"confirmed: removal keeps an instance's data keyed by name and the docs do not say so","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.295401Z","id":"441fe2934fb0694cd1144c0bf53d9723f861c190c0b9cbdef5317df876bb85e8","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:176-179 says only to set behind_cloudflare = true. crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:496-514 (Hosted::client) parses cf-connecting-ip from any request when behind_cloudflare is set, with no check of the peer address.","kind":"comment","references":"0e3e2c3a9fcdbc9ad02c13c35fe2f8d760b3d858cf574eab382893fbede3bf37","summary":"confirmed: the behind_cloudflare advice omits restricting the Ingress to Cloudflare","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.160043Z","id":"8e1fb26fa26495b883f99350a7ae03bf99446cc01027a6d48ca792e29a80ffa2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:730-739: /status lists f.running(), this pod's only. deploy/k8s/base/service.yaml exposes port 80 and deploy/k8s/sqlite/peers.yaml port 7480; neither exposes 7481. grep -n 'clax_fleet_leases' docs/kubernetes.md finds nothing (only clax_fleet_members, line 135). main.rs Cmd (main.rs:48-59) has only serve and check-catalog.","kind":"comment","references":"708f6b4dca223f7349f0f81bd2ea5eef621520b4da78267e32ae7ac011e5b86f","summary":"confirmed: no fleet-wide view of instance placement; port 7481 is in no Service","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.160678Z","id":"24ad3bd6760d1e60be4c22bcc72ead04b6b2533680234a94aa59a19aafef13f7","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:162-165 ('Backups') covers only pg_dump -t 'clax_fleet_*'; the SQLite section (docs/kubernetes.md:87-111) says nothing about backing up the volumes, and grep -n -i restore docs/kubernetes.md finds no restore procedure. The dump pattern includes clax_fleet_members and clax_fleet_leases (docs/kubernetes.md:134-140 names the tables).","kind":"comment","references":"c314337323b1c745bcf529c8822fb6832c6afb8af81e0a23adc1c2e26dff3c7c","summary":"confirmed: backups are documented only for PostgreSQL, and restore not at all","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:22.426187Z","id":"bdd08a6dd765000ab1faf6dffb7dcf40dbe6f25aca834d42644a3140591bf511","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::open records the origin an instance's data was made for (fleet-origin in a SQLite home, clax_fleet_instances on PostgreSQL via pg::claim_origin) and refuses a name whose origin changed; test fleet::tests::a_home_keeps_its_key_and_its_origin; docs/kubernetes.md, The catalog, says never to reuse a name.","supersedes":"3b2623d8acce4053159d7e3166c7ed15a2d13a5539c1c5c7dac5e980d4e43b0a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:22.506294Z","id":"51c7d4c130b676d1ebcfe43d8369394f8ea6c46685bada264572cedf9bb01987","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md has a Backups and restore section for both backends: SQLite via pause and copy, PostgreSQL scaled to 0 with members and leases emptied.","supersedes":"c314337323b1c745bcf529c8822fb6832c6afb8af81e0a23adc1c2e26dff3c7c","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:22.588266Z","id":"fabba87294c99d3887b033ee14a07ad4431b42c90c4414d6e08700cb54a6904e","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Security, says the Ingress must accept only Cloudflare when behind_cloudflare is set, with the IP ranges URL and ingress-nginx's whitelist-source-range annotation.","supersedes":"0e3e2c3a9fcdbc9ad02c13c35fe2f8d760b3d858cf574eab382893fbede3bf37","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:22.668938Z","id":"8888e4c6a6eca66025cc74174d41e1c9da06fc6ef47e6d6b69b3577c31894229","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"`clax-fleet status --backend postgres` lists every lease, its pod, address, epoch and expiry; `--backend sqlite` asks each shard's /status with the peer key; docs/kubernetes.md, Operating it.","supersedes":"708f6b4dca223f7349f0f81bd2ea5eef621520b4da78267e32ae7ac011e5b86f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772728Z","id":"167de0e40a5590c505c73c21cbcbf25bb8e3ce777d520f48e10174c08147ee03","body":{"detail":"main.rs:173-177 defaults CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL to 8 and main.rs:27-29 adds a coordinator pool of 2, so a pod holds 10. hpa.yaml:14 allows 10 pods and deployment.yaml:11-13 surges one more during a rollout: 110, plus one for each `clax-fleet status`. PostgreSQL's default max_connections is 100 with superuser_reserved_connections 3 (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html), so a non-superuser role gets 97, and the pods past it fail their pool connect at start and crash-loop. Record 16a32d00 (closed) raised this with a pool of 16; halving the pool still leaves the shipped defaults over a default server, and the text at line 205 reads as if 100 fits.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":202},"end":{"line":207},"content_hash":"ba7f5528c5086f3225299f72177ca71eec3c6dc9418eeb7774a07ab4ec26f579"},"suggested_fix":"Make the shipped defaults fit a default server (maxReplicas 8, or pool 6), or state plainly in docs/kubernetes.md and the postgres overlay's comments that the defaults need max_connections of at least 115 and how to set it on common managed databases.","summary":"The docs say 10 pods take 100 connections, but the autoscaler's 10 pods plus a rollout's surge take 110, over a default PostgreSQL's 97 usable connections","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:14.037074Z","id":"673da339c428099bfb22a7b82a1a0f7bf4ad89bb423bf1684a619e5a5fa66e02","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:173-177 CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL default_value_t = 8; main.rs:27-29 COORD_POOL = 2. deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml:14 maxReplicas 10; deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:9-13 RollingUpdate maxSurge 1. 11 x 10 = 110. PostgreSQL defaults max_connections=100, superuser_reserved_connections=3 (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html). docs/kubernetes.md:202-205 says to keep pods x (pool + 2) plus surge under max_connections and then \"10 pods take 100\", without saying the shipped defaults exceed a default server; only postgres-dev raises max_connections to 200 (deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/postgres.yaml:56).","kind":"comment","references":"167de0e40a5590c505c73c21cbcbf25bb8e3ce777d520f48e10174c08147ee03","summary":"confirmed: defaults give 10 connections per pod; maxReplicas 10 plus maxSurge 1 is 110, over a default PostgreSQL's 97 non-superuser slots","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:42.475325Z","id":"6834942bc584f729846defc9ae3806fbcc756be90f3addca8bcc20d1bbd87594","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"maxReplicas is 8, so 9 pods x 10 connections fits a default server's 97; hpa.yaml and docs Connections give the formula (maxReplicas + 1) x (pool + 2). Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"167de0e40a5590c505c73c21cbcbf25bb8e3ce777d520f48e10174c08147ee03","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T18:31:29.544277Z","id":"cf083589ed7b03eca332e2da8d9e1652b7f651b3a3a0bae859041b325fa63bad","body":{"detail":"Cloudflare's API token permissions reference names no Artifacts permission, and wrangler's lookups are not documented per permission. The first deploy-prod.yml run with a token made from this table confirms it; amend the table with what that run needed.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":85},"end":{"line":116},"content_hash":"cdb8038d6424e3f63ba94c5872a9b6d1bc79d2cdcbb78322ab4af89192950610"},"summary":"The Cloudflare token's permissions are partly unconfirmed: Artifacts, queue event subscriptions, Zone Read, Account Settings Read, User Details and Memberships Read, DNS Edit","supersedes":"0934210125200cf3ef90b09589e5333fad980d2add6b671bc6e8925638a4acfa","tags":["untested","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T18:31:32.509301Z","id":"5364d8a1d1d85df0b8c8a9873a7b8d929b17eeeee034f4e12241a7c44ada793a","body":{"detail":"scripts/perf-app-launch.sh --android now reads each launch's timings from the app's log, because a release APK is not debuggable and 'adb run-as dev.empathic.clax cat files/timings.jsonl' fails on it (the earlier script read the file that way, so it could not measure such a build either). The baseline step 5 measures the build installed on the phone, which comes from before the app ran on the embedded core and so from before the log lines: every launch would count as missing. Options: (a) take the phone baseline from a debuggable build of the baseline commit and give the script back a run-as path for it; (b) backport the clax-timing: log lines onto the baseline commit in a scratch build; (c) take no phone baseline and compare the phone against its first measured in-process build.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1195},"end":{"line":1210},"content_hash":"299e52f36dd259f1a71b2741714ed1699c91f3b52ea945df12b491bd319dc4ef"},"summary":"The phone's launch baseline (step 5) cannot be taken on a release build from before 7c58e3dd28c6: it logs no clax-timing: lines and run-as cannot read its timings.jsonl","supersedes":"b91ee745b29b0fcd46371e1d23b740f56eb9a70c8ec8cea62c775f896c64a99e","tags":["perf","android","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/verification.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T18:31:32.607901Z","id":"e84c9864075ce4b7e17b47472dd44bd9d753a2ccfb152ef177d6ff1d370f3a72","body":{"detail":"§10.1 drives a real `claude -p` with this branch's plugin against a scratch daemon and a scripted Messages API that calls the publish tool, then replies as a LAN viewer with curl and validates the export with Ajv. Unlike scripts/smoke-claude-ask.sh, its fake model (a variant of scripts/fake-anthropic.py whose first turn calls `mcp__plugin_clax_clax__publish`) and its driver were not committed, so the check is described, not rerunnable as is. A scripts/smoke-claude-toolpath.sh, reusing fake-anthropic.py with a publish scenario, would make it repeatable.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1087},"end":{"line":1102},"content_hash":"2a2bc6f18be95108c6873fc23dd7f10d97f1cebac973297f961d3f8979d1de8b"},"summary":"The Toolpath manual check (§10.1) has no script in the repository","supersedes":"7081ac09c683ef93c85d3b251d305fb9141ee4180cbe604b224348fe9b1ef900","tags":["verification","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T18:31:54.062101Z","id":"35b91c4fc57aa7cb5bb99a6361eee97bff12c32ed17fc2c08325321809ec2062","body":{"detail":"JobsOwners::claim (crates/clax-http/src/jobs_owner.rs) spawns the drain task before answering, so when the old write ends at once the event is published before the HTTP answer is written, and the stream may deliver it first. A worker that follows the contract literally (on owner_draining, wait for held true, then claim) waits forever. The app's queue handles it (apps/clax_app/test/queue/update_test.dart, 'a drain's end heard before the claim's answer claims again'), but the contract and the route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs:182-197) state only the order that follows.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3143},"end":{"line":3153},"content_hash":"1ed79589ec9ea593cb40c67a9b41715bc5a99934bdb268290822df6833545275"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the contract: a held true heard for the client after the claim was sent counts, so on owner_draining claim again at once if one was heard.","summary":"The contract does not say that the drain's jobs_owner held true can arrive on the stream before the claim's 409 owner_draining","supersedes":"dfbad1dc2ecbe2eb3ff3aa5e6846a78a9c3fb8d7f21794972932ffbac6971be7","tags":["session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T18:31:54.164674Z","id":"56b32cc9c3cbeded98670868257c0b40a13650a4c798a0565cd12d29c198bbaf","body":{"detail":"Since the Clax app dropped its launch snapshot, HomeHost.call (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_host.dart, case 'shown' and case 'tiles') resolves shown() with null and takes tiles() without doing anything, and the shipped home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) calls neither tiles() nor reads opened. The contract text here, its copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (runtime capabilities list), web/contract/clax-extensions.d.ts, the app's prompt copies (apps/clax_app/assets/prompt, kept by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh) and web/shell/src/caps/home.ts still offer both. Options: remove tiles() and the {opened} answer from the contract and every copy (an agent then stops writing tiles() calls into home apps), or keep them as documented no-ops for home apps already written.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from docs/contract.md:6036:6038 to docs/contract.md:6103:6105: originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai); the code it describes moved when this branch changed the file.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:5ba3638a-dc4b-4c8f-8349-140e70a28c92)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6963},"end":{"line":6965},"content_hash":"0a55d8e7f11a6020ae8eeefaa8b14c0f9b4a9262bf64dbce34dd54c2d6266e9a"},"summary":"The home capability's contract still describes the launch image: shown() resolving {opened: id} and tiles(list) kept with an image shown on the next cold start, which the Clax app no longer does","supersedes":"15ca54c0075e7a1539e0ff38d9038faf6db77500be199ca7ee88a281a946e4ea","tags":["status:needs-decision","home","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-30T08:56:21.597104Z","id":"cbc559b7a18186567b3f842cbb804b2b03bd3208c1f4d2a38a93260469f979b2","body":{"detail":"scripts/perf-app-launch.sh --android now reads each launch's timings from the app's log, because a release APK is not debuggable and 'adb run-as dev.empathic.clax cat files/timings.jsonl' fails on it (the earlier script read the file that way, so it could not measure such a build either). The baseline step 5 measures the build installed on the phone, which comes from before the app ran on the embedded core and so from before the log lines: every launch would count as missing. Options: (a) take the phone baseline from a debuggable build of the baseline commit and give the script back a run-as path for it; (b) backport the clax-timing: log lines onto the baseline commit in a scratch build; (c) take no phone baseline and compare the phone against its first measured in-process build.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1260},"end":{"line":1275},"content_hash":"299e52f36dd259f1a71b2741714ed1699c91f3b52ea945df12b491bd319dc4ef"},"summary":"The phone's launch baseline (step 5) cannot be taken on a release build from before 7c58e3dd28c6: it logs no clax-timing: lines and run-as cannot read its timings.jsonl","supersedes":"b91ee745b29b0fcd46371e1d23b740f56eb9a70c8ec8cea62c775f896c64a99e","tags":["perf","android","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae","session:claude-code:988152cb-8f78-4b12-acdd-a455490bed05"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-30T09:51:33.693174Z","id":"f0feee6971afa6df533fa2029b48b83315f2ab3c5bebe590086a17a07fc657fa","body":{"detail":"The owner cookie is SameSite=Lax; Path=/ (crates/clax-http/src/local_trust.rs:291), so the browser sends it on any request to that host name on another port, subresources included, not only navigations. clax logout revokes a captured cookie, but the next visit to that port captures the new one, so logout is a revocation, not a remedy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":2639},"end":{"line":2647},"content_hash":"f3db9805c08448329bcab507b006efa451753fb6c362435b121cdba1489f8265"},"summary":"The port-residual paragraph understates the owner cookie's reach and overstates logout","tags":["review","session:claude-code:988152cb-8f78-4b12-acdd-a455490bed05"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-30T23:31:03.322432Z","id":"dfa66a44128dfbe35146dd40038a503aa4534205e624d1bd09449d883ffe05af","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md \"The owner and other viewers\": the owner browser session bullet now names the proof (localStorage, X-Clax-Owner-Proof, ?owner_proof= on the realtime routes alone) and what the cookie alone gets (a bare page load); the paragraph this record pinned is now \"Why two secrets\", which states the cookie's reach as SameSite=Lax on Path=/ to every port, navigations and subresources alike, why the replay gets nothing (the proof is the origin's, port included), and clax logout as a revocation. \"Browser login\" describes GET /login's page and the streams ended at logout. The claims are tested by api_local_trust::an_owner_cookie_replayed_without_its_proof_is_no_credential and logging_out_ends_the_streams_and_sockets_opened_with_a_session (bazelisk test //crates/clax-server/... passes).","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5ecbdf9bc","summary":"Rewritten for the cookie-and-proof design","supersedes":"f0feee6971afa6df533fa2029b48b83315f2ab3c5bebe590086a17a07fc657fa","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:988152cb-8f78-4b12-acdd-a455490bed05"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T03:31:22.745407Z","id":"5eb90d6133621e6b9a647d6e941a913b7f5741148708fcca8c2b6b98121ea6dc","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"dfa66a44128dfbe35146dd40038a503aa4534205e624d1bd09449d883ffe05af","summary":"Commit 5ecbdf9bc cited by this close was re-signed as 784091c47 (same tree, author and message).","tags":["session:claude-code:988152cb-8f78-4b12-acdd-a455490bed05"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T03:21:51.601672Z","id":"4e6ebe54ec113b238060268a8a4abdc11c04d4403bb37f2b513815c2c16e3b7b","body":{"detail":"JobsOwners::claim (crates/clax-http/src/jobs_owner.rs) spawns the drain task before answering, so when the old write ends at once the event is published before the HTTP answer is written, and the stream may deliver it first. A worker that follows the contract literally (on owner_draining, wait for held true, then claim) waits forever. The app's queue handles it (apps/clax_app/test/queue/update_test.dart, 'a drain's end heard before the claim's answer claims again'), but the contract and the route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs:182-197) state only the order that follows.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae)'s record.\n\nRe-anchored after merge c20bac570 moved the lines; first recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3792},"end":{"line":3802},"content_hash":"1ed79589ec9ea593cb40c67a9b41715bc5a99934bdb268290822df6833545275"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the contract: a held true heard for the client after the claim was sent counts, so on owner_draining claim again at once if one was heard.","summary":"The contract does not say that the drain's jobs_owner held true can arrive on the stream before the claim's 409 owner_draining","supersedes":"35b91c4fc57aa7cb5bb99a6361eee97bff12c32ed17fc2c08325321809ec2062","tags":["session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-01T22:26:13.629477Z","id":"ab6f37ba54bbaaac9a680f48a12c004a02a6d276b061cdce4b96642f6ed997d6","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md:8820-8821 (\"a run is refused (model_not_priced) on a model missing any of them until an admin prices it\") contradicts the new Pricing section (contract.md:9025ff: lines, then catalog, then default rates; refused only with require_explicit_price). The admin audit table row at contract.md:8937 lists POST …/catalog/refresh and …/tokenulator with view.admin/admin.cost_line, but the catalog refresh records nothing (routes/guards.rs unaudited entry for admin::refresh_catalog). DELETE /api/admin/pricing/models without ?model= answers 400 invalid_query (api_claxbot.rs model_rates_are_set_and_deleted_by_hand), undocumented.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":8819},"end":{"line":8822},"content_hash":"f71ce5923cf7453042a3fe7b78ab2a7b590a9de0b5e3517a836a1e7cdc1501e5"},"suggested_fix":"Reword 8820-8821 to point at the Pricing section's resolution; mark the catalog refresh unaudited in the table row; add the 400 invalid_query to the DELETE text.","summary":"Contract still says a model missing a token line is refused model_not_priced, and the audit table implies the catalog refreshes are recorded","tags":["review","severity:low","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-01T22:52:26.473931Z","id":"81c002cfe7224857f4e75b238432d4fbddeccdf20c5ad0088295bc0d74703da8","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"ab6f37ba54bbaaac9a680f48a12c004a02a6d276b061cdce4b96642f6ed997d6","summary":"Fixed in 933c774cf: the credit paragraph says a model without lines is priced from the catalog or the default rates and only require_explicit_price refuses a run on the instance key (model_not_priced; never on an own key); the audit table gives POST …/catalog/refresh its own row recording nothing; the Pricing section names DELETE's 400 invalid_query and says a refresh is not audited.","tags":["session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-01T23:33:58.825922Z","id":"0ea1b204d3f3df3fd7c71d45ee2c9c501b055497dbaa35352f60b9c757e94580","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Fixed: docs/contract.md:8821-8826 points at the Pricing resolution; the audit table row for POST /api/admin/pricing/catalog/refresh records nothing; the DELETE text names 400 invalid_query. bazel test //crates/clax-server/... //crates/clax-store/... //crates/clax-worker/... in clax-pricing-e at 94d258851: 'Executed 13 out of 22 tests: 22 tests pass.'","supersedes":"ab6f37ba54bbaaac9a680f48a12c004a02a6d276b061cdce4b96642f6ed997d6","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:f97e2315-5ffc-47c7-94a0-050e4e7b9835"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945071Z","id":"ba14aa5cf3834d62ed10a366251fef84b1166ed66f97ece8858f4af8c5bdd395","body":{"detail":"Failure: an overlay exactly as docs/kubernetes.md:63-82 shows (resources: deploy/k8s/sqlite; images: [{name: clax-fleet, newName: <registry>/clax-fleet, newTag: <tag>}]) renders `image: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0`, not the operator's image. Reproduced locally with kustomize v5.8.1 (`kubectl kustomize` of such an overlay with a relative path to deploy/k8s/sqlite and newName registry.example/clax-fleet, newTag mytag): the StatefulSet's image stays ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0. The inner overlay (deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml:24-27) has already rewritten `clax-fleet` to `ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet`, and kustomize's image transformer matches on the current name, so the outer `name: clax-fleet` matches nothing and no error is raised. Changing the outer entry to `name: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet` renders `registry.example/clax-fleet:mytag` as intended. The operator gets the upstream image (or an ImagePullBackOff while 0.6.0 is unpublished) and never learns why their build is not running. The kind recipe at docs/kubernetes.md:84-91 sidesteps this only by editing the repository's own deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml (and needs the standalone `kustomize` binary), which the paragraph above it says to avoid.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":63},"end":{"line":91},"content_hash":"390f20ac8bbad135788d40b64846d5c8d91a50ff10e4f8ec1bb467c47029b3d8"},"suggested_fix":"In docs/kubernetes.md:71-74 write `- name: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet` (the name the sqlite overlay produces), and say why; rewrite the kind recipe as the same overlay of one's own with newName clax-fleet, newTag dev, instead of `kustomize edit` inside deploy/k8s/sqlite. Optionally add a test that renders such an outer overlay and asserts the image changed.","summary":"The documented overlay of one's own sets `images: name: clax-fleet`, which no longer matches after deploy/k8s/sqlite renamed the image, so the operator's image is silently ignored","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945135Z","id":"6d2ec3fdaebdb1edc73c9a7b178d7391f0ec61001510802361d06d72ef122642","body":{"detail":"`git ls-tree -r --name-only v0.6.0 -- deploy` is empty: the v0.6.0 tag has no deploy/ directory, so the overlay of one's own at docs/kubernetes.md:70 (`github.com/empathic/clax//deploy/k8s/sqlite?ref=v0.6.0`) fails to build. The page itself says (lines 43-45) the first published image is the first release after 0.6.0, yet line 146 runs `docker run ... ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0 check-catalog`, and the Deploying steps (lines 168-173) apply deploy/k8s/sqlite, whose newTag is \"0.6.0\" (deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml:27), giving ImagePullBackOff on every shard. scripts/bump-version.sh will correct the strings at the next release, but until then every copy-paste command on the page fails, and the Deploying section does not repeat the 'build it first' caveat.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":63},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"63bf48ee1394109041a79824a7941563b83e1a395c4ad2d7537e245726eee0c0"},"suggested_fix":"Until the first release with deploy/k8s lands, have the Deploying section start with building and naming one's own image (via the overlay of one's own), use `?ref=<commit>` or `?ref=main` in the remote-base example with a note, and use the locally built tag in the check-catalog example; or have bump-version.sh write these only once a published version exists.","summary":"Every version the docs name today (the remote base at ?ref=v0.6.0, the check-catalog image and the overlay's default image ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0) points at something that does not exist","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945165Z","id":"02aa0b850b74cf4be02bebfef8e13f2cbf5b18296326b1cc61ca568f5f22860d","body":{"detail":"Failure: an operator sets `paused = true`, runs `kubectl apply -k`, then at once `kubectl exec clax-N -- tar ... instances/<name>` as docs/kubernetes.md:285-286 says. The pod sees the new catalog only after the kubelet refreshes the mounted ConfigMap (the page says up to a minute, line 139-140) and its next 5 s reload, so the instance is still running and writing; tar reads the main database, its -wal and other files at different moments, which is neither consistent nor crash-consistent and can restore as a corrupt or torn home. The Moving procedure (lines 193-195) does say to wait for the pod to log `instance stopped` (logged at crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:594), but the backup and restore paragraph only points to it for the tar command, not for the wait. Restore (`pause it, copy the home back`) has the same gap, writing under a store that may still be open.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":282},"end":{"line":287},"content_hash":"ac1fa946102625339902b390701f40955c4456f18d4a2245ecb20ce6f24a04bc"},"suggested_fix":"In Backups and restore, spell out the steps: set paused, apply, wait until `kubectl logs clax-N` shows `instance stopped` for that instance (or `/status` on 7482 no longer lists it as running), then copy; the same wait before a restore. Better still, give a command that waits (e.g. polling `clax-fleet status` for the instance).","summary":"The backup procedure says to pause the instance and copy its home, without waiting for the instance to stop, so a copy taken right after applying the pause is of a live database","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945190Z","id":"20bcfad3fc5a8f2064d46d83d6aa4b2905899c07fc5b56e8691cf13d5bd2ce52","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:286-287 restores by 'copy the home back', and line 197 moves with `tar -C /var/lib/clax/instances -xf -` on the target. tar -x overwrites the files in the archive but keeps every file in the destination the archive lacks: SQLite -wal/-shm files left by a store that overran its 10 s stop (line 203-205), files created after the backup was taken, and pre-migration copies. A leftover -wal next to a restored main database is replayed onto it by SQLite and can corrupt it (https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html, on journal and WAL files separated from or mismatched with their database). The move also leaves the full home on the old shard; if the instance's `shard` is ever set back (or a typo points at it), the old pod starts the stale data without complaint, because the home exists and `create` is not needed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":189},"end":{"line":198},"content_hash":"fabb3a7a2e726ef1cda1065a1b04fbd7f898e3ef7105687408d5ec41a0236701"},"suggested_fix":"Restore into an empty directory: move the current home aside (`mv instances/<name> instances/<name>.before-restore`) and then extract; after a move is verified, say to remove or rename the home on the old shard. Show both as commands.","summary":"Restoring or moving a home with `tar -xf` merges into whatever is already there, and moving leaves the old copy behind on the source shard","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945215Z","id":"4d62d65dd93663b8742bdd6a2d424c94979512e2a5197d22f0754e0636e7f082","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:168-173 creates clax-secrets and runs `kubectl apply -k deploy/k8s/sqlite`, which deploys deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml: an instance named `example` with `create = true` at https://clax.example.com. Following it as written makes a home bound to that origin under the name `example` (the fleet then refuses to reuse the name for another origin, lines 149-153). Nothing in the section says to label the Ingress controller's namespace `clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true`; on a CNI that enforces NetworkPolicy (Calico, Cilium, and kind's kindnet since v0.24) every request through the controller then times out, and the only mention is in Security (lines 226-236). deploy/k8s/examples/ingress.yaml is not in any kustomization and is not mentioned here either. An operator gets running pods that serve nothing reachable, with no failing step to point at the cause.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":166},"end":{"line":182},"content_hash":"c6afa0bcff6f7916c481fe456e46284e3e7f983933b202824d1a75285106bb52"},"suggested_fix":"Make Deploying an ordered checklist: write my-clax/ with fleet.toml and the image (the overlay of one's own from The image), create clax-secrets with one CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_<NAME> per instance, `kubectl label namespace <ingress-ns> clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true`, apply my-clax, apply an edited Ingress, and a verification step (`kubectl exec clax-0 -- clax-fleet status`, `curl https://<origin>/healthz`).","summary":"The Deploying section applies the repository's example catalog and leaves out the steps a working deployment needs: one's own overlay, the Ingress, and the namespace label the NetworkPolicy requires","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945241Z","id":"d875af44ddd99470f717281f0ed0bb48b87b2b8f47938428331c4cc838591a9a","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:7 has `replicas: 2` and base/fleet.toml:14 `shards = 2`. The overlay of one's own (docs/kubernetes.md:63-82) replaces the catalog but has no `replicas:` entry, so a catalog with `shards = 3` and an instance on shard 2 deploys two pods: clax-2 never exists and that instance's requests fail at the forward (clax-2.clax-peers does not resolve), while check-catalog passes because it does not know the replica count. The scaling steps use `kubectl scale sts clax --replicas=N`, which leaves the manifests saying 2; re-creating the namespace, a disaster-recovery apply, or a GitOps controller that reconciles the StatefulSet (Argo CD, Flux) puts replicas back to 2 and takes the new shards' instances down. Scaling in is not documented at all: what to do with instances on the removed shards, that their PVCs are retained (no persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy is set, so the default Retain applies, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#persistentvolumeclaim-retention), and that `shards` must be lowered only after those instances moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":184},"end":{"line":187},"content_hash":"019e7132fe3ec9111a00f904f9a7772120141001a1c8dfdc635c6734f70b0b66"},"suggested_fix":"Add `replicas: [{name: clax, count: <shards>}]` to the overlay-of-one's-own example and have the scaling steps edit it there instead of `kubectl scale`; add a Scaling in paragraph (move instances off, lower shards, lower replicas, then decide on the retained PVC). Consider a startup log or /status field when the catalog names a shard >= the number of pods the peers Service resolves.","summary":"The catalog's `shards` and the StatefulSet's replicas are two numbers kept in step by hand, and neither the overlay of one's own nor the scaling steps keep them in one place","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945314Z","id":"7fc9cad5e91c6faa095b5770628e7ee9055feddaf7e7862850a78276c50303d5","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:549-567 (`Hosted::client`) uses CF-Connecting-IP only with behind_cloudflare, else the TCP peer; there is no trusted-proxy or X-Forwarded-For option. Behind ingress-nginx (examples/ingress.yaml) the peer is the controller pod, and for forwarded requests the forwarding fleet pod (docs/kubernetes.md:370-372). crates/clax-http/src/socket_caps.rs:34 caps `MAX_PER_ADDRESS` at 128 event streams and sockets per address, and sign-in attempts are rate-limited per address (`Hosted::attempt`). So the topology the manifests assume (base/service.yaml:1-6, networkpolicy.yaml) caps a tenant at 128 open browser connections across all its users and lets one user's failed sign-ins lock out everyone, with 429 too_many_streams / rate_limited. Line 256-257 states the fact ('the controller's address for every client') but not these consequences, and gives no remedy short of Cloudflare.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":249},"end":{"line":257},"content_hash":"fb7ef159968dccc4f9d25da87c94916fad799dd0d91e0dbcb47e419b180d149c"},"suggested_fix":"State the consequences in Security and Limits (shared 128-connection cap and shared sign-in limits per instance when not behind Cloudflare), and record the need for a trusted-proxy setting (e.g. `[hosted] trusted_proxies` CIDRs with X-Forwarded-For) so an in-cluster Ingress can pass the client address; until then recommend Cloudflare in front for any multi-user tenant.","summary":"Behind the documented in-cluster Ingress without Cloudflare, every client of an instance shares one address, so one instance's whole user base gets one sign-in budget and 128 long-lived connections in total","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945373Z","id":"b606c30da3550dff74487255c0eb3fcd346ab3c7f2920cd0505f8c9eac470962","body":{"detail":"envFrom (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:77-79) exposes all of clax-secrets to every instance. crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:1395-1401 defaults `github_client_secret_env` to GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET, and config.rs:1055-1063 defaults `[bot] api_key_env` to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, which 'takes precedence over a stored instance key'. An operator who puts ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in clax-secrets for one tenant gives it to every tenant whose config leaves the default, overriding the keys those tenants stored and billing their use to that key; GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET likewise, while each tenant's GitHub OAuth app needs its own (its callback is the tenant's origin). docs/kubernetes.md:155-161 explains the uniqueness rule for setup secrets only and does not name `github_client_secret_env` or `[bot] api_key_env` at all.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":155},"end":{"line":164},"content_hash":"643f440bdafd308c1dccfe6522be4a2386c3124c238af9edeffcd8968a6cf114"},"suggested_fix":"List every `*_env` setting an instance's config can name, with its default, and recommend per-instance names (CLAX_GITHUB_SECRET_<NAME>, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_<NAME>) in the example catalog; consider having check-catalog refuse two instances that leave github_client_secret_env or bot api_key_env at the same name, as it does for setup_secret_env.","summary":"Every key in clax-secrets reaches every instance, and the per-instance variable rule covers only the setup secret: GitHub's client secret and ClaxBot's API key default to one shared name","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945395Z","id":"49df856cfabdfa05c57da317352c4c7b33b11e70cb5b18c80f44e552d7154bdc","body":{"detail":"The StatefulSet's RollingUpdate (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:10-11) waits only for /readyz, and instances start on their first request (docs/kubernetes.md:363-365), so a release whose migration fails becomes Ready on every shard and the rollout continues through the whole fleet; failures show only as 503s and `failing` in /status. Rolling back the image does not help after a migration succeeded: crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:824-825 refuses a schema newer than the binary (`SchemaNewer`), so every migrated instance stays down on the old image. docs/kubernetes.md:279-280 says the pre-migration copy is kept in each home but not where, under what name, or how to put it back, and the page has no Rollback heading.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":276},"end":{"line":280},"content_hash":"7fba24b11e05d03667b5246d569ae9e69419c0aa394b5b1d0c390df5e34b6a8b"},"suggested_fix":"Document a canary upgrade with `updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition` (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#partitions): upgrade the highest shard, request each of its instances, check /status `failing`, then lower the partition. Add a Rollback section: per instance, pause, swap the database for its pre-migration copy (name the backup directory), deploy the old image, unpause; and say that writes since the upgrade are lost.","summary":"An upgrade is not gated on instances starting, and there is no rollback procedure, though an older image refuses a database a newer one migrated","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945418Z","id":"5c25b2662691c659659727ed24ce2155c7159e37950dfafd14f1404a4af28a0d","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:121-128 requests 10Gi through volumeClaimTemplates. Kubernetes rejects updates to a StatefulSet spec other than replicas, template, updateStrategy, persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy, ordinals and minReadySeconds ('spec: Forbidden: updates to statefulset spec for fields other than ...'), so an operator who follows 'raise them' (docs/kubernetes.md:274) in an overlay gets an apply error. Growing a shard's volume means patching each PVC data-clax-N directly (only when its StorageClass has allowVolumeExpansion, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes-claims) and, to keep the template in step, deleting the StatefulSet with `--cascade=orphan` and re-applying. A full volume takes down every instance on that shard. No storageClassName is set either, so the volume class (and whether it can expand) is whatever the cluster's default is.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":268},"end":{"line":274},"content_hash":"ee58f416412caca8893f309ec9b1510a9749f02e750f1ec465bc666aa0fe7244"},"suggested_fix":"Replace 'raise them' for the volume with the PVC-patch procedure and the orphan-delete step, and say to choose a StorageClass with allowVolumeExpansion before the first apply (show storageClassName in the overlay of one's own); mention a disk-usage alert on the data volume.","summary":"The docs say to raise the volume size with a shard's instances, but a StatefulSet's volumeClaimTemplates cannot be changed, so editing the size and applying fails","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945495Z","id":"33eca180cddfaefd1b677d2acb2cb5046af17ddef6eecde7110225b70d09ccea","body":{"detail":"examples/ingress.yaml uses ingressClassName nginx and nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations, and docs/kubernetes.md:254 points to nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range. The Kubernetes project announced ingress-nginx's retirement, with best-effort maintenance ending March 2026 and no further releases or security fixes after (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/). New clusters will mostly use a Gateway API implementation, for which the docs give no route, timeout or body-limit equivalent, and an operator translating the annotations has to find each by hand.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":259},"end":{"line":266},"content_hash":"b3d226aba6871e1261e11519325898e47f3bf6afc2a25a37abad84f1994b40d2"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Gateway API example (Gateway listeners for the origin and the wildcard site domain, an HTTPRoute to the clax Service, and timeouts via HTTPRoute `timeouts` or the implementation's policy), keep the ingress-nginx one as legacy, and state each setting generically (read timeout above 3660 s, body limit above 16 MB, WebSocket upgrade, source allow list).","summary":"The only Ingress example, and the Cloudflare allow-list advice, are for ingress-nginx, which Kubernetes retired in March 2026","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:37:52.945517Z","id":"e45700fe0c45c1ec050c80c7d81013005a163688d465ef498c21393270b4b9e6","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 dismisses a volume snapshot as 'only crash-consistent' and offers pause-and-copy per instance, which means downtime per tenant for every backup and no scheduled option. SQLite in WAL mode is designed to recover from exactly the state a power loss leaves (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, 'Durability'; https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html lists non-atomic copies, not atomic snapshots, as the risk), and every file of a shard's homes lives on one PVC, so one CSI VolumeSnapshot captures them at one instant (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/). If some part of a home is not safe to restore from a crash-consistent image, the docs should say which; if all is, operators can back up without downtime.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":282},"end":{"line":287},"content_hash":"ac1fa946102625339902b390701f40955c4456f18d4a2245ecb20ce6f24a04bc"},"suggested_fix":"State whether a crash-consistent snapshot of data-clax-N is a supported restore source; if so, show a VolumeSnapshot of each PVC and a restore (new PVC from the snapshot, scale the shard down, swap), and keep pause-and-copy for single-instance restores. Mention SQLite's online backup (`VACUUM INTO`) if the binary could offer it as a command.","summary":"The only documented backup takes each instance offline, though an atomic snapshot of a shard's volume is a valid SQLite recovery point","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.702478Z","id":"1c5f36d17511f617541ab9887bf689a42fef1371fd7648b4dfb0188ab8b27413","body":{"detail":"Rendered with kubectl v1.37.1 / kustomize v5.8.1 an overlay of one's own (resources: a relative path to deploy/k8s/sqlite; images: [{name: clax-fleet, newName: registry.example/clax-fleet, newTag: mytag}]): `kubectl kustomize <overlay> | grep image:` prints `image: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0`. The same overlay with `name: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet` prints `image: registry.example/clax-fleet:mytag`. deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml:24-27 rewrites clax-fleet to ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0 first; docs/kubernetes.md:71-74 uses `name: clax-fleet`. A concrete, silent wrong image for anyone following the page.","kind":"comment","references":"ba14aa5cf3834d62ed10a366251fef84b1166ed66f97ece8858f4af8c5bdd395","summary":"confirmed: the outer overlay's `images: name: clax-fleet` matches nothing after deploy/k8s/sqlite renamed the image, so the operator's image is ignored","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.702887Z","id":"fd325afa34ba9b98c7c0a7178a9e7bee67dc98dcc8d566f2b78814ce81d2be05","body":{"detail":"`git ls-tree -r --name-only v0.6.0 -- deploy` prints nothing (tag v0.6.0 = b49ce615514b22c1d846cadaad1e64ea11518096; its top level has no deploy/). docs/kubernetes.md:70 uses `github.com/empathic/clax//deploy/k8s/sqlite?ref=v0.6.0`, line 146 runs `ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0 check-catalog`, and deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml:27 sets newTag \"0.6.0\", while docs/kubernetes.md:43-45 says the first published image is the first release after 0.6.0. The Deploying section (lines 166-173) does not repeat the build-first caveat.","kind":"comment","references":"6d2ec3fdaebdb1edc73c9a7b178d7391f0ec61001510802361d06d72ef122642","summary":"confirmed: the v0.6.0 tag has no deploy/ tree and the page's image and remote-base references name 0.6.0","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.703240Z","id":"b9d6fa5a2a61b7a756e6e9c08c1b8cc8380df35cce63ad47bf3b9b10dc20c153","body":{"detail":"The gap is real: docs/kubernetes.md:284-287 says pause, copy, unpause, with no wait, and lines 138-141 say a mounted ConfigMap takes up to a minute to update, so a copy taken at once can be of a running store. But line 286 points to 'as in Moving an instance', whose step 1 (lines 193-195) is 'Set paused = true on the instance and wait for its pod to log instance stopped' (logged at crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:594). The failure needs an operator to read that cross-reference as covering only the tar command, and writes during the window; it is a documentation-clarity risk, not a defect in shipped behaviour. Kind should be concern.","kind":"comment","references":"02aa0b850b74cf4be02bebfef8e13f2cbf5b18296326b1cc61ca568f5f22860d","summary":"downgraded: concern — the backup paragraph omits the wait for `instance stopped`, but it defers to the Moving procedure that states it","tags":["verified:downgraded","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.703591Z","id":"b6460e951d42a5b9c997b412a61eeb4b1fbd3e6fbb29c1397ce02f698cbbabdb","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:197 extracts with `tar -C /var/lib/clax/instances -xf -`, and 286-287 restores by 'copy the home back'; tar -x keeps destination files absent from the archive (e.g. a -wal/-shm left beside the database). Steps 1-3 at lines 193-198 end with setting the new shard and unpausing; nothing says to remove or rename the home left on the old shard, and lines 190-191 say only that a missing home keeps an instance down, so a home that still exists on the old shard would start if `shard` were set back. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"20bcfad3fc5a8f2064d46d83d6aa4b2905899c07fc5b56e8691cf13d5bd2ce52","summary":"confirmed: restore and move extract with tar into an existing directory, and the move never removes the source copy","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.703939Z","id":"3ddf34e1cc85b3630a4f01276dbc2ae8f9edefa7a6394fe9bb6b0edcd4fd3acd","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:168-173 creates clax-secrets with CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_EXAMPLE and runs `kubectl apply -k deploy/k8s/sqlite`, which ships deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml:16-28 (instance `example`, create = true, https://clax.example.com). The ingress namespace label appears only in Security (lines 226-228), the Ingress example only in its own section (line 261); deploy/k8s/base/kustomization.yaml:11-15 does not include examples/ingress.yaml. No verification step follows the apply.","kind":"comment","references":"4d62d65dd93663b8742bdd6a2d424c94979512e2a5197d22f0754e0636e7f082","summary":"confirmed: Deploying applies the repository's example catalog and omits the overlay of one's own, the Ingress and the namespace label","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.704315Z","id":"274e039aeaa73d631e6e877ab39cbaa89116ed93ae0fa918af2553a6cb5a2c19","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:7 `replicas: 2`; deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml:14 `shards = 2`. The overlay at docs/kubernetes.md:63-82 has no `replicas:` entry. Scaling out (lines 184-187, and deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml:9-12) uses `kubectl scale sts clax --replicas=N`, which leaves the manifests at 2, so a re-apply resets it. The page has no scaling-in procedure, and the StatefulSet sets no persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy (statefulset.yaml:1-128).","kind":"comment","references":"d875af44ddd99470f717281f0ed0bb48b87b2b8f47938428331c4cc838591a9a","summary":"confirmed: `shards` and the StatefulSet's replicas are kept in step by hand, the overlay example sets no replicas, and scaling in is undocumented","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.705503Z","id":"d649396a507c99fbc1ab300e6f0088d6df9676c78bd2da657a42e6abd6192009","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:549-567 (`Hosted::client`) reads CF-Connecting-IP only when behind_cloudflare, else the TCP peer; there is no other proxy-header option. hosted.rs:598-606 (`attempt`) rate-limits by that address, and crates/clax-http/src/socket_caps.rs:34 sets MAX_PER_ADDRESS = 128. docs/kubernetes.md:255-257 and 370-372 state the shared peer address but not these consequences.","kind":"comment","references":"7fc9cad5e91c6faa095b5770628e7ee9055feddaf7e7862850a78276c50303d5","summary":"confirmed: without Cloudflare every client behind the Ingress counts as one address, sharing the per-address stream cap and sign-in limits","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.706240Z","id":"02098f5df6fc4000e515cf2b9391ccf18a5f3ae0729c0c0c356b57bd356d8b4d","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:77-79 exposes all of clax-secrets to the one process. crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:744 and 1408 default github_client_secret_env to GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET; config.rs:1061 and 1094 default `[bot] api_key_env` to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, read with std::env::var at crates/clax-server/src/core.rs:431 and preferred over a stored instance key (crates/clax-bot/src/run.rs:201-204). crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:227-235 checks only setup_secret_env for sharing. docs/kubernetes.md:155-164 names neither setting.","kind":"comment","references":"b606c30da3550dff74487255c0eb3fcd346ab3c7f2920cd0505f8c9eac470962","summary":"confirmed: the bot API key and the GitHub client secret default to one shared variable name, and every key in clax-secrets reaches every instance","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.706589Z","id":"71fa7df8723ca847fdca6d2850ec973ab17c3958946fa0b3ca371ba326466b76","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:10-11 RollingUpdate with no partition; the readiness probe is /readyz (lines 91-95). docs/kubernetes.md:363-365 says an instance starts (and migrates) on its first request. crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:825 and 897 refuse a schema newer than the binary (SchemaNewer), so an old image cannot open a migrated home. docs/kubernetes.md:276-280 says a pre-migration copy is kept in each home without naming it, and the page has no rollback section.","kind":"comment","references":"49df856cfabdfa05c57da317352c4c7b33b11e70cb5b18c80f44e552d7154bdc","summary":"confirmed: the rolling upgrade is gated only on /readyz while instances migrate lazily, and no rollback procedure is documented","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.706936Z","id":"04c9f6abe22454760106c92f31179b84dc53a88440e4164fc902707ff109407d","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:121-128 requests 10Gi through volumeClaimTemplates and sets no storageClassName. docs/kubernetes.md:273-274 says 'its volume is 10 Gi; raise them'. The StatefulSet API allows updates only to replicas, template, updateStrategy, persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy, ordinals and minReadySeconds (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/), so changing the template's storage in an overlay fails at apply; growing a volume means patching each PVC (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes-claims).","kind":"comment","references":"5c25b2662691c659659727ed24ce2155c7159e37950dfafd14f1404a4af28a0d","summary":"confirmed: 'raise them' for the volume cannot be done by editing volumeClaimTemplates, which a StatefulSet update rejects","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.707986Z","id":"55dd67ea07b05b82eb0ceee6a987e415829a4435ebe8642ad0de4d14528091ed","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/examples/ingress.yaml:11-16 uses nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations and ingressClassName nginx; docs/kubernetes.md:254 names nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range and 264-266 'the example's are ingress-nginx's'. The retirement is announced at https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/. No Gateway API example exists under deploy/k8s. Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"33eca180cddfaefd1b677d2acb2cb5046af17ddef6eecde7110225b70d09ccea","summary":"confirmed: the only Ingress example and the allow-list advice are ingress-nginx's, which the Kubernetes project retired","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:09.708334Z","id":"3e23981c31be19f885f12abee4189eabc14e1be89c7d7302346440704becd491","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 'A volume snapshot of a live home is only crash-consistent', and 284-287 offer only pause, copy, unpause. Every home of a shard lives on its one PVC (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:107-109, 121-128). SQLite documents WAL-mode durability across power loss (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html), so whether a snapshot is a supported restore source is a question the page leaves open. Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"e45700fe0c45c1ec050c80c7d81013005a163688d465ef498c21393270b4b9e6","summary":"confirmed: the docs dismiss an atomic volume snapshot and document only per-instance pause-and-copy","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:42:51.163703Z","id":"0b0e231bf57719134c9bf5bd2a89d9228e5283eef073a6f83257adab925eb58e","body":{"detail":"Failure: an operator sets `paused = true`, runs `kubectl apply -k`, then at once `kubectl exec clax-N -- tar ... instances/<name>` as docs/kubernetes.md:285-286 says. The pod sees the new catalog only after the kubelet refreshes the mounted ConfigMap (the page says up to a minute, line 139-140) and its next 5 s reload, so the instance is still running and writing; tar reads the main database, its -wal and other files at different moments, which is neither consistent nor crash-consistent and can restore as a corrupt or torn home. The Moving procedure (lines 193-195) does say to wait for the pod to log `instance stopped` (logged at crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:594), but the backup and restore paragraph only points to it for the tar command, not for the wait. Restore (`pause it, copy the home back`) has the same gap, writing under a store that may still be open.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":282},"end":{"line":287},"content_hash":"ac1fa946102625339902b390701f40955c4456f18d4a2245ecb20ce6f24a04bc"},"suggested_fix":"In Backups and restore, spell out the steps: set paused, apply, wait until `kubectl logs clax-N` shows `instance stopped` for that instance (or `/status` on 7482 no longer lists it as running), then copy; the same wait before a restore. Better still, give a command that waits (e.g. polling `clax-fleet status` for the instance).","summary":"The backup procedure says to pause the instance and copy its home, without waiting for the instance to stop, so a copy taken right after applying the pause is of a live database","supersedes":"02aa0b850b74cf4be02bebfef8e13f2cbf5b18296326b1cc61ca568f5f22860d","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:23.115992Z","id":"4a7152192ce1cbd01d4d7f6651986cd1cfabceb0fe6f6a8018b32020c8496487","body":{"detail":"envFrom (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:77-79) exposes all of clax-secrets to every instance. crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:1395-1401 defaults `github_client_secret_env` to GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET, and config.rs:1055-1063 defaults `[bot] api_key_env` to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, which 'takes precedence over a stored instance key'. An operator who puts ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in clax-secrets for one tenant gives it to every tenant whose config leaves the default, overriding the keys those tenants stored and billing their use to that key; GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET likewise, while each tenant's GitHub OAuth app needs its own (its callback is the tenant's origin). docs/kubernetes.md:155-161 explains the uniqueness rule for setup secrets only and does not name `github_client_secret_env` or `[bot] api_key_env` at all.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":164},"end":{"line":173},"content_hash":"643f440bdafd308c1dccfe6522be4a2386c3124c238af9edeffcd8968a6cf114"},"suggested_fix":"List every `*_env` setting an instance's config can name, with its default, and recommend per-instance names (CLAX_GITHUB_SECRET_<NAME>, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_<NAME>) in the example catalog; consider having check-catalog refuse two instances that leave github_client_secret_env or bot api_key_env at the same name, as it does for setup_secret_env.","summary":"Every key in clax-secrets reaches every instance, and the per-instance variable rule covers only the setup secret: GitHub's client secret and ClaxBot's API key default to one shared name","supersedes":"b606c30da3550dff74487255c0eb3fcd346ab3c7f2920cd0505f8c9eac470962","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:23.306479Z","id":"053d136b3bdeea54375d7e79cceeb5eb2e25a260f7deb5fd5f866450b118a0cb","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:7 has `replicas: 2` and base/fleet.toml:14 `shards = 2`. The overlay of one's own (docs/kubernetes.md:63-82) replaces the catalog but has no `replicas:` entry, so a catalog with `shards = 3` and an instance on shard 2 deploys two pods: clax-2 never exists and that instance's requests fail at the forward (clax-2.clax-peers does not resolve), while check-catalog passes because it does not know the replica count. The scaling steps use `kubectl scale sts clax --replicas=N`, which leaves the manifests saying 2; re-creating the namespace, a disaster-recovery apply, or a GitOps controller that reconciles the StatefulSet (Argo CD, Flux) puts replicas back to 2 and takes the new shards' instances down. Scaling in is not documented at all: what to do with instances on the removed shards, that their PVCs are retained (no persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy is set, so the default Retain applies, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#persistentvolumeclaim-retention), and that `shards` must be lowered only after those instances moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":217},"end":{"line":220},"content_hash":"019e7132fe3ec9111a00f904f9a7772120141001a1c8dfdc635c6734f70b0b66"},"suggested_fix":"Add `replicas: [{name: clax, count: <shards>}]` to the overlay-of-one's-own example and have the scaling steps edit it there instead of `kubectl scale`; add a Scaling in paragraph (move instances off, lower shards, lower replicas, then decide on the retained PVC). Consider a startup log or /status field when the catalog names a shard >= the number of pods the peers Service resolves.","summary":"The catalog's `shards` and the StatefulSet's replicas are two numbers kept in step by hand, and neither the overlay of one's own nor the scaling steps keep them in one place","supersedes":"d875af44ddd99470f717281f0ed0bb48b87b2b8f47938428331c4cc838591a9a","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:23.495116Z","id":"7ac97a5dc2fb42df17762340ec356262133a29be3b6c498b7cf57359d8239e48","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:286-287 restores by 'copy the home back', and line 197 moves with `tar -C /var/lib/clax/instances -xf -` on the target. tar -x overwrites the files in the archive but keeps every file in the destination the archive lacks: SQLite -wal/-shm files left by a store that overran its 10 s stop (line 203-205), files created after the backup was taken, and pre-migration copies. A leftover -wal next to a restored main database is replayed onto it by SQLite and can corrupt it (https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html, on journal and WAL files separated from or mismatched with their database). The move also leaves the full home on the old shard; if the instance's `shard` is ever set back (or a typo points at it), the old pod starts the stale data without complaint, because the home exists and `create` is not needed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":222},"end":{"line":231},"content_hash":"fabb3a7a2e726ef1cda1065a1b04fbd7f898e3ef7105687408d5ec41a0236701"},"suggested_fix":"Restore into an empty directory: move the current home aside (`mv instances/<name> instances/<name>.before-restore`) and then extract; after a move is verified, say to remove or rename the home on the old shard. Show both as commands.","summary":"Restoring or moving a home with `tar -xf` merges into whatever is already there, and moving leaves the old copy behind on the source shard","supersedes":"20bcfad3fc5a8f2064d46d83d6aa4b2905899c07fc5b56e8691cf13d5bd2ce52","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:23.695060Z","id":"8de7320a8855077096d5cd58563ecffd57b7fdd502409f21e51cbfec17410aba","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:549-567 (`Hosted::client`) uses CF-Connecting-IP only with behind_cloudflare, else the TCP peer; there is no trusted-proxy or X-Forwarded-For option. Behind ingress-nginx (examples/ingress.yaml) the peer is the controller pod, and for forwarded requests the forwarding fleet pod (docs/kubernetes.md:370-372). crates/clax-http/src/socket_caps.rs:34 caps `MAX_PER_ADDRESS` at 128 event streams and sockets per address, and sign-in attempts are rate-limited per address (`Hosted::attempt`). So the topology the manifests assume (base/service.yaml:1-6, networkpolicy.yaml) caps a tenant at 128 open browser connections across all its users and lets one user's failed sign-ins lock out everyone, with 429 too_many_streams / rate_limited. Line 256-257 states the fact ('the controller's address for every client') but not these consequences, and gives no remedy short of Cloudflare.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":282},"end":{"line":290},"content_hash":"fb7ef159968dccc4f9d25da87c94916fad799dd0d91e0dbcb47e419b180d149c"},"suggested_fix":"State the consequences in Security and Limits (shared 128-connection cap and shared sign-in limits per instance when not behind Cloudflare), and record the need for a trusted-proxy setting (e.g. `[hosted] trusted_proxies` CIDRs with X-Forwarded-For) so an in-cluster Ingress can pass the client address; until then recommend Cloudflare in front for any multi-user tenant.","summary":"Behind the documented in-cluster Ingress without Cloudflare, every client of an instance shares one address, so one instance's whole user base gets one sign-in budget and 128 long-lived connections in total","supersedes":"7fc9cad5e91c6faa095b5770628e7ee9055feddaf7e7862850a78276c50303d5","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:23.871154Z","id":"8a04ddcdb599055950b660976d6741bd1b22939c8ee1c4d20aa987efacf124b9","body":{"detail":"examples/ingress.yaml uses ingressClassName nginx and nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations, and docs/kubernetes.md:254 points to nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range. The Kubernetes project announced ingress-nginx's retirement, with best-effort maintenance ending March 2026 and no further releases or security fixes after (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/). New clusters will mostly use a Gateway API implementation, for which the docs give no route, timeout or body-limit equivalent, and an operator translating the annotations has to find each by hand.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":292},"end":{"line":299},"content_hash":"b3d226aba6871e1261e11519325898e47f3bf6afc2a25a37abad84f1994b40d2"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Gateway API example (Gateway listeners for the origin and the wildcard site domain, an HTTPRoute to the clax Service, and timeouts via HTTPRoute `timeouts` or the implementation's policy), keep the ingress-nginx one as legacy, and state each setting generically (read timeout above 3660 s, body limit above 16 MB, WebSocket upgrade, source allow list).","summary":"The only Ingress example, and the Cloudflare allow-list advice, are for ingress-nginx, which Kubernetes retired in March 2026","supersedes":"33eca180cddfaefd1b677d2acb2cb5046af17ddef6eecde7110225b70d09ccea","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.049168Z","id":"07c11d83a4b11cde9b97fe9d38e9bd39db316eb708b98b5742af617d750bf1f9","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:121-128 requests 10Gi through volumeClaimTemplates. Kubernetes rejects updates to a StatefulSet spec other than replicas, template, updateStrategy, persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy, ordinals and minReadySeconds ('spec: Forbidden: updates to statefulset spec for fields other than ...'), so an operator who follows 'raise them' (docs/kubernetes.md:274) in an overlay gets an apply error. Growing a shard's volume means patching each PVC data-clax-N directly (only when its StorageClass has allowVolumeExpansion, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes-claims) and, to keep the template in step, deleting the StatefulSet with `--cascade=orphan` and re-applying. A full volume takes down every instance on that shard. No storageClassName is set either, so the volume class (and whether it can expand) is whatever the cluster's default is.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":301},"end":{"line":307},"content_hash":"ee58f416412caca8893f309ec9b1510a9749f02e750f1ec465bc666aa0fe7244"},"suggested_fix":"Replace 'raise them' for the volume with the PVC-patch procedure and the orphan-delete step, and say to choose a StorageClass with allowVolumeExpansion before the first apply (show storageClassName in the overlay of one's own); mention a disk-usage alert on the data volume.","summary":"The docs say to raise the volume size with a shard's instances, but a StatefulSet's volumeClaimTemplates cannot be changed, so editing the size and applying fails","supersedes":"5c25b2662691c659659727ed24ce2155c7159e37950dfafd14f1404a4af28a0d","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.210593Z","id":"fa66ab1b240adb3adfb64f08b28251b5c5217fdd0d85fff075e0f11bf3897cc4","body":{"detail":"The StatefulSet's RollingUpdate (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:10-11) waits only for /readyz, and instances start on their first request (docs/kubernetes.md:363-365), so a release whose migration fails becomes Ready on every shard and the rollout continues through the whole fleet; failures show only as 503s and `failing` in /status. Rolling back the image does not help after a migration succeeded: crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:824-825 refuses a schema newer than the binary (`SchemaNewer`), so every migrated instance stays down on the old image. docs/kubernetes.md:279-280 says the pre-migration copy is kept in each home but not where, under what name, or how to put it back, and the page has no Rollback heading.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":309},"end":{"line":313},"content_hash":"7fba24b11e05d03667b5246d569ae9e69419c0aa394b5b1d0c390df5e34b6a8b"},"suggested_fix":"Document a canary upgrade with `updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition` (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#partitions): upgrade the highest shard, request each of its instances, check /status `failing`, then lower the partition. Add a Rollback section: per instance, pause, swap the database for its pre-migration copy (name the backup directory), deploy the old image, unpause; and say that writes since the upgrade are lost.","summary":"An upgrade is not gated on instances starting, and there is no rollback procedure, though an older image refuses a database a newer one migrated","supersedes":"49df856cfabdfa05c57da317352c4c7b33b11e70cb5b18c80f44e552d7154bdc","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.379142Z","id":"a94cdafe1ed895be7c4aac98318ff771cac5223b2a8ed51d616acd4635b3d762","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 dismisses a volume snapshot as 'only crash-consistent' and offers pause-and-copy per instance, which means downtime per tenant for every backup and no scheduled option. SQLite in WAL mode is designed to recover from exactly the state a power loss leaves (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, 'Durability'; https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html lists non-atomic copies, not atomic snapshots, as the risk), and every file of a shard's homes lives on one PVC, so one CSI VolumeSnapshot captures them at one instant (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/). If some part of a home is not safe to restore from a crash-consistent image, the docs should say which; if all is, operators can back up without downtime.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":315},"end":{"line":320},"content_hash":"ac1fa946102625339902b390701f40955c4456f18d4a2245ecb20ce6f24a04bc"},"suggested_fix":"State whether a crash-consistent snapshot of data-clax-N is a supported restore source; if so, show a VolumeSnapshot of each PVC and a restore (new PVC from the snapshot, scale the shard down, swap), and keep pause-and-copy for single-instance restores. Mention SQLite's online backup (`VACUUM INTO`) if the binary could offer it as a command.","summary":"The only documented backup takes each instance offline, though an atomic snapshot of a shard's volume is a valid SQLite recovery point","supersedes":"e45700fe0c45c1ec050c80c7d81013005a163688d465ef498c21393270b4b9e6","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.540545Z","id":"6579eed384525950f2023a1f23d3cf2a400ba1e73ee3954e1d4b5e0dfd53c949","body":{"detail":"Failure: an operator sets `paused = true`, runs `kubectl apply -k`, then at once `kubectl exec clax-N -- tar ... instances/<name>` as docs/kubernetes.md:285-286 says. The pod sees the new catalog only after the kubelet refreshes the mounted ConfigMap (the page says up to a minute, line 139-140) and its next 5 s reload, so the instance is still running and writing; tar reads the main database, its -wal and other files at different moments, which is neither consistent nor crash-consistent and can restore as a corrupt or torn home. The Moving procedure (lines 193-195) does say to wait for the pod to log `instance stopped` (logged at crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:594), but the backup and restore paragraph only points to it for the tar command, not for the wait. Restore (`pause it, copy the home back`) has the same gap, writing under a store that may still be open.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":315},"end":{"line":320},"content_hash":"ac1fa946102625339902b390701f40955c4456f18d4a2245ecb20ce6f24a04bc"},"suggested_fix":"In Backups and restore, spell out the steps: set paused, apply, wait until `kubectl logs clax-N` shows `instance stopped` for that instance (or `/status` on 7482 no longer lists it as running), then copy; the same wait before a restore. Better still, give a command that waits (e.g. polling `clax-fleet status` for the instance).","summary":"The backup procedure says to pause the instance and copy its home, without waiting for the instance to stop, so a copy taken right after applying the pause is of a live database","supersedes":"0b0e231bf57719134c9bf5bd2a89d9228e5283eef073a6f83257adab925eb58e","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:14:21.549057Z","id":"ea4f5a1028e91c6858c1aa32e253da082334261e7eb26ebc0e77406dea4ce495","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7cdb81e4b","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md's overlay of one's own now names `ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet`, the name deploy/k8s/sqlite gives the image. That overlay, written out with its base swapped for the local deploy/k8s/sqlite and newName registry.example/clax-fleet, newTag mytag, renders with `kubectl kustomize` (kustomize v5.8.1) as `image: registry.example/clax-fleet:mytag`, and with newName clax-fleet, newTag dev as `image: clax-fleet:dev`. The old `name: clax-fleet` still renders `image: ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:0.6.0`. New test tests::the_documented_overlay_renames_the_image_the_sqlite_overlay_names checks the guide's name against the overlay's newName; `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/...` all PASSED.","supersedes":"ba14aa5cf3834d62ed10a366251fef84b1166ed66f97ece8858f4af8c5bdd395","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:14:21.732428Z","id":"098ca1e761bae899906e9715bf58e89081224e95021374b4e54ffb6e5187f7f2","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7cdb81e4b","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, The image, now says that no release publishes the image or ships deploy/k8s yet (the first to do both is the first after 0.6.0) and to build and push one's own until then. The overlay's comment says to use `?ref=<commit>` of main until then, the check-catalog example says to use one's own build, and Deploying's first step is the image. The 0.6.0 strings stay: scripts/check-version.sh requires them and scripts/bump-version.sh rewrites them at the next release. `bazelisk test //scripts:version_bump //scripts:release` PASSED.","supersedes":"6d2ec3fdaebdb1edc73c9a7b178d7391f0ec61001510802361d06d72ef122642","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:14:21.873816Z","id":"8ffbafd18d79dffaff7885131d4a42f5d71d5659ec62d36ce9e13bfb66b73269","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7cdb81e4b","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md's Deploying is now an ordered checklist: the image; one's own overlay and catalog, checked with check-catalog, with a warning that applying deploy/k8s/sqlite itself binds the example instance to https://clax.example.com; the Secret with one setup secret per instance; `kubectl label namespace <controller's namespace> clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true`; apply and `kubectl rollout status sts/clax`; the Ingress from deploy/k8s/examples/ingress.yaml; and a check (`kubectl exec clax-0 -- clax-fleet status`, `curl https://<origin>/healthz`). The overlay also sets `replicas` for the StatefulSet; with count 3, `kubectl kustomize` renders `replicas: 3`.","supersedes":"4d62d65dd93663b8742bdd6a2d424c94979512e2a5197d22f0754e0636e7f082","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:40.751112Z","id":"ab37210875c37f20f8f07ff16b6e286401385b240e0acb8537af665810412a30","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:49f40ba5a","summary":"Catalog::parse (crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs) now refuses a variable read by two instances or for two secrets, for github_client_secret_env as for setup_secret_env, the default GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET included, and a fleet variable named as either. The new cases in tests::a_bad_catalog_is_refused_naming_what_is_wrong failed before the change (panicked at catalog.rs:518) and pass, and tests::each_instance_reads_its_github_secret_from_a_variable_of_its_own passes. The `[bot] api_key_env` part does not hold for fleet instances: a hosted core reads no [bot] and no such variable (crates/clax-server/src/core.rs:414-416, and `if s.hosted.is_some() { return bot; }` before the variable is read at core.rs:451), and nothing in crates/clax-server reads [sample]. docs/kubernetes.md, Instances' secrets (e4515f7e1), now lists both variables an instance reads with their defaults, and says a hosted instance reads no [bot] or [sample] key variable. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED, and `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/...` is clean.","supersedes":"4a7152192ce1cbd01d4d7f6651986cd1cfabceb0fe6f6a8018b32020c8496487","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.018632Z","id":"a9b46e817609b51940b40fede4339d95c8433c6be2e0e8ee5e04a349bca5e1dc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e4515f7e1","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Moving an instance, now copies into no existing home: a home of that name on the new shard is moved aside first with `mv … acme.before-move`, and the reason is given (tar -x keeps files the copy lacks, and a -wal beside a copied database is replayed onto it). Step 4 moves the old shard's copy aside (`acme.moved`) once the instance serves from the new shard. Restore moves the current home aside (`acme.before-restore`) before extracting. Names with a dot are no instance's (instance names are lowercase letters, digits and inner dashes, crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs valid_name), and the fleet opens a home only at instances/<name> (fleet.rs, Fleet::open). `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED, and `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/...` is clean.","supersedes":"7ac97a5dc2fb42df17762340ec356262133a29be3b6c498b7cf57359d8239e48","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.150168Z","id":"0a1018d204396220a05b5fe70ffec684e600ff7f0824ebb45c83799691277131","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e4515f7e1","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Backups and restore, is now steps: pause, apply, and wait until it no longer runs, as in Moving an instance; then copy; then unpause. Restore waits the same way. The wait is now until `kubectl exec clax-N -- clax-fleet status` no longer lists the instance among the shard's running ones (/status's `running`, crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs Fleet::status), rather than for the `instance stopped` log line, which Fleet::stop logs only for an instance that was running. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED, and `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/...` is clean.","supersedes":"6579eed384525950f2023a1f23d3cf2a400ba1e73ee3954e1d4b5e0dfd53c949","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.550158Z","id":"b649349907729ef25b7c8f25805cb18d244c09a8d9cede786311b1de76555365","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:7 has `replicas: 2` and base/fleet.toml:14 `shards = 2`. The overlay of one's own (docs/kubernetes.md:63-82) replaces the catalog but has no `replicas:` entry, so a catalog with `shards = 3` and an instance on shard 2 deploys two pods: clax-2 never exists and that instance's requests fail at the forward (clax-2.clax-peers does not resolve), while check-catalog passes because it does not know the replica count. The scaling steps use `kubectl scale sts clax --replicas=N`, which leaves the manifests saying 2; re-creating the namespace, a disaster-recovery apply, or a GitOps controller that reconciles the StatefulSet (Argo CD, Flux) puts replicas back to 2 and takes the new shards' instances down. Scaling in is not documented at all: what to do with instances on the removed shards, that their PVCs are retained (no persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy is set, so the default Retain applies, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#persistentvolumeclaim-retention), and that `shards` must be lowered only after those instances moved.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":229},"end":{"line":232},"content_hash":"019e7132fe3ec9111a00f904f9a7772120141001a1c8dfdc635c6734f70b0b66"},"suggested_fix":"Add `replicas: [{name: clax, count: <shards>}]` to the overlay-of-one's-own example and have the scaling steps edit it there instead of `kubectl scale`; add a Scaling in paragraph (move instances off, lower shards, lower replicas, then decide on the retained PVC). Consider a startup log or /status field when the catalog names a shard >= the number of pods the peers Service resolves.","summary":"The catalog's `shards` and the StatefulSet's replicas are two numbers kept in step by hand, and neither the overlay of one's own nor the scaling steps keep them in one place","supersedes":"053d136b3bdeea54375d7e79cceeb5eb2e25a260f7deb5fd5f866450b118a0cb","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.687038Z","id":"7b94f8b0ff83868bc5276379476174dcbe79141d6073b32af4dc3cfdc93774ce","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:549-567 (`Hosted::client`) uses CF-Connecting-IP only with behind_cloudflare, else the TCP peer; there is no trusted-proxy or X-Forwarded-For option. Behind ingress-nginx (examples/ingress.yaml) the peer is the controller pod, and for forwarded requests the forwarding fleet pod (docs/kubernetes.md:370-372). crates/clax-http/src/socket_caps.rs:34 caps `MAX_PER_ADDRESS` at 128 event streams and sockets per address, and sign-in attempts are rate-limited per address (`Hosted::attempt`). So the topology the manifests assume (base/service.yaml:1-6, networkpolicy.yaml) caps a tenant at 128 open browser connections across all its users and lets one user's failed sign-ins lock out everyone, with 429 too_many_streams / rate_limited. Line 256-257 states the fact ('the controller's address for every client') but not these consequences, and gives no remedy short of Cloudflare.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":312},"end":{"line":320},"content_hash":"fb7ef159968dccc4f9d25da87c94916fad799dd0d91e0dbcb47e419b180d149c"},"suggested_fix":"State the consequences in Security and Limits (shared 128-connection cap and shared sign-in limits per instance when not behind Cloudflare), and record the need for a trusted-proxy setting (e.g. `[hosted] trusted_proxies` CIDRs with X-Forwarded-For) so an in-cluster Ingress can pass the client address; until then recommend Cloudflare in front for any multi-user tenant.","summary":"Behind the documented in-cluster Ingress without Cloudflare, every client of an instance shares one address, so one instance's whole user base gets one sign-in budget and 128 long-lived connections in total","supersedes":"8de7320a8855077096d5cd58563ecffd57b7fdd502409f21e51cbfec17410aba","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.819324Z","id":"c555f3f67a4429d57c32ac5644fb9082ac42a2a468e3d9bf9249cf65b348445c","body":{"detail":"examples/ingress.yaml uses ingressClassName nginx and nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations, and docs/kubernetes.md:254 points to nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range. The Kubernetes project announced ingress-nginx's retirement, with best-effort maintenance ending March 2026 and no further releases or security fixes after (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/). New clusters will mostly use a Gateway API implementation, for which the docs give no route, timeout or body-limit equivalent, and an operator translating the annotations has to find each by hand.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":322},"end":{"line":329},"content_hash":"b3d226aba6871e1261e11519325898e47f3bf6afc2a25a37abad84f1994b40d2"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Gateway API example (Gateway listeners for the origin and the wildcard site domain, an HTTPRoute to the clax Service, and timeouts via HTTPRoute `timeouts` or the implementation's policy), keep the ingress-nginx one as legacy, and state each setting generically (read timeout above 3660 s, body limit above 16 MB, WebSocket upgrade, source allow list).","summary":"The only Ingress example, and the Cloudflare allow-list advice, are for ingress-nginx, which Kubernetes retired in March 2026","supersedes":"8a04ddcdb599055950b660976d6741bd1b22939c8ee1c4d20aa987efacf124b9","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:41.955515Z","id":"61b50641243890d4f415700550865512d5f25d2e787b2414ea6a83e43c3f1eb9","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:121-128 requests 10Gi through volumeClaimTemplates. Kubernetes rejects updates to a StatefulSet spec other than replicas, template, updateStrategy, persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy, ordinals and minReadySeconds ('spec: Forbidden: updates to statefulset spec for fields other than ...'), so an operator who follows 'raise them' (docs/kubernetes.md:274) in an overlay gets an apply error. Growing a shard's volume means patching each PVC data-clax-N directly (only when its StorageClass has allowVolumeExpansion, https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#expanding-persistent-volumes-claims) and, to keep the template in step, deleting the StatefulSet with `--cascade=orphan` and re-applying. A full volume takes down every instance on that shard. No storageClassName is set either, so the volume class (and whether it can expand) is whatever the cluster's default is.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":331},"end":{"line":337},"content_hash":"ee58f416412caca8893f309ec9b1510a9749f02e750f1ec465bc666aa0fe7244"},"suggested_fix":"Replace 'raise them' for the volume with the PVC-patch procedure and the orphan-delete step, and say to choose a StorageClass with allowVolumeExpansion before the first apply (show storageClassName in the overlay of one's own); mention a disk-usage alert on the data volume.","summary":"The docs say to raise the volume size with a shard's instances, but a StatefulSet's volumeClaimTemplates cannot be changed, so editing the size and applying fails","supersedes":"07c11d83a4b11cde9b97fe9d38e9bd39db316eb708b98b5742af617d750bf1f9","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.091239Z","id":"18f34c4e44356b5259a7d1243fd5419edb6cc00fde214d420ea8649804a028bb","body":{"detail":"The StatefulSet's RollingUpdate (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:10-11) waits only for /readyz, and instances start on their first request (docs/kubernetes.md:363-365), so a release whose migration fails becomes Ready on every shard and the rollout continues through the whole fleet; failures show only as 503s and `failing` in /status. Rolling back the image does not help after a migration succeeded: crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:824-825 refuses a schema newer than the binary (`SchemaNewer`), so every migrated instance stays down on the old image. docs/kubernetes.md:279-280 says the pre-migration copy is kept in each home but not where, under what name, or how to put it back, and the page has no Rollback heading.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":339},"end":{"line":343},"content_hash":"7fba24b11e05d03667b5246d569ae9e69419c0aa394b5b1d0c390df5e34b6a8b"},"suggested_fix":"Document a canary upgrade with `updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition` (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#partitions): upgrade the highest shard, request each of its instances, check /status `failing`, then lower the partition. Add a Rollback section: per instance, pause, swap the database for its pre-migration copy (name the backup directory), deploy the old image, unpause; and say that writes since the upgrade are lost.","summary":"An upgrade is not gated on instances starting, and there is no rollback procedure, though an older image refuses a database a newer one migrated","supersedes":"fa66ab1b240adb3adfb64f08b28251b5c5217fdd0d85fff075e0f11bf3897cc4","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.228565Z","id":"5fdede31def7a12781eebc740f218848f6a1e791e1869d564cc8989730def152","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 dismisses a volume snapshot as 'only crash-consistent' and offers pause-and-copy per instance, which means downtime per tenant for every backup and no scheduled option. SQLite in WAL mode is designed to recover from exactly the state a power loss leaves (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, 'Durability'; https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html lists non-atomic copies, not atomic snapshots, as the risk), and every file of a shard's homes lives on one PVC, so one CSI VolumeSnapshot captures them at one instant (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/). If some part of a home is not safe to restore from a crash-consistent image, the docs should say which; if all is, operators can back up without downtime.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":345},"end":{"line":347},"content_hash":"1332a3bf70865e7b2971ad3e65a70340fd4797cde12878729c734d0bd7df4067"},"suggested_fix":"State whether a crash-consistent snapshot of data-clax-N is a supported restore source; if so, show a VolumeSnapshot of each PVC and a restore (new PVC from the snapshot, scale the shard down, swap), and keep pause-and-copy for single-instance restores. Mention SQLite's online backup (`VACUUM INTO`) if the binary could offer it as a command.","summary":"The only documented backup takes each instance offline, though an atomic snapshot of a shard's volume is a valid SQLite recovery point","supersedes":"a94cdafe1ed895be7c4aac98318ff771cac5223b2a8ed51d616acd4635b3d762","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/environments.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T02:56:18.632707Z","id":"9ddf475728292d507fb350995046f8a164d9162ac5f6828f3f6322503b6e3087","body":{"detail":"GitHub OAuth Apps with wildcard matching on accept any subdomain of the callback URL's host (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps, Redirect URLs), so one preview App serves every https://pr-<N>.<PREVIEW_HOST_ZONE> with no code change (crates/clax-server/src/github.rs sends redirect_uri=<origin>/auth/github/callback). A GitHub App takes at most 10 exact callback URLs, which would need a relay or a pool of preview slots.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":90},"end":{"line":111},"content_hash":"c29055842b32fdb7dc74bd6cecf93005cfa2d5bd9e34c2e09f79164c8a2fa626"},"summary":"A fixed-host OAuth relay that forwards GitHub's code to each pr-<N> host, in place of one OAuth App with wildcard matching","tags":["environments","revisit:GitHub drops wildcard matching for OAuth Apps, or sign-in moves to a GitHub App","session:claude-code:1c02587f-4cef-4c2a-bb03-a7d5f5fa98b9"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/environments.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T02:56:18.632747Z","id":"28117e1cc3086e1d8beb94841819f108a8ae80296bfc85317555514b01386f4c","body":{"detail":"A Cloudflare token's Workers Scripts: Edit is account-wide, and preview.yml runs a same-repository pull request's own scripts with the preview environment's token, so on clax.dev's account that token can replace the Worker clax. docs/environments.md recommends a separate account for dev and previews. To decide: the account; dev's PUBLIC_HOST and SITE_DOMAIN (and SITE_ZONE if below a zone); PREVIEW_HOST_ZONE and PREVIEW_SITE_ZONE (two registrable domains, with Advanced Certificate Manager and Total TLS on the site zone).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":112},"end":{"line":141},"content_hash":"fe3517ced1d41566735583503546d6ceb4cce1fbb5935ddaab05a3e9b22f59b9"},"summary":"Which Cloudflare account dev and previews run on, and their domains, are not chosen: the workflows read them from the dev and preview environments' variables, which nothing has set","tags":["environments","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:1c02587f-4cef-4c2a-bb03-a7d5f5fa98b9"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/environments.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:17:54.047240Z","id":"74e91bb29898600d1de9ca73bcc33b7c17a742eb5046dceec22677cb0d08f7f3","body":{"detail":"To choose: dev's PUBLIC_HOST and SITE_DOMAIN (and SITE_ZONE if its site domain is below a zone), and PREVIEW_HOST_ZONE and PREVIEW_SITE_ZONE: two registrable domains, with Advanced Certificate Manager and Total TLS on the site zone. Their tokens are scoped to these zones, never clax.dev's or clax.run's.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":112},"end":{"line":146},"content_hash":"9e3e89898c1910be2c30d78256eb0b5b24d7d7079040f4dcd11e38acd1427c38"},"summary":"dev and the previews use clax.dev's Cloudflare account (decided; the waiver on .github/workflows/preview.yml); their domains are not chosen yet, so the dev and preview environments' variables are unset","supersedes":"28117e1cc3086e1d8beb94841819f108a8ae80296bfc85317555514b01386f4c","tags":["environments","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:1c02587f-4cef-4c2a-bb03-a7d5f5fa98b9"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/environments.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:22:52.493Z","id":"e712b54f7182c7af3dbff818608244095c3367bbdf90df6f0dc44cbd2c84840f","body":{"detail":"To make: the account, on Workers Paid with the Artifacts beta. To choose: dev's PUBLIC_HOST and SITE_DOMAIN (and SITE_ZONE if its site domain is below a zone), and PREVIEW_HOST_ZONE and PREVIEW_SITE_ZONE: two registrable domains on that account, with Advanced Certificate Manager and Total TLS on the site zone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":114},"end":{"line":153},"content_hash":"3abb51d6c62c67247c3304d05447aa448361b7a92919883945df6630c5a1c6a6"},"summary":"dev and the previews run on a Cloudflare account of their own (decided); the account, its ID and the domains are not made or chosen yet, so the dev and preview environments' variables are unset","supersedes":"74e91bb29898600d1de9ca73bcc33b7c17a742eb5046dceec22677cb0d08f7f3","tags":["environments","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:1c02587f-4cef-4c2a-bb03-a7d5f5fa98b9"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/environments.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:28:49.304859Z","id":"8bf9d2ef0e4a663a125163a63b4d40695998b5464471a4d1066bd4e037309a58","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"e712b54f7182c7af3dbff818608244095c3367bbdf90df6f0dc44cbd2c84840f","summary":"Decided: the account is clax-development, and its two zones are clax-preview.dev (PREVIEW_HOST_ZONE; previews at pr-<N>.clax-preview.dev, dev at dev.clax-preview.dev) and clax-preview.run (PREVIEW_SITE_ZONE and dev's SITE_ZONE; sites at <ID>.pr-<N>.clax-preview.run and <ID>.dev.clax-preview.run). Subdomains of clax.dev and clax.run were not taken: a subdomain as a zone of its own takes Cloudflare Enterprise, and browsers would hold previews same-site with clax.dev and clax.run.","tags":["status:decided","session:claude-code:1c02587f-4cef-4c2a-bb03-a7d5f5fa98b9"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:29.539127Z","id":"febe94377c74f802aa2bb60a74a8d2d2e2deea66adac74413bff864a978d0a63","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e6edfb48","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Scaling out, now raises `shards` and the overlay's `replicas` count together, and says not to use `kubectl scale`, which the next apply or a GitOps controller undoes. Scaling in is a new procedure: move instances off the shards being removed (the catalog refuses an instance past `shards`), lower both, then delete the retained claims. deploy/k8s/sqlite/kustomization.yaml's comment matches. A startup check against the number of pods the peers Service resolves was not added. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http:all //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED; `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http/...` is clean.","supersedes":"b649349907729ef25b7c8f25805cb18d244c09a8d9cede786311b1de76555365","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:29.669327Z","id":"b31aaed018fc5ea1a06faea5157c8201b113f61e646bf7b991093d4996109a81","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e6edfb48","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Sizing, now says to choose an expandable StorageClass and the size before the first apply, with a JSON 6902 patch to volumeClaimTemplates. `kubectl kustomize` of an overlay with that patch renders `storageClassName: expandable` and `storage: 20Gi`. It grows a shard's volume by patching its claim, and keeps the template in step with `kubectl delete sts clax --cascade=orphan` and an apply. It also names the kubelet volume metrics to alert on. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http:all //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED; `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http/...` is clean.","supersedes":"61b50641243890d4f415700550865512d5f25d2e787b2414ea6a83e43c3f1eb9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:29.799856Z","id":"2e43a3a1143f5a9dfc6bf29a55380da522a349407883bec8af6ce47b917c3a66","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e6edfb48","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Upgrading, now upgrades one shard first: `rollingUpdate.partition` set to the highest ordinal, the instances started and `clax-fleet status` checked for `failing`, then the partition set to 0. A new Rolling back section pauses each migrated instance, sets clax.db and its -wal/-shm aside, and copies the newest `backups/clax-<schema version>-<UTC time>.db` (crates/clax-store/src/backup.rs) into place before the old image is applied. It says that writes since the migration are lost. The command, run on a made-up home with copies clax-9-20261101…, clax-10-20261020… and clax-10-20261102…, puts clax-10-20261102… in place: the newest by time, not by name. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http:all //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED; `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http/...` is clean.","supersedes":"18f34c4e44356b5259a7d1243fd5419edb6cc00fde214d420ea8649804a028bb","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.058217Z","id":"cef0284653d81aa2c8d6b8e8d176f16b5b1e01768d8da1011c66079b3568e6f9","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0e6edfb48","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Security, Client addresses, now states the consequences without Cloudflare. One person's failed sign-ins (429 rate_limited) lock everyone out, and 128 event streams and WebSockets in all are open at once (429 too_many_streams; crates/clax-http/src/socket_caps.rs MAX_PER_ADDRESS). It recommends Cloudflare for an instance with more than a few users, and says hosted mode does not yet believe a forwarded-for header. The trusted-proxy setting is recorded as a question for the owner on crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs (Hosted::client). `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http:all //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED; `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http/...` is clean.","supersedes":"7b94f8b0ff83868bc5276379476174dcbe79141d6073b32af4dc3cfdc93774ce","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.189862Z","id":"43ed129155fc9808213ea07bee9a310d9fcd0693f9ea58d703650fd9fe8b419b","body":{"detail":"examples/ingress.yaml uses ingressClassName nginx and nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations, and docs/kubernetes.md:254 points to nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range. The Kubernetes project announced ingress-nginx's retirement, with best-effort maintenance ending March 2026 and no further releases or security fixes after (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/). New clusters will mostly use a Gateway API implementation, for which the docs give no route, timeout or body-limit equivalent, and an operator translating the annotations has to find each by hand.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":391},"end":{"line":398},"content_hash":"b3d226aba6871e1261e11519325898e47f3bf6afc2a25a37abad84f1994b40d2"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Gateway API example (Gateway listeners for the origin and the wildcard site domain, an HTTPRoute to the clax Service, and timeouts via HTTPRoute `timeouts` or the implementation's policy), keep the ingress-nginx one as legacy, and state each setting generically (read timeout above 3660 s, body limit above 16 MB, WebSocket upgrade, source allow list).","summary":"The only Ingress example, and the Cloudflare allow-list advice, are for ingress-nginx, which Kubernetes retired in March 2026","supersedes":"c555f3f67a4429d57c32ac5644fb9082ac42a2a468e3d9bf9249cf65b348445c","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.321513Z","id":"7c8127dcc0a28f504d7cea4c5c2532698a7064f70ba83ef4f5d8074fc971bb02","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 dismisses a volume snapshot as 'only crash-consistent' and offers pause-and-copy per instance, which means downtime per tenant for every backup and no scheduled option. SQLite in WAL mode is designed to recover from exactly the state a power loss leaves (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, 'Durability'; https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html lists non-atomic copies, not atomic snapshots, as the risk), and every file of a shard's homes lives on one PVC, so one CSI VolumeSnapshot captures them at one instant (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/). If some part of a home is not safe to restore from a crash-consistent image, the docs should say which; if all is, operators can back up without downtime.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":474},"end":{"line":476},"content_hash":"1332a3bf70865e7b2971ad3e65a70340fd4797cde12878729c734d0bd7df4067"},"suggested_fix":"State whether a crash-consistent snapshot of data-clax-N is a supported restore source; if so, show a VolumeSnapshot of each PVC and a restore (new PVC from the snapshot, scale the shard down, swap), and keep pause-and-copy for single-instance restores. Mention SQLite's online backup (`VACUUM INTO`) if the binary could offer it as a command.","summary":"The only documented backup takes each instance offline, though an atomic snapshot of a shard's volume is a valid SQLite recovery point","supersedes":"5fdede31def7a12781eebc740f218848f6a1e791e1869d564cc8989730def152","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:27.481503Z","id":"2d1f32370178c4d1b87eb44771e7b6168837ee161012302e7b3cb6a7fb309240","body":{"detail":"docs/feedback.md 'Licences of the code you copy' (lines 91-102) and the TypeScript section (line 109) and the dynamic-import section (line 377) say the files a backend copies are AGPL-3.0-only and that a product which does not accept those terms must reimplement the protocol. LICENSE (the 'LGPL-2.1-only' entry) now lists web/sdk/, web/overlay/ and web/bridge/ under LGPL-2.1-only 'so the apps that use them may be under any license', web/sdk/package.json says LGPL-2.1-only, and the npm package @empathic/clax-client-sdk ships an LGPL LICENSE (web/scripts/build-sdk.mjs licenseNotice()). A third party reading the guide is told the opposite of what the repository grants, and may avoid the SDK. Record 693c2da4 on these lines asks whether to relicense these files; the LGPL entry in LICENSE (commit d7812795c) appears to have answered it without the guide being updated.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":91},"end":{"line":110},"content_hash":"12125f9dbf312e2ea25b563d9c27dd8e81bf46929403a798e879376f5841165f"},"suggested_fix":"Rewrite 'Licences of the code you copy' to say web/sdk, web/overlay (overlay.d.ts included) and web/bridge are LGPL-2.1-only, leaving only clax-core's GPL-2.0-only OR AGPL-3.0-only note for the Rust path; drop the AGPL mentions at lines 109 and 377; and propose closing 693c2da4 to the owner with LICENSE as the evidence.","summary":"The guide says web/sdk/src and overlay.d.ts are AGPL-3.0-only, but LICENSE and the published package make them LGPL-2.1-only","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:27.481537Z","id":"a46458dc0437bb7c66669ed0a4247833a8fcf1bfb1ddb32f0399c2940cd09469","body":{"detail":"Section 2 tells a TypeScript backend to 'copy service.ts, client.ts, sign.ts and types.ts from web/sdk/src', and section 3 tells a page to save <instance>/_clax/overlay.d.ts beside its code for types. The package @empathic/clax-client-sdk (web/sdk/README.md, published on npm at 0.1.0) gives the same ServiceClient from '@empathic/clax-client-sdk/service', the overlay from '/overlay' and declarations for both, with no checkout. docs/feedback.md never mentions it, and web/sdk/README.md never links back to docs/feedback.md, so a stranger who starts at the guide copies files they will not get updates for, and one who starts at npm never learns how to make a queue. Copied files also drift: client.ts imports ./sign and ./types, so the copy set must track any new import.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":104},"end":{"line":110},"content_hash":"180d4d726ec790a7e7d18d76f50f41b2df11e6a1452ecec9a701997167cb396b"},"suggested_fix":"Lead section 2 with `npm install @empathic/clax-client-sdk` and `import { ServiceClient } from \"@empathic/clax-client-sdk/service\"`, keep copying as the fallback for a backend that cannot take a dependency, and offer `import { overlay } from \"@empathic/clax-client-sdk/overlay\"` beside the script tag and dynamic import in section 3.","summary":"The integration guide has backends copy web/sdk/src files from a checkout and never names the published npm package","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:27.481612Z","id":"6c2b9349add4f5fb615a87012127c47084f400db3e811803aa0b189cde388a96","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md ends at /setup and links neither docs/feedback.md nor the SDK. A third party running their app in the same cluster will point ServiceClient's `instance` at the clax Service (http://clax.<ns>.svc:7480). That fails twice: base/networkpolicy.yaml admits 7480 only from the fleet's pods and namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true (Security, NetworkPolicy), so the request times out; and the fleet picks the instance by Host (lines 28-33), so a Service host names no instance and gets 404, or the default instance. The SDK cannot send the public Host to an internal address: signedFetch (web/sdk/src/client.ts:53-60) signs and sends the host of `instance`, and fetch cannot override Host. The guide also does not say that `instance` is the instance's `[hosted] public_origin`, that the Ingress must pass Authorization and Clax-Signature through and rewrite neither the path nor Host (the signature covers both, crates/clax-http/src/grant_auth.rs head()), or that it must not answer CORS itself on /api/queues/*/reporter* (crates/clax-http/src/reporter.rs cors() answers for the queue's origins).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":28},"end":{"line":33},"content_hash":"d4f1ccae443541605dc3fd7c73170f3b437aaced2d16432e8d4659537159d399"},"suggested_fix":"Add a 'Report queues and the SDK' section to docs/kubernetes.md: `instance` is the instance's public_origin; backends call it through the Ingress at that origin, not the Service; the Ingress keeps Host and the path and adds no CORS of its own on the reporter routes; then link docs/feedback.md for the queue, grant and overlay steps.","summary":"The Kubernetes guide never says how a deployer's backend and pages reach an instance's report queue, and the obvious in-cluster route fails","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:38:53.748634Z","id":"ed030ae4b48679515c9c47e8009e29c91621471cad5d4aad713b29221e727674","body":{"detail":"Upgrading, step 3, says to check that `kubectl exec clax-2 -- clax-fleet status` shows none `failing`. `clax-fleet status` prints one line per shard built by shard_status in crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:476-481: `(<pod>): ready <bool>, running <list>`. The `failing` map is in each shard's /status JSON (Fleet::failing, crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:550-572) but the command never prints it. An instance whose migration failed on the new release is not in `running`, which reads the same as an instance not yet requested, so an operator following the step sees no failure and releases the rest of the rollout (step 4), which is the case the step exists to catch.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":448},"end":{"line":449},"content_hash":"60976bd56888045c937fd6ad0023781e774112500945fb4458de858109f72949"},"suggested_fix":"Either print each shard's `failing` entries in `clax-fleet status` (name, error, failures in a row), or change step 3 to read the JSON: `kubectl port-forward pod/clax-2 7482` and `curl -s localhost:7482/status | jq .failing`, expecting `{}`.","summary":"The upgrade's check step says `clax-fleet status` shows `failing` instances, but the command prints only readiness and the running instances","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:38:53.748668Z","id":"4704d91f298eb05264c4294200d9f829ee3144b24d21964930a08e647db10699","body":{"detail":"The command picks `ls acme/backups | grep '^clax-.*\\.db$' | sort -t- -k3,3 | tail -n 1`: the newest copy by time, of any schema version. A copy is made only when a migration changes the database (crates/clax-store/src/backup.rs:143-145), and the directory keeps the newest three plus one older (backup.rs:10-12). For an instance that did not migrate on this release (no schema change, or its first request came after the rollback began), the newest copy is the one an earlier upgrade made, possibly months old; the snippet moves the live clax.db aside and puts that copy in place, and nothing it prints shows the copy's version or time. 'What an instance stored after its migration is lost' (line 472) then understates the loss.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":463},"end":{"line":466},"content_hash":"9b4e95a62b94a05edd7e3843f23739b1336a6ad6f2439880a973629489266631"},"suggested_fix":"Have the snippet echo the chosen file and refuse a copy older than the upgrade (compare its UTC stamp with when the new pod started, or select by the schema version the old release expects, which the instance's 'upgrading the database; copied it first' log line names with from/to), and tell the reader to skip instances whose log shows no copy made on this upgrade.","summary":"The rollback snippet restores the newest backup in the home without checking that this upgrade made it, so it can restore a copy from an earlier upgrade and lose everything since","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:38:53.748732Z","id":"2b2a2a653b41fb9bf863e769491f623493fd5b4ab3df7a294f5cd607d63c1b01","body":{"detail":"Step 1 sets spec.updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition with kubectl patch, outside the overlay. A plain `kubectl apply -k` keeps the field (it is not in the last applied configuration), but Argo CD or Flux with self-heal reverts drift from the overlay, so the partition is removed and the StatefulSet rolls every shard, the failure the procedure exists to avoid ('the rollout goes on through every shard even when a release's migration fails', lines 440-442). The same page already warns that `kubectl scale` is undone by the next apply or a GitOps controller (lines 275-277) and tells readers to set replicas in the overlay.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":444},"end":{"line":451},"content_hash":"a54e53a5cc0eea8e2ac192b3f5bcaa89abe0743240a1126cc84960367d03d335"},"suggested_fix":"Set the partition in the overlay as a patch on the StatefulSet (path /spec/updateStrategy/rollingUpdate/partition), applied together with the new release, and lower it to 0 in a second commit; keep the kubectl patch form only as an alternative for clusters applied by hand.","summary":"The one-shard-first upgrade holds the rollout with `kubectl patch`, which a GitOps controller undoes, releasing every shard at once","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:07.377206Z","id":"e8e51a916b4ed63611cc4c51d39530ba0adc976fcf9b6a8176f3316020e62c87","body":{"detail":"docs/feedback.md:91-96 ('web/sdk/src (...) and the overlay's overlay.d.ts are AGPL-3.0-only'), :109 ('(AGPL-3.0-only; see ...)'), :376-377 ('AGPL-3.0-only, see \"Licences of the code you copy\"'). LICENSE:65-71 puts web/sdk/, web/overlay/ and web/bridge/ under LGPL-2.1-only 'so the apps that use them may be under any license'; web/sdk/package.json:13 \"license\": \"LGPL-2.1-only\"; web/scripts/build-sdk.mjs:106-121 licenseNotice() ships the LGPL-2.1-only notice; web/sdk/README.md:73-76 says LGPL-2.1-only. overlay.d.ts lives at web/overlay/overlay.d.ts, inside the LGPL directory. The guide contradicts the repository's grant.","kind":"comment","references":"2d1f32370178c4d1b87eb44771e7b6168837ee161012302e7b3cb6a7fb309240","summary":"confirmed: docs/feedback.md calls web/sdk/src and overlay.d.ts AGPL-3.0-only; LICENSE and the package say LGPL-2.1-only","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:07.377691Z","id":"19b1e0f427561942d3731b1cfd101abffed542ecd07d45dd498e1e6349e7c772","body":{"detail":"`grep -n 'clax-client-sdk\\|npm' docs/feedback.md` prints nothing; docs/feedback.md:104-109 says 'copy service.ts, client.ts, sign.ts and types.ts from web/sdk/src into your backend'. web/scripts/build-sdk.mjs:65-69 ENTRIES publishes '.', './overlay' and './service' (ServiceClient from sdk/src/service.ts), and web/sdk/README.md:16-20,62-71 shows `npm install @empathic/clax-client-sdk` and `import { ServiceClient } from \"@empathic/clax-client-sdk/service\"`. `grep -n 'feedback.md\\|contract.md\\|docs/' web/sdk/README.md` prints nothing. Copy-set drift is real: service.ts:5-7 imports ./types, ./client and ./sign, client.ts:3-4 imports ./types and ./sign. (Not checked here: whether 0.1.0 is live on the npm registry; web/sdk/package.json:3 is 0.1.0.)","kind":"comment","references":"a46458dc0437bb7c66669ed0a4247833a8fcf1bfb1ddb32f0399c2940cd09469","summary":"confirmed: the guide has backends copy web/sdk/src files and never names @empathic/clax-client-sdk; the README never links back","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:07.378897Z","id":"3eeb19234bd074e9e2158ec5c56859e9aa048413698820f7161b3bc47d994732","body":{"detail":"`grep -n 'feedback\\|SDK\\|queue\\|CORS' docs/kubernetes.md` matches nothing about report queues (only public_origin at :146). deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits 7480 only from fleet pods and namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/ingress=true, so another app's pod times out on a CNI that enforces policies. docs/kubernetes.md:28-33: instances are matched by Host, and a host no instance names gets 404 (or the default instance). web/sdk/src/client.ts:53-60 signs url.host of `instance`, and crates/clax-http/src/grant_auth.rs:781-805 head() verifies against the received Host and path_and_query, so the public Host cannot be sent to an internal address and an Ingress that rewrites either breaks every signature. Kind concern (a documentation gap) is right.","kind":"comment","references":"6c2b9349add4f5fb615a87012127c47084f400db3e811803aa0b189cde388a96","summary":"confirmed: docs/kubernetes.md says nothing about reaching report queues, and the in-cluster Service route is blocked and misrouted","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:17.207364Z","id":"d2ce169830bc6ce137fe6fb66f5d7e9f72a584e69440418d1c2171ab2ee867f3","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:448-449: 'check that `kubectl exec clax-2 -- clax-fleet status` shows none `failing`'. crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:475-480 formats each shard as `({pod}): ready {ready}, running {running}` from the /status JSON; `grep -n failing crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs` returns nothing. The map is built by `Fleet::failing` (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:550-571) and only reaches /status.","kind":"comment","references":"ed030ae4b48679515c9c47e8009e29c91621471cad5d4aad713b29221e727674","summary":"confirmed: `clax-fleet status` prints only pod, ready and running, never the failing map step 3 tells the reader to check","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:17.207738Z","id":"9edf50619ce5f3fae3e584debf9d8c43d3e566c8dc6b278e068eb308e471405b","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:463-466 selects `ls acme/backups | grep '^clax-.*\\.db$' | sort -t- -k3,3 | tail -n 1` and prints nothing about it. A copy is made only when 0 < version < known (crates/clax-store/src/backup.rs:143-145), and older copies are kept (KEEP = 3 plus one, backup.rs:10-12). For an instance that truly migrated on this release the newest copy is this upgrade's, so the hazard is applying it to an instance that did not migrate (line 457 says 'for each such instance' without saying how to recognise one): the snippet then restores an earlier upgrade's copy. Also, a home created on the new release (version 0, no copy) has no backups/ entry, so `cp` fails after the live clax.db was already moved aside. The finding's second case ('first request came after the rollback began') does not arise, since step 1 pauses the instance. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"4704d91f298eb05264c4294200d9f829ee3144b24d21964930a08e647db10699","summary":"confirmed: the rollback snippet takes the newest copy of any version and time, and the page gives no way to tell which instances migrated on this upgrade","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:41:17.208434Z","id":"1b0115701366e656e9e4afc77563ebade6fd91f22422bbabaf63f839ddf17f93","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:444-451 holds and releases the rollout with `kubectl patch sts clax` on spec.updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition; `grep -rn partition deploy/k8s` finds no partition in the manifests. The Flux FAQ (https://fluxcd.io/flux/faq/) says of objects edited with kubectl: 'all changes will be undone when kustomize-controller reconciles a Flux Kustomization containing that object'. Argo CD's default client-side diff may not flag a field absent from both desired and last-applied state, so the risk is certain for Flux and setting-dependent for Argo CD. The same page already warns about this for `kubectl scale` (docs/kubernetes.md:275-277). Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"2b2a2a653b41fb9bf863e769491f623493fd5b4ab3df7a294f5cd607d63c1b01","summary":"confirmed: the partition is set by kubectl patch outside the overlay, which Flux reverts on its next reconcile","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.683396Z","id":"deb42d6e799cd48cf07737ca58d8f1cda68c5cab070637683b6f50f8bb15eaa6","body":{"detail":"Commit 917e41e0e 'fix(fleet): clax-fleet status prints the instances failing to start' (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs failing_lines, appended by shard_status: '  failing <name>: <n> in a row, next try in <ms> ms: <error>'); the check step's wording is in commit 8fd4a8500 'docs(k8s): hold an upgrade's rollout with a partition in the overlay', and Operating it describes the lines. Test: main.rs tests::a_shard_that_refuses_or_does_not_answer_says_so asserts the exact lines for a shard whose /status lists two failing instances, sorted by name, and the unchanged line for one without. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/...` at 917e41e0e: clax-fleet_bin_test PASSED.","kind":"comment","references":"ed030ae4b48679515c9c47e8009e29c91621471cad5d4aad713b29221e727674","summary":"fixed: clax-fleet status prints a `failing` line under each shard for every instance in its /status failing map, and the upgrade's check step reads those lines","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.684133Z","id":"d4c3da49ccb94ca0d3c03c174428d34eaa62ee8915e6f45ac66dd040a8432ae2","body":{"detail":"Commit 7759d9910 'docs(k8s): how an app's backend and pages reach a report queue': `instance` is the instance's [hosted] public_origin, reached through the Ingress also from a backend in the same cluster, never http://clax.<namespace>.svc:7480 (Host matching, the NetworkPolicy, and the SDK signing the host it sends to); the Ingress keeps Host and the path, passes Authorization and Clax-Signature, and adds no CORS on /api/queues/*/reporter*; then docs/feedback.md for the queue, the grant, the token route and the overlay. Documentation only; no test.","kind":"comment","references":"6c2b9349add4f5fb615a87012127c47084f400db3e811803aa0b189cde388a96","summary":"fixed: docs/kubernetes.md has a Report queues and the SDK section, linked from the Host-matching paragraph, and links docs/feedback.md","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.685073Z","id":"43396a1ca52addb3c24a36b80ba3d3811198656c3a7a1da44ba657b3b42407dd","body":{"detail":"Commit 8fd4a8500 'docs(k8s): hold an upgrade's rollout with a partition in the overlay' (docs/kubernetes.md, Upgrading: a JSON patch adding /spec/updateStrategy/rollingUpdate with partition N-1 beside the release, and why kubectl patch is undone by Argo CD or Flux). The base StatefulSet (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml) has updateStrategy.type RollingUpdate and no rollingUpdate, so the `add` op applies. Documentation only; no test.","kind":"comment","references":"2b2a2a653b41fb9bf863e769491f623493fd5b4ab3df7a294f5cd607d63c1b01","summary":"fixed: the one-shard-first upgrade sets the partition as a patch in the overlay, applied with the new release and lowered to 0 in a second change; kubectl patch is kept only for clusters applied by hand","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.685789Z","id":"a42df8ddd312424c137bcb6e317b0cadabeadebf58b736c512c7c0d3a1fb7669","body":{"detail":"Commit c4ba6ecc4 'docs(k8s): roll back only from the copy this upgrade made' (docs/kubernetes.md, Rolling back; step 1 of Upgrading notes `date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S`). It also says which instances need it (those that logged 'upgrading the database; copied it first') and that one made on the new release has no copy. Checked by running the snippet's shell under dash and macOS sh against scratch homes: copy clax-70-20261102T130000123Z.db with SINCE=20261102T120000 -> 'restoring acme/backups/clax-70-20261102T130000123Z.db', exit 0, live clax.db and clax.db-wal moved to acme.rolled-back; SINCE=20261103T000000 -> 'acme has no copy made since 20261103T000000: it did not migrate on this upgrade', exit 1, nothing moved; no copy -> the same refusal, exit 1, nothing moved.","kind":"comment","references":"4704d91f298eb05264c4294200d9f829ee3144b24d21964930a08e647db10699","summary":"fixed: the rollback snippet takes SINCE (noted before the upgrade), refuses a home whose newest copy predates it or that has none before moving anything, and prints the copy it restores","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:28.320958Z","id":"c8479e332966f3c58e14f0562173881c6c277e222a5e622784afe0de4bea7ddc","body":{"detail":"examples/ingress.yaml uses ingressClassName nginx and nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io annotations, and docs/kubernetes.md:254 points to nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/whitelist-source-range. The Kubernetes project announced ingress-nginx's retirement, with best-effort maintenance ending March 2026 and no further releases or security fixes after (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/). New clusters will mostly use a Gateway API implementation, for which the docs give no route, timeout or body-limit equivalent, and an operator translating the annotations has to find each by hand.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged after the lines moved; originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":444},"end":{"line":451},"content_hash":"b3d226aba6871e1261e11519325898e47f3bf6afc2a25a37abad84f1994b40d2"},"suggested_fix":"Add a Gateway API example (Gateway listeners for the origin and the wildcard site domain, an HTTPRoute to the clax Service, and timeouts via HTTPRoute `timeouts` or the implementation's policy), keep the ingress-nginx one as legacy, and state each setting generically (read timeout above 3660 s, body limit above 16 MB, WebSocket upgrade, source allow list).","summary":"The only Ingress example, and the Cloudflare allow-list advice, are for ingress-nginx, which Kubernetes retired in March 2026","supersedes":"43ed129155fc9808213ea07bee9a310d9fcd0693f9ea58d703650fd9fe8b419b","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:28.574354Z","id":"5af7d7d105e725bcca08205170dcd8d0d1c9c10b09b00a2b16828e04bff09010","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md:284 dismisses a volume snapshot as 'only crash-consistent' and offers pause-and-copy per instance, which means downtime per tenant for every backup and no scheduled option. SQLite in WAL mode is designed to recover from exactly the state a power loss leaves (https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, 'Durability'; https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html lists non-atomic copies, not atomic snapshots, as the risk), and every file of a shard's homes lives on one PVC, so one CSI VolumeSnapshot captures them at one instant (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/). If some part of a home is not safe to restore from a crash-consistent image, the docs should say which; if all is, operators can back up without downtime.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged after the lines moved; originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":592},"end":{"line":594},"content_hash":"1332a3bf70865e7b2971ad3e65a70340fd4797cde12878729c734d0bd7df4067"},"suggested_fix":"State whether a crash-consistent snapshot of data-clax-N is a supported restore source; if so, show a VolumeSnapshot of each PVC and a restore (new PVC from the snapshot, scale the shard down, swap), and keep pause-and-copy for single-instance restores. Mention SQLite's online backup (`VACUUM INTO`) if the binary could offer it as a command.","summary":"The only documented backup takes each instance offline, though an atomic snapshot of a shard's volume is a valid SQLite recovery point","supersedes":"7c8127dcc0a28f504d7cea4c5c2532698a7064f70ba83ef4f5d8074fc971bb02","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:46:40.153955Z","id":"1475954d28bf51b5deec291b9680fb1b0422168ac21587ae76aece809e9dc89d","body":{"detail":"docs/feedback.md has outside products copy web/sdk/src service.ts, client.ts, sign.ts and types.ts into their backends, save <instance>/_clax/overlay.d.ts beside their code, and optionally depend on clax-core for clax_core::sig. Per LICENSE, web/ is AGPL-3.0-only and crates/clax-core is GPL-2.0-only OR AGPL-3.0-only, so a third-party backend that follows the guide takes on copyleft obligations; Empathic's own products are unaffected (same copyright holder). The guide now says so (section 'Licences of the code you copy') and points at the protocol-only path (docs/contract.md 'Signed requests' and 'Report queue routes'). The option: license the backend-facing SDK files, web/overlay/overlay.d.ts and web/overlay/guide/ under 0BSD or MIT, as apps/clax_app/assets/kit already is 0BSD, and update LICENSE and web/package.json to match.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged after the fixes of the review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02 SDK findings moved its lines; first recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":91},"end":{"line":103},"content_hash":"cd69fc2402eee1bace9f5433d527a43d45360491a257a7046e272455bc1b90e2"},"summary":"Relicense web/sdk reporter files, overlay.d.ts and the signing code permissively (as the starter kit is 0BSD) so outside products may copy them?","supersedes":"693c2da4eb0376e28e6f2790a5a6d9c75916c9b8641ca8b94c1d39f11fc076bb","tags":["status:needs-decision","licence","feedback","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:47:53.437401Z","id":"6f980808f89fd93a5bd44897755ab27e65d54218b8b5872065aa55faffe9d7b1","body":{"detail":"Commit \"docs(feedback): name the npm package, its LGPL license and the clients' retries\". docs/feedback.md 'Licences of the code you use' (lines 91-101) names LGPL-2.1-only for web/sdk, web/overlay and web/bridge and keeps only clax-core's GPL-2.0-only OR AGPL-3.0-only for the Rust path; the dynamic-import section now says LGPL-2.1-only. `grep -n -i agpl docs/feedback.md` matches only the two clax-core mentions (lines 99 and 177). Record 693c2da4 (re-anchored as 1475954d) is left open with a reply proposing its close.","kind":"comment","references":"2d1f32370178c4d1b87eb44771e7b6168837ee161012302e7b3cb6a7fb309240","summary":"fixed: the guide's licence section now says web/sdk, web/overlay (overlay.d.ts included) and web/bridge are LGPL-2.1-only, as LICENSE does","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:47:53.438030Z","id":"984116c83ef957a021616a311c68c130d1189db04d2b5b35b8ba02ef9b1be868","body":{"detail":"Commit \"docs(feedback): name the npm package, its LGPL license and the clients' retries\". docs/feedback.md section 2 'TypeScript' leads with `npm install @empathic/clax-client-sdk` and imports ClaxError and ServiceClient from '@empathic/clax-client-sdk/service' (which now exports ClaxError: commit \"feat(sdk): build the service entry as CommonJS too, under a require condition\"); section 3 shows `import { overlay } from \"@empathic/clax-client-sdk/overlay\"` before the script tag. web/sdk/README.md now links docs/feedback.md and docs/contract.md 'Report queue routes'.","kind":"comment","references":"a46458dc0437bb7c66669ed0a4247833a8fcf1bfb1ddb32f0399c2940cd09469","summary":"fixed: the TypeScript backend starts from npm install @empathic/clax-client-sdk and its /service entry; copying is the fallback; section 3 offers the /overlay entry","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:47:53.441745Z","id":"9dbd828bde64eb876494962313d7a0f743868a1c31632bee8252188fe9aa1618","body":{"detail":"LICENSE's LGPL-2.1-only entry lists web/sdk/, web/overlay/ and web/bridge/ 'so the apps that use them may be under any license', web/sdk/package.json says LGPL-2.1-only, and docs/feedback.md 'Licences of the code you use' (lines 91-101, commit \"docs(feedback): name the npm package, its LGPL license and the clients' retries\") now says the same. The remaining copyleft on the guide's paths is clax-core's GPL-2.0-only OR AGPL-3.0-only for the Rust backend. Whether that answers this question (or whether clax_core::sig should also be relicensed) is the owner's call; this record is left open for them.","kind":"comment","references":"1475954d28bf51b5deec291b9680fb1b0422168ac21587ae76aece809e9dc89d","summary":"Proposed close: LICENSE now puts web/sdk, web/overlay (overlay.d.ts included) and web/bridge under LGPL-2.1-only, and the guide says so","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:57.194067Z","id":"d7e531723ff44f699fcab5c8bd8d51eb3d0ff9a18207ac2c1ed04d22354dd14e","body":{"detail":"a_shard_that_refuses_or_does_not_answer_says_so, extended to assert the failing line, passes in //crates/clax-fleet. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:a6a4812c4","summary":"fixed by a6a4812c4: clax-fleet status prints each shard's failing instances (name, failures in a row, next try, error), and the upgrade step reads it","supersedes":"ed030ae4b48679515c9c47e8009e29c91621471cad5d4aad713b29221e727674","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:57.383476Z","id":"7300e94a30fa5e209152cf771741c1747e36b649a2192e3308e8a17ebffeceb5","body":{"detail":"`grep -n 'Report queues and the SDK' docs/kubernetes.md` finds the section at 2faa8748c. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:91c4a1434","summary":"fixed by 91c4a1434: docs/kubernetes.md has a 'Report queues and the SDK' section: instance is the public_origin, reached through the Ingress keeping Host and path with no CORS of its own, linking docs/feedback.md","supersedes":"6c2b9349add4f5fb615a87012127c47084f400db3e811803aa0b189cde388a96","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:57.566950Z","id":"4ddade80ca52c65a906ac131e71469f6e7c16215cb21eb3fe9c2f290a0e0e13d","body":{"detail":"docs/kubernetes.md 'Upgrading' at 2faa8748c shows the overlay patch on /spec/updateStrategy/rollingUpdate/partition. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0a8c843d2","summary":"fixed by 0a8c843d2: the upgrade's partition is a patch in the overlay applied with the release and lowered in a second commit; kubectl patch is kept only for clusters applied by hand","supersedes":"2b2a2a653b41fb9bf863e769491f623493fd5b4ab3df7a294f5cd607d63c1b01","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/kubernetes.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:57.755452Z","id":"b63540f844c4f93c13b562b582c3f27a15c4e6f704ab2f89132e377fb40a023d","body":{"detail":"the snippet was run under dash and sh against scratch homes with a copy after SINCE (restored), a copy before it (refused) and none (refused). `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:0b3d7840b","summary":"fixed by 0b3d7840b: the rollback snippet takes a SINCE noted before the upgrade, refuses a home with no copy made since then before moving anything, and prints the copy it restores","supersedes":"4704d91f298eb05264c4294200d9f829ee3144b24d21964930a08e647db10699","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:57.940381Z","id":"995d968678135040b765ce8ead59b461de1a16aa52e600d543de97178eeb2234","body":{"detail":"`grep -n AGPL docs/feedback.md` at 2faa8748c matches only the two clax-core notes (lines 99 and 177).`bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:53565cba9","summary":"fixed by 53565cba9: docs/feedback.md says web/sdk, web/overlay and web/bridge are LGPL-2.1-only, as LICENSE does; only clax-core's dual licence remains for the Rust path","supersedes":"2d1f32370178c4d1b87eb44771e7b6168837ee161012302e7b3cb6a7fb309240","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/feedback.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T05:40:58.133098Z","id":"deb65fdf695f86f1bed1dc1a16e7c3de73aef16d1fbc79d7b69803a8fb10586a","body":{"detail":"docs/feedback.md sections 2 and 3 at 2faa8748c. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-server:all //web:all //crates/clax-http/...` on branch k8s-review-fixes at 2faa8748c: 'Executed 0 out of 20 tests: 20 tests pass.' (all cached from the same inputs before the rebase onto f23218fb5)","kind":"resolve","ref":"git:53565cba9","summary":"fixed by 53565cba9: docs/feedback.md leads the TypeScript backend with npm install @empathic/clax-client-sdk and its /service entry, copying as the fallback, and offers the /overlay entry","supersedes":"a46458dc0437bb7c66669ed0a4247833a8fcf1bfb1ddb32f0399c2940cd09469","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T07:58:07.830539Z","id":"bde626d999ac1aa70eb057631f9760eebc79fb73a717e98133c133f589c00f07","body":{"detail":"JobsOwners::claim (crates/clax-http/src/jobs_owner.rs) spawns the drain task before answering, so when the old write ends at once the event is published before the HTTP answer is written, and the stream may deliver it first. A worker that follows the contract literally (on owner_draining, wait for held true, then claim) waits forever. The app's queue handles it (apps/clax_app/test/queue/update_test.dart, 'a drain's end heard before the claim's answer claims again'), but the contract and the route's doc comment (crates/clax-server/src/routes/jobs.rs:182-197) state only the order that follows.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae)'s record.\n\nRe-anchored after merge c20bac570 moved the lines; first recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3800},"end":{"line":3810},"content_hash":"1ed79589ec9ea593cb40c67a9b41715bc5a99934bdb268290822df6833545275"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the contract: a held true heard for the client after the claim was sent counts, so on owner_draining claim again at once if one was heard.","summary":"The contract does not say that the drain's jobs_owner held true can arrive on the stream before the claim's 409 owner_draining","supersedes":"4e6ebe54ec113b238060268a8a4abdc11c04d4403bb37f2b513815c2c16e3b7b","tags":["session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T07:58:07.958516Z","id":"f522d017deb8150dfa0d248f1cea76749e22a88ea205ac72fd50566f23d9db48","body":{"detail":"Since the Clax app dropped its launch snapshot, HomeHost.call (apps/clax_app/lib/home/home_host.dart, case 'shown' and case 'tiles') resolves shown() with null and takes tiles() without doing anything, and the shipped home app (apps/clax_app/home/home.js) calls neither tiles() nor reads opened. The contract text here, its copies in plugins/*/skills/clax/SKILL.md (runtime capabilities list), web/contract/clax-extensions.d.ts, the app's prompt copies (apps/clax_app/assets/prompt, kept by scripts/sync-app-prompt.sh) and web/shell/src/caps/home.ts still offer both. Options: remove tiles() and the {opened} answer from the contract and every copy (an agent then stops writing tiles() calls into home apps), or keep them as documented no-ops for home apps already written.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged from docs/contract.md:6036:6038 to docs/contract.md:6103:6105: originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (ai); the code it describes moved when this branch changed the file.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:5ba3638a-dc4b-4c8f-8349-140e70a28c92)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6980},"end":{"line":6982},"content_hash":"74dbcbcc0abf09fed3dec186500510d36558c0baba57f883b198117ec06512ae"},"summary":"The home capability's contract still describes the launch image: shown() resolving {opened: id} and tiles(list) kept with an image shown on the next cold start, which the Clax app no longer does","supersedes":"56b32cc9c3cbeded98670868257c0b40a13650a4c798a0565cd12d29c198bbaf","tags":["status:needs-decision","home","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/prod.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T07:58:08.088642Z","id":"70d513111177cd72c1e03d8a12e35c86c9fd863d2a030e67916bbcd3d4ee4e69","body":{"detail":"Cloudflare's API token permissions reference names no Artifacts permission, and wrangler's lookups are not documented per permission. The first deploy-prod.yml run with a token made from this table confirms it; amend the table with what that run needed.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:b2fb951f-3ceb-4c65-a7b7-a705e815488a)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":92},"end":{"line":123},"content_hash":"e6e96d1bf1c2a6bee8d592a59e7a463b75e5b90d803f93581df408e07f409d15"},"summary":"The Cloudflare token's permissions are partly unconfirmed: Artifacts, queue event subscriptions, Zone Read, Account Settings Read, User Details and Memberships Read, DNS Edit","supersedes":"cf083589ed7b03eca332e2da8d9e1652b7f651b3a3a0bae859041b325fa63bad","tags":["untested","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T07:58:10.639992Z","id":"80525a608cd652cbb34f412d2aedbfe48e71a218c684b818c2d5ec0b435c60df","body":{"detail":"scripts/perf-app-launch.sh --android now reads each launch's timings from the app's log, because a release APK is not debuggable and 'adb run-as dev.empathic.clax cat files/timings.jsonl' fails on it (the earlier script read the file that way, so it could not measure such a build either). The baseline step 5 measures the build installed on the phone, which comes from before the app ran on the embedded core and so from before the log lines: every launch would count as missing. Options: (a) take the phone baseline from a debuggable build of the baseline commit and give the script back a run-as path for it; (b) backport the clax-timing: log lines onto the baseline commit in a scratch build; (c) take no phone baseline and compare the phone against its first measured in-process build.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1297},"end":{"line":1312},"content_hash":"bdc73d7ae5d6b0e391d3817f967906815c9d66b791ca1b705b4a47ca3502af44"},"summary":"The phone's launch baseline (step 5) cannot be taken on a release build from before 7c58e3dd28c6: it logs no clax-timing: lines and run-as cannot read its timings.jsonl","supersedes":"cbc559b7a18186567b3f842cbb804b2b03bd3208c1f4d2a38a93260469f979b2","tags":["perf","android","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae","session:claude-code:988152cb-8f78-4b12-acdd-a455490bed05"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/usage.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T08:01:01.043668Z","id":"ea44b8a987a6d70d173624ba572c0089543e3bd6b66e2390027e623cc0790496","body":{"detail":"scripts/perf-app-launch.sh --android now reads each launch's timings from the app's log, because a release APK is not debuggable and 'adb run-as dev.empathic.clax cat files/timings.jsonl' fails on it (the earlier script read the file that way, so it could not measure such a build either). The baseline step 5 measures the build installed on the phone, which comes from before the app ran on the embedded core and so from before the log lines: every launch would count as missing. Options: (a) take the phone baseline from a debuggable build of the baseline commit and give the script back a run-as path for it; (b) backport the clax-timing: log lines onto the baseline commit in a scratch build; (c) take no phone baseline and compare the phone against its first measured in-process build.\nRe-anchored unchanged from mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae)'s record.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1226},"end":{"line":1241},"content_hash":"f1e66da92c83791031464ce64fea17e17a9057461db578d2b1f52ab387511185"},"summary":"The phone's launch baseline (step 5) cannot be taken on a release build from before 7c58e3dd28c6: it logs no clax-timing: lines and run-as cannot read its timings.jsonl","supersedes":"5364d8a1d1d85df0b8c8a9873a7b8d929b17eeeee034f4e12241a7c44ada793a","tags":["perf","android","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:985c579c-9fcb-4f5b-b70f-deedb4f3ea58"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:24:31.894195Z","id":"76e202fc6c84bd50e882a484e3f027722ef9509f7d8e7f89a3996d296b2d3ae8","body":{"detail":"Written with the protocol's messages (`crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs`), the hub's rules (`crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs`) and the fixture (`docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json`), before the routes. Still to come, and each to be held to this section and the fixture when it lands: the three routes and their statuses (`clax_surface::wire::status`), with `invalid_request` for a body that is not the message; the store behind `Pending`; the run loop's provider that enqueues a call and reads its outcome; parking a run on a call that waits; starting a run of a host's kind (`not_yet` for `bind`); a host process for tests that replays the fixture's `script` over HTTP. The fixture's `script` has no `runs` step, because nothing here starts a run. When the routes are served, the sentence that says they are not, and the entry under \"What is not yet available\", go.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":10265},"end":{"line":10281},"content_hash":"dbc914ee04c0e39eab5a69885b3a306ad61c9e1d10282e45c17c86fe95125887"},"summary":"This section describes routes no daemon serves yet, and a store that keeps calls which is not written","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T19:44:07.306361Z","id":"8ce356a8076e9c14543c0733cb2f94dc8c4a8b38b322964380285ed6d4d6e0cb","body":{"detail":"A host written from this section and docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json only (stdlib Python, replaying the script's host steps) had to guess: 1. each route's success status and content type, and how the token is sent; 2. whether `answers` may be left out (`ServeRequest` defaults it) and what a `wait_ms` over 12,000 does (clamped, not refused); 3. the range of `retry_after_ms` (0 means at once; any u64); 4. that a delivery of a kind the host does not know may have no `id` and needs no answer (the `tolerated` event has none, while `ServeResponse::calls` says every delivery has one), and whether it is sent again; 5. what a host does with an `end` or a `withheld.reason` it does not know; 6. what 4 KiB and 1 MiB are measured on (UTF-8 bytes of the message; the result as compact JSON re-written by the daemon, not the bytes sent); 7. the form of IDs: a registration ID is `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,256}`, an answer ID over 256 bytes is ignored; 8. that the host must keep its registration ID to be sent its waiting calls after its own restart, and that on `unknown_registration` it registers *with* that ID; 9. that up to four serves of one registration may be open, each is sent everything due, and a fifth evicts the oldest; 10. the largest request body a route takes, and the largest answer (64 deliveries of up to 1 MiB); 11. that answers are taken in order and the first per ID wins, `accepted` included; 12. which error codes a host may answer; 13. the codes a route may answer that the table lacks (`internal_error`, `storage_failed` at 500, the latter after some answers were taken); 14. what `bind: {}` does; 15. that resuming a held registration while another host serves it makes both its hosts. The reverse, what `crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs` takes that the section does not say: an answer with `\"accepted\": null` or `\"error\": null` beside its one member is read as that member alone; an `ended` with `\"result\": null` differs from one without; `ServeResponse` without `calls` reads as empty.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":10283},"end":{"line":10303},"content_hash":"ca425ca94de47ba17cf503e6240f812c25ec64bef6f1f1d40ec3eb2c49da781a"},"summary":"What a host written from this section and the fixture alone has to guess","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-11T20:05:02.883115Z","id":"4fbd702bea01645767f5c76255452518694b4e51dc457cbdeb8b824df6076e3e","body":{"detail":"docs/contract.md, \"ClaxBot surfaces\": how a request is made (`Authorization: Bearer`, JSON, 200, the 8 MiB body and `too_large`); `answers` optional and `wait_ms` read as 12,000 at the most; the range of `retry_after_ms`; a delivery of an unknown kind may have no `id`, needs no answer and is not sent again; an unknown `end` is an end and an unknown `withheld` reason is \"withheld\"; what 4 KiB and 1 MiB are measured on; the form of IDs; the host keeps its registration ID and registers with it on `unknown_registration`; four serves, each sent everything due, a fifth ends the oldest; 64 deliveries and 8 MiB an answer; answers in order and what follows `accepted`; which codes a host may answer; `unauthorized`, `too_large`, `internal_error` and `storage_failed` in the table; `bind`, `{}` too, is `not_yet`; two hosts of one registration. The reverse: `null` beside an answer's one member is now refused (`present`, crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs, and the fixture's `refused`); an `ended` without `result` has none; a `calls` left out is empty. What the routes will do is stated from the hub's limits and `clax_surface::wire::status`; the routes are still to be held to it.","kind":"comment","references":"8ce356a8076e9c14543c0733cb2f94dc8c4a8b38b322964380285ed6d4d6e0cb","summary":"The fifteen gaps, and the three of the reverse, are answered in the section","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:54:59.240066Z","id":"e911ea360d119481162aeb6cf3762b97b91a5fdc2eafc19d1dc46f1ecc02dcb7","body":{"detail":"Written with the protocol's messages (`crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs`), the hub's rules (`crates/clax-bot/src/bot_surfaces.rs`) and the fixture (`docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json`), before the routes. Still to come, and each to be held to this section and the fixture when it lands: the three routes and their statuses (`clax_surface::wire::status`), with `invalid_request` for a body that is not the message; the store behind `Pending`; the run loop's provider that enqueues a call and reads its outcome; parking a run on a call that waits; starting a run of a host's kind (`not_yet` for `bind`); a host process for tests that replays the fixture's `script` over HTTP. The fixture's `script` has no `runs` step, because nothing here starts a run. When the routes are served, the sentence that says they are not, and the entry under \"What is not yet available\", go.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":10270},"end":{"line":10287},"content_hash":"371200bf5ad5ba9341838229a9a31ec0ac63d6fc364fdfe17debf97f03b9316f"},"summary":"This section describes routes no daemon serves yet, and a store that keeps calls which is not written","supersedes":"76e202fc6c84bd50e882a484e3f027722ef9509f7d8e7f89a3996d296b2d3ae8","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","follow-up","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T00:54:59.428580Z","id":"75e4203e901fbb1057014ded60d6fec8233dd04d8bbd28592d25028e7f9b3ca8","body":{"detail":"A host written from this section and docs/claxbot-surfaces/contract.json only (stdlib Python, replaying the script's host steps) had to guess: 1. each route's success status and content type, and how the token is sent; 2. whether `answers` may be left out (`ServeRequest` defaults it) and what a `wait_ms` over 12,000 does (clamped, not refused); 3. the range of `retry_after_ms` (0 means at once; any u64); 4. that a delivery of a kind the host does not know may have no `id` and needs no answer (the `tolerated` event has none, while `ServeResponse::calls` says every delivery has one), and whether it is sent again; 5. what a host does with an `end` or a `withheld.reason` it does not know; 6. what 4 KiB and 1 MiB are measured on (UTF-8 bytes of the message; the result as compact JSON re-written by the daemon, not the bytes sent); 7. the form of IDs: a registration ID is `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,256}`, an answer ID over 256 bytes is ignored; 8. that the host must keep its registration ID to be sent its waiting calls after its own restart, and that on `unknown_registration` it registers *with* that ID; 9. that up to four serves of one registration may be open, each is sent everything due, and a fifth evicts the oldest; 10. the largest request body a route takes, and the largest answer (64 deliveries of up to 1 MiB); 11. that answers are taken in order and the first per ID wins, `accepted` included; 12. which error codes a host may answer; 13. the codes a route may answer that the table lacks (`internal_error`, `storage_failed` at 500, the latter after some answers were taken); 14. what `bind: {}` does; 15. that resuming a held registration while another host serves it makes both its hosts. The reverse, what `crates/clax-surface/src/wire.rs` takes that the section does not say: an answer with `\"accepted\": null` or `\"error\": null` beside its one member is read as that member alone; an `ended` with `\"result\": null` differs from one without; `ServeResponse` without `calls` reads as empty.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":10288},"end":{"line":10347},"content_hash":"e30e8911e199b08ac4ab97ddf7787a11522f2df6ab9570f9f4ad47756a2b6a88"},"summary":"What a host written from this section and the fixture alone has to guess","supersedes":"8ce356a8076e9c14543c0733cb2f94dc8c4a8b38b322964380285ed6d4d6e0cb","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","review","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T05:55:57.186953Z","id":"85948141a2ec21c4715bf2f654290eeba0fa402043d3772af956def2c8576a31","body":{"detail":"The only brief run start is crates/clax-server/src/routes/artifacts.rs (POST /api/artifacts:new), which makes the artifact and its brief thread and starts the run in one store call; a join needs the artifact to exist first (Store::replace_joins refuses join_artifact_unknown). So the brief's run is always made with bindings of version 1. Either drop \"a brief\" from the section or say a brief's run is never joined.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":10651},"end":{"line":10654},"content_hash":"1d7db05a9f3ff6d61b37d5c4ac72b81e574767ff486b5f1c687738d52815afa7"},"summary":"The contract says a join binds into a brief's run, but no brief's run can be joined: its artifact is made by the same request","tags":["claxbot-surfaces","joins","docs","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/contract.md","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T07:06:19.280243Z","id":"8d77ddd1140da0300990f12bb3ee4d7de9df9bbe95b97ac171549c2766ca6ea3","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"85948141a2ec21c4715bf2f654290eeba0fa402043d3772af956def2c8576a31","summary":"Fixed in f75924221: the Joins section says a join binds into a thread sent to ClaxBot and a follow-up, and that a brief's run is never joined since its artifact is made by the same request; crates/clax-bot/src/joins.rs's module doc no longer names a brief. Proposing the owner close it.","tags":["joins","docs","session:claude-code:443e6ffa-9215-48fe-9313-5be76354ae11"]}}
